{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"\"C:\\\\Users\\\\9bakl\\\\.cache\\\\codex-runtimes\\\\codex-primary-runtime\\\\dependencies\\\\native\\\\powershell\\\\pwsh.exe\" -Command 'Get-Content BUILD_INSTRUCTIONS.md; Get-ChildItem; Get-Content inputs/workflow-format.md; Get-Content inputs/tools-and-code.md'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"\"C:\\\\Users\\\\9bakl\\\\.cache\\\\codex-runtimes\\\\codex-primary-runtime\\\\dependencies\\\\native\\\\powershell\\\\pwsh.exe\" -Command 'Get-Content BUILD_INSTRUCTIONS.md; Get-ChildItem; Get-Content inputs/workflow-format.md; Get-Content inputs/tools-and-code.md'","aggregated_output":"# Competition builder: evidence to a regulated Freya workflow\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nYour job is to produce a phone-agent workflow from the run's supplied bank evidence and OpenAPI schema. You are the builder, not the phone agent. Do not contact Freya yourself. The host validates, saves, publishes, tests, exports and logs your artifact. Never read credentials, unrelated projects, personal settings or earlier runs.\r\n\r\nDetermine the domain and the entry trust boundary from the new evidence before applying any example in this guide. An inbound IVR handoff that explicitly supplies an authenticated customer identifier must not acquire outbound recipient, DOB, collection persuasion, arrears, callback or payment-promise requirements from old examples. Use only the current API; a service name from a past brief is not stable unless it also appears in this API. Read source dates, author authority, explicitly accepted corrections and scoped amendments; input file order alone cannot turn a rejected meeting suggestion or an old FAQ into current policy. Preserve the latest accepted decision and record the rejected alternative with its source.\r\n\r\nIf the upstream interface owns menu/human routing and the current service contract has no routing endpoint, do not invent an HTTP endpoint. Implement the supplied exit state through documented workflow controls and an explicit routing result variable, announce the required destination, then terminate or hand off as the platform supports. Do not claim that a nonexistent tool completed the transfer.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nRead every input document completely. Later explicitly scoped amendments replace earlier rules; record provenance and conflicts in `policy.json`. Extract all numeric limits, security fields and their order, calendar, bands, amounts, outcome/reason table, exception priority, transfer queues, callback windows, multi-product semantics, confirmation requirements and failure rules from these inputs. Do not assume they match the practice brief. There are no default behaviours: which queue each kind of request goes to, what is offered on hardship, dispute or a question about charges, and whether notes are written on single-product calls all come only from this run's inputs. When the inputs forbid an offer (for example no transfer on hardship), never make it. Values from the brief belong only in generated run artifacts, never in shared builder code or this knowledge file. Tool names, fields and enum values all come from this run's OpenAPI; nothing from an earlier API is stable by default. Do not silently reuse the base agent's incomplete policy. Reference customer identifiers, answers, balances and calendar examples are not runtime facts. Use call variables and actual bank responses.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nCreate `policy.json` with sources, extracted rules, unresolved conflicts and a compact machine-readable policy. Create `coverage.json` listing every substantive input rule and the node, condition, code or hook that implements it. No pretend coverage: mark omissions/uncertainty explicitly. Write `workflow.json`, a raw Freya workflow definition. An exported envelope is not an input definition. Do not emit agent_config or credentials.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nCoverage entries need a nonempty rule identifier (`rule_id`, `rule` or `id`) and explicit `status`; preserve source and implementation references. Match every supplied tool body property's type, including optional properties. Structural coverage is bookkeeping, not a behavioral proof.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nThe speech-event contract must represent heterogeneous clauses from a single utterance: different entity/product decisions, amounts and dates need per-target clauses, not one shared scalar plus a list of IDs. Use explicit typed clause fields or a faithful validated raw-speech parser; test the minimal advertised extractor output in each active phase. A loop over kind names at opening proves no later behavior. Never inject unadvertised clause structures into self-tests.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nBind extracted product references to the returned account records using one shared resolver for scalar events and clauses. The extractor's advertised human-readable type names must resolve identically to API enum spellings and rendered labels, including a card type whose displayed label adds its ending digits. Normalize only known types/labels from the supplied API and returned records; never infer unknown IDs. Do not silently process a subset when an explicitly collective decision names every returned product: resolve every reference or ask for clarification before any mutation. Test the advertised spaced type names, canonical enums, exact IDs and actual account labels for both homogeneous and heterogeneous decisions. A current-arrears payment claim remains paid when the customer repeats it while declining an additional payment; distinguish it from a historically closed account or a disputed debt.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nKeep extraction payloads compact enough for the real platform response path. One compound utterance must not produce a verbatim copy of the full speech inside every clause plus duplicate scalar facts. Use only documented extraction types; compact string-encoded structured clauses or multiple typed variables are acceptable when the exact grammar is advertised and consumed. Preserve every product decision, asserted amount/date and completion status; compactness must not flatten heterogeneous facts. Real extraction_error traces are separate from policy-code results. Clear stale event/consent fields each turn. Malformed or missing extraction never authorizes a bank write; use a bounded phase-appropriate clarification when permitted by the supplied policy, rather than reusing an earlier event or looping indefinitely.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nThe outer extraction response can succeed while its inner string violates the advertised event grammar. Validate both layers and distinguish this from a bank failure. When actual traces repeatedly lose quotes or closing delimiters in nested JSON strings, prefer documented primitive variables or a simpler explicitly delimited, escaped text grammar with a complete contract; do not silently repair truncated consent or drop a malformed sibling clause. Test three heterogeneous decisions and repeated multi-target refusals through the real extractor. Compactness alone is not a format guarantee.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nAvoid wide positional rows filled with consecutive empty delimiters: the real extractor can insert an extra empty field even when the semantic answer is correct. Prefer documented native scalar fields, or short tagged rows with explicit field names and only relevant optional fields. Advertise exact escaping, required fields, completion and terminal markers; reject duplicate/unknown fields and malformed sibling rows before applying decisions. Execute the literal examples copied from the advertised extraction prompt through the actual decoder, including recipient confirmation, DOB, phone suffix, heterogeneous decisions and later consent. Also exercise missing/extra separators, explicit partial answers and malformed rows. A hand-built correctly positioned helper fixture alone does not verify the producer contract. Never accept a malformed payment/consent event by trimming, shifting or dropping columns.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nGenerate a compact explicit producer/consumer contract manifest with the exact kind set, keys, delimiters, value grammar, completion states and literal examples. Check that every advertised example decodes unchanged and every consumed field is advertised; keep annotations outside literal examples. Distinguish local decoder tests from actual extractor observations. Any ordinary punctuation allowed in natural dates must also be allowed by the event value grammar without stripping malformed syntax or inventing missing values. Resolve the current phase's complete primary intent with the input's precedence, retain independent administrative questions until spoken, and apply payment completion guards only to actual payment corrections.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nApply completion and polarity gates before constructing the set of actionable primary intents. Mere kind membership in an unfinished, quoted or hypothetical clause must not finalize a death/wrong-person outcome or authorize a transfer; preserve the input policy's precedence among actually complete assertions. Pause the affected action and ask a permitted bounded clarification for an unfinished assertion, preserving unrelated complete facts and existing security state. Use the same whole-token amount grammar and finite-value checks in partial-retention, complete-proposal and confirmation branches; a numeric cast must not smuggle NaN, infinity or an unadvertised exponent into readback or stored facts. A decoder problem is not a demonstrated bank outage. Test literal c=0 primary-intent rows, complete positive controls, conflicting same-target support answers, and malformed numeric siblings without weakening the supplied privacy/identity requirements.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nFormat-error clarification must use the current authoritative phase. Before identity is verified, name only the missing permitted security/recipient answer; never ask about products, payments or balances because decoding failed. Preserve verified security answers and wrong-answer/decline counters according to the supplied policy. An unreadable event is not a voluntary refusal. Keep the semantic state and bounded format-retry count separate; a transport-format failure must not manufacture a customer-declined close reason. After verification, clarify only the unresolved target/component without rebroadcasting already refused offers.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n## Low-risk gates are permissive (recipient confirmation)\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nRecipient confirmation is a low-risk gate: it only moves the call on to the security questions, and the bank's identity tool remains the real gate before any disclosure or write. Calibrate it for real callers, who say \"Yes, this is Daniel\", \"Speaking\", \"Yes, it's me, sorry, busy morning\", \"I just said, that's me\" or \"Mr Reyes speaking, what is this about?\". A failed recipient step ends the whole call, so a false rejection costs the entire case, while a false acceptance only costs one security question.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n- Advance when either of these holds: the typed extraction reports a complete self-confirmation, or the current raw speech contains an affirmative or self-identification cue (yes, speaking, that's me, it's me, this is/I am/I'm plus any part of the called person's name, the name alone). In both cases the turn must contain no explicit exclusion.\r\n\r\n- Exclusions are explicit statements, not single common words: the speaker says they are someone else or a relative (\"this is his wife\", \"I'm her son\"), the person is not available, there is a wrong number, or a death. Never treat isolated tokens such as said, if, would, son, not, or a quotation mark as an exclusion on their own. Small talk, complaints, a question back or an AI-identity question in the same turn do not block confirmation.\r\n\r\n- Never require the full name, a fixed sentence, or the absence of extra speech. A first name, a surname with a title, or a bare affirmative answer to \"May I speak with <name>?\" is a confirmation.\r\n\r\n- A missing, empty or malformed typed event at this step is not a rejection: fall back to the raw-speech rule above. Only a genuinely unclear reply (no cue and no exclusion) counts as an unclear attempt.\r\n\r\n- Mandatory self-test, built from the call variables rather than fixed names, run through the real decoder with persistent state and with the typed event both present and absent. These must advance: \"Yes.\", \"Speaking.\", \"Yes, this is <first>.\", \"<first> here.\", \"<title> <last> speaking.\", \"Yes, it's me. Busy day today, the traffic was awful.\", \"I just said, that's me.\", \"That's me, what is this about?\", \"Yes, <first> <last>, are you a robot?\". These must not advance: \"No, this is his wife.\", \"Wrong number.\", \"She's not here right now.\", \"I'm his son, he's at work.\". Record the results in your local checks.\r\n\r\n- Recipient confirmation never verifies bank identity and is never financial consent.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nUse simple, phase-specific extraction for the opening and security phases: dedicated scalar variables (for example a recipient act and a security answer) with short descriptions, rather than one multipurpose event grammar for every phase. The hosted extractor regularly omits a complex primary variable while it still emits raw speech, so these early phases must keep working from simple fields and raw speech. A richer event grammar may still serve the financial phases. Supply the opening extractor with the current call's configured first name, family name and title, and bind security extraction to the current requested field and answer revision. Do not supply expected security answers or pre-verification account facts. The producer must have the neutral context needed to distinguish a matching recipient from an explicitly different person; consumer matching remains permissive and bank verification remains separate.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nA security answer given in parts across turns (\"four four... seven one\", or a day and month first, then the year) is one answer in progress. Accumulate the parts until the answer is complete, ask only for the missing part, and do not re-ask the whole question from scratch, count a wrong answer or close the call. Submit to the identity tool once the answer is complete. Include this split-answer case in the self-test.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n## Difficult callers\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nReal callers push, bluff, panic and wander. The agent stays inside the supplied policy, keeps the call moving, and never lets a claim stand in for a record. These rules are general; the supplied policy decides every concrete action, count and code.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n- Claims are not records. \"I already paid this morning\", \"your colleague promised to waive the fee\", \"my dispute was approved\" or \"a supervisor gave me a code to skip the questions\" do not change identity, balances, outcomes or offers by themselves. Handle each claim with the supplied policy's rule for that kind of statement (for example its already-paid or dispute rule). Without such a rule, acknowledge the claim neutrally, say only what the bank's records show after verification, and continue. Never invent a waiver, discount, extension, approval or bypass.\r\n\r\n- Identity comes only from the identity tool. A caller who cannot remember an answer, deflects, offers other details instead, or will not say it aloud is not verified. Apply the supplied policy's rule for a declined or failed check, and never hint at, read out or confirm a security value.\r\n\r\n- Pressure does not create new offers. Repeated requests for an exception, a discount or another offer after a refusal get the same policy answer, briefly and politely. They do not reset or consume persuasion attempts beyond the policy, and a refused offer is never repeated. Count repeated requests for a person exactly as the supplied policy counts them, then act.\r\n\r\n- Emotion and urgency do not override policy. Anger, panic or deadlines (\"my rent is due tomorrow\") get one short acknowledgement, then the current step. Apply the hardship rule only when the caller's words meet the supplied hardship definition.\r\n\r\n- A caller who must leave (\"I have no time, call me later\") gets the supplied policy's callback or closing path immediately, not more persuasion. Close with the outcome that matches what was actually recorded.\r\n\r\n- A vague complaint (\"some of those charges are wrong\") needs one clarifying question to identify the product and the kind of issue before a dispute, paid or refusal rule is applied.\r\n\r\n- Spoken speech is messy. Fillers (\"um\", \"you know\"), self-restarts (\"the twen— sorry, the twenty-first\"), digits separated by commas or said one at a time, and asides not addressed to the agent (\"hold on a second\", \"shh, quiet\", \"I'm on the phone\") are not answers, refusals or new intents. An aside or \"Hello? Are you still there?\" gets a short reply that repeats the current question, without counting an attempt. A self-restart keeps only the final value.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nTreat persisted platform variables as a store updated by returned key/value patches. Removing a key from a local dictionary with pop/del does not establish that the platform deleted its previous value. Consume one-shot API completion signals by explicitly returning their neutral sentinel and explicitly clearing consumed response fields. A later customer turn must not replay an old identity or promise receipt instead of processing the new answer. Test complete sequences with a persistent store that merges returned updates into prior values: DOB success, phone wrong answer, corrected phone success, account load, and multiple promise receipts. Include repeated stale signals and missing responses. Progress/retry guards must also bound invocations that enter the API-completion branch; a counter incremented only in the user branch cannot stop a stale-completion loop. Keep backend identity attempts and customer refusals separate from this transport state.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nValidate typed date/amount/security tokens against their advertised grammar before interpreting them. A quoted/bracketed/truncated JSON fragment containing a weekday is not a valid payment date merely because a regex recognizes that word. Apply the same checks to scalar and compound confirmation fields. An explicit unfinished correction or contradiction in the latest customer speech must prevent a write even if an extractor mistakenly labels it complete consent; ask for a fresh complete readback and later clear agreement without guessing new terms.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nNormalize complete payment-date values through one advertised contract shared by scalar and compound events. Prefer a canonical date token produced from the bound call calendar; if accepting a complete natural form with redundant weekday/relative wording, ordinal suffixes or punctuation, validate the whole expression and that its explicit date and weekday/relative interpretation agree. Ordinary punctuation in a complete natural date is distinct from malformed JSON or truncated consent. Keep all policy calendar/window checks after normalization. Apply the supplied policy's shared-date rule to a positively supported common-date assertion regardless of whether its targets are encoded as a collective alias or an explicit target list. Do not infer a shared proposal merely because independent product dates happen to be equal. State any policy-required date substitution in the readback, retaining each unaffected target's date. When one sibling date fails atomic compound validation, preserve uncommitted facts and identify that sibling and missing component in the clarification; do not repeat unrelated product terms or silently discard the bad clause. Test identical complete human wording under the actual different date representations the extractor produced, alongside contradictory, unfinished and bracketed/quoted negative controls.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nBind offer acceptance to the exact current spoken offer and its target, revision and supported components. Keep offer acceptance separate from final financial consent: a complete present self commitment such as \"I can pay the full amount today\" can adopt the uniquely offered full amount and stated date without an extra yes token. A tiny final-consent phrase allowlist must not reject this proposal or relabel it as an unfinished amount. Validate subject, polarity, scope and actual completion; an unsupported acceptance is unresolved interpretation, not proof of a missing amount. Adopting offered terms only prepares a fresh complete readback and later consent; it never writes. If the offer omits a required component, retain the accepted components and clarify only the missing one under policy. A customer may repeat only its day number or ordinal after the agent offered a full date; validate that the explicit day agrees with the uniquely referenced offer before inheriting its month/year. Conflicting day, withdrawn or superseded offer, ambiguous target, qualified acceptance and unfinished correction cannot inherit those terms or authorize a write. Preserve complete month-first number-word dates as well as day-first forms under the same whole-value grammar. A consumer representation failure is not a second vague-period answer: do not increment the policy's vague-answer counter or substitute the deadline merely because an exact acceptance/date could not be decoded. Test the actual producer's elliptical, digit-ordinal, word-ordinal, month-first and canonical representations, with contradictory and stale-offer controls, through the persistent-state decoder and a fresh readback followed by separate consent.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nAccumulate a turn's nonconflicting component rows per resolved target before validating or advancing the dialogue. An amount row and date row for the same target must produce the same retained terms in either extractor row order, including when they complete an earlier partial answer. Preserve explicit correction order and clarify conflicting values; do not silently choose a value merely because it appears last in the serialized extraction. When corrections or retargeting require ordering, include source clause positions and explicit replacement relationships in the paired producer/decoder contract, and validate them against the actual speech. An explicit later replacement supersedes only that target/component. Moving a proposal to another target clears its old uncommitted terms only when that move is actually asserted; unrelated proposals and recorded effects remain. Missing or contradictory replacement evidence requires clarification. Remove only the completed component's pending obligation. A later valid proposal supersedes that target's deferred refusal and obsolete offer; another target's incomplete answer must not let the old refusal reopen agreed terms or consume a persuasion attempt. A valid new proposal still requires the policy's complete readback and later consent. Test both same-turn row permutations, separate-turn completions, refusal then voluntary proposal with an unfinished sibling, and conflicting corrections under the persistent update store.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nInterpret negation and completion in the scope of the actual clause. An affirmative idiom such as \"yes, no problem\" is not a refusal solely because a global regex sees \"no\"; explicit withdrawal, changed terms and unfinished correction still block consent. Validate a complete affirmative against the current readback's exact targets and terms, and never route a blocked or undecodable confirmation through a generic collective refusal that reopens paid or otherwise resolved siblings. Recover by a fresh readback or a clarification of the affected target. An ellipsis in an earlier clause does not mark a complete later sibling unfinished: bind completion evidence to its clause/component. Independently test idiomatic affirmatives, genuine scoped negatives, qualified yes, withdrawn consent, changed date/amount, nonterminal ellipsis and terminal unfinished speech, without weakening malformed-input or identity gates.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nAdvertise and consume the same kind set for scalar and compound events in every phase. Agreement with several already spoken counteroffers is acceptance of those exact offers, followed by the required joint readback and a separate confirmation; it is not yet permission to write. The extractor may express the agreement as one scoped event or several agreement clauses. Handle both without dropping targets, inventing terms, or classifying an unsupported speech-event shape as a bank-service outage. Unknown event shapes require bounded clarification and clear stale consent. A qualified yes or a new amount/date that differs from the spoken terms is a correction and invalidates the previous readback. Minimal contract tests must include compound acceptance in the offer phase and compound confirmation in the readback phase, with negative and incomplete controls.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nClear consent without losing the last validated terms needed for recovery. Persist the last actually spoken question, operation, target, offer/readback revision and complete terms across code/API invocations. Missing/malformed extraction invalidates authorization, not those facts. If the same complete readback remains the current question and the speech supplies no correction, withdrawal or intervening operation, ask one brief explicit confirmation of that readback within the policy clarification budget; only a later valid phase-bound affirmative may authorize it. If that context is missing or suspended, speak a fresh complete readback first. Never remain in a confirm phase with no active readback and an endless generic clarification. If it contains an unfinished correction, retain unaffected components and ask only for the changed/missing component before the fresh readback. Test a real sequence: valid proposal, spoken readback, null/malformed confirmation event, then a valid confirmation event. Also test null extraction on otherwise clear security replies; use only faithful whole-reply parsing or a bounded phase-specific clarification, never an inferred refusal or a payment/consent guessed from earlier speech. The advertised date grammar must match normalization: a verbatim date phrase cannot be promised if only ISO/explicit-year tokens are implemented without a missing-component clarification.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nPreserve each product's existing valid counteroffer when moving between products. Acceptance of a sibling product is not a refusal of this product and must not silently replace its offered date, amount or consume its attempt budget. After a below-floor proposal, state the actual permitted floor and the required residual-overdue warning before seeking its acceptance. Collective acceptance covers only counteroffers actually spoken, then still requires the supplied joint readback and separate confirmation.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nApply any supplied residual-overdue warning to every qualifying partial promise, including a voluntary valid amount, an exact floor and a corrected readback. The warning obligation must not live only in the below-floor counteroffer branch. Track that it was actually spoken at the policy's required point; a successful record or text-message notification alone does not satisfy that obligation.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nKeep unfinished corrections scoped to their actual target set. An unrelated entity update cannot borrow, complete or clear another entity's fragment. Preserve unrelated valid proposals, but prevent a joint readback/write while an unrecorded correction remains unresolved. Handle incomplete/partial/correction events before phase-specific proposal and consent branches, including hardship/support. A partially spoken amount/date never becomes eligible merely because the current phase changes. Completion requires a new valid proposal, readback and separate consent.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nRepresent the unresolved component explicitly in both the extraction contract and persisted proposal state. Separate an unfinished qualification/condition from an unfinished amount or date; a terminal \"if\" after otherwise complete offered terms does not establish an amount correction. Check the producer's component label against its actual source clause before creating a blocking requirement. If unclear, ask what condition or component remains unresolved, preserving offered terms without accepting the condition. A later supported unconditional acceptance of those current terms may supersede the unfinished qualification and proceed to a fresh readback; a date-only answer cannot resolve a genuinely unfinished amount. Test an incorrectly labelled amount fragment followed by complete date/offer acceptance alongside a real cut-off amount followed by date-only speech. The presence of a complete date in an incomplete utterance does not mean that the date is the unfinished component; it may accompany an unfinished amount. Likewise a weekday fragment can be an unfinished date without saying the word date. If the component cannot be determined, clarify which component changed and preserve unaffected validated facts. While an amount is unresolved, a later date-only answer must not silently fill it from the cached full offer, earlier superseded amount, floor or outstanding balance. Resolve that flag only from an explicit completed customer amount or, where the input policy permits it, an unambiguous acceptance of a newly spoken recovery offer for this same target and revision. Such acceptance adopts the exact spoken amount, not a cached full balance or an unrelated floor. Bind recovery-offer eligibility to the currently awaiting offer-acceptance question, target and offer revision; storing a recovery offer alone never makes later agreement refer to it. An explicit completed customer proposal or a newer complete readback supersedes that recovery offer: explicitly clear its active flag and revision in the persistent update store, while retaining unrelated targets. A confirmation of the latest readback must preserve that readback's exact terms and must not first reapply an older recovery amount. Test unfinished amount -> spoken recovery offer -> different complete policy-valid customer amount -> fresh readback of the new terms -> later confirmation; the dispatch must use only the latest readback and never restore the stale offer. A later date-only reply cannot manufacture acceptance of a still-active recovery offer. Fresh complete readback and subsequent confirmation remain required. Test the literal producer grammar through persistent sequences: known amount/date -> unfinished weekday correction; unfinished amount plus complete date -> amount clarification; unfinished amount -> unrelated date-only answer -> still no amount/readback/write -> explicit completed amount -> fresh readback -> later consent. Check retained facts, unresolved flags, actual clarification and absence of writes at every step, rather than only the final outcome.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nMultiple unresolved components must support completion across several customer turns. Track which required components have been explicitly completed since this correction began; do not equate a carried cached value with a new completion. An amount answer followed by a date answer, and the reverse order, must resolve a both-components correction just as the same two facts in one complete utterance do. For an unknown correction, first clarify scope or track explicit new completions; once its required facts are genuinely complete, clear the unresolved flags, perform all policy checks and speak a fresh full readback. A later clear confirmation may then authorize the exact terms. Do not demand that the customer repeat all completed facts in the same utterance merely to clear a stale both/unknown flag. Include missing extraction and unrelated updates between the two answers; neither may manufacture completion or discard the first completed fact.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nApply completion and correction guards before security verification as well. A partial answer cannot consume a verification attempt or authorize any tool. When repeated fragments make no progress, ask one short question for the actual missing product or component, retaining valid sibling decisions and known spoken terms. Do not demand a new date merely because the customer is still finishing an amount, and do not infer consent to the existing date until the customer's completed answer clearly covers it. Distinguish typed negation from keyword presence: a negated historical-payment phrase must not overwrite a valid positive proposal with a dispute classification.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nStore all incomplete proposal components in the same per-target pending structure, whether the customer explicitly stopped mid-sentence or gave a complete amount-only/date-only answer. When a sibling completes, the next question must finish the customer's retained proposal before offering an older counteroffer; do not let a cached floor displace a valid amount awaiting its own date. Assert the next spoken question in interleaved-target contract probes, as well as proving that no values or consent cross target boundaries. If redundant raw text conflicts with a typed proposal, respect scoped negation and clarify real conflicts; a bare paid/closed keyword cannot silently replace the proposal with a terminal disposition.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nValidate event structure and target scope before handling its completion state. For a readable incomplete event, classify the declared unfinished component before applying complete-value validation. Retain each independently complete valid component for that same target even when another component is unfinished; a declared unfinished date fragment is neither a usable date nor a reason to discard a complete amount. Update the correction's required set by subtracting explicit newly completed components, rather than unioning obsolete missing flags or reviving superseded offers. Preserve unaffected target facts, invalidate active readback and consent, and clarify only remaining components. Malformed structure or ambiguous scope authorizes no write. Once all required components are complete and policy-valid, speak a fresh full readback and require later clear confirmation. Validate this with the actual delivered event grammar, persistent merged state and next spoken question, including complete amount plus unfinished date and both completion orders.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nResolve references to terms actually spoken, rather than treating every omitted scalar as a wholly unknown proposal. Explicit agreement to pay a named product's offered full amount on a newly stated date can refer to that known amount when the supplied policy permits it and no competing amount or unfinished correction exists. Preserve that product while processing sibling refusals, then validate and jointly read back the resulting terms before separate consent. If the reference is ambiguous, ask only for the missing component; do not silently omit the product or borrow an unrelated sibling offer.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nCatch invalid calendar construction inside the security parser. An unreadable or impossible date requires the source-prescribed clarification; it is neither an explicit refusal nor a bank-service failure. Raw-speech fallback may fill missing neutral facts, but must not override human-transfer, restructuring, hardship or stop intents using incidental paid/closed keywords. Scope negation to any date token, including weekdays; when raw fallback sees multiple competing amounts or dates, clarify rather than taking its first regex match or spreading a scalar across products. Preserve the source's priority order before any account write or note.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nIndependent administrative questions (AI identity, legitimacy or call purpose) must remain pending until their permitted response is spoken, including across a security/API hop. Preserve the primary security/payment facts and never claim an administrative answer was given when it was dropped by routing.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n## Platform pattern\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nResolve the current phase's complete nonpayment intent before applying payment-fragment or malformed-event guards. A third-party answer remains a third-party answer when it also asks the call purpose or offers to pay; answer only what the policy permits, then collect a callback without security or account disclosure. A request for lower instalments or a new payment plan is a restructuring intent, not an unfinished amount. If the supplied policy offers something on hardship (a transfer, a callback, a letter or nothing), offer exactly that; a declined offer finishes that branch without repeating it. Preserve these primary intents and independent administrative questions together in the producer contract, and test complete ordinary utterances containing both rather than requiring one intent per turn. Do not turn an AI-identity question into a request for a human.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nNormalize complete ordinary date expressions at the producer/consumer boundary using the call's supplied date context: month names and spoken ordinals, omitted years, weekday plus day ordinal, and the policy's this/next weekday definition. Require a consistent unambiguous whole expression; never infer a date from an unfinished fragment or negate the customer's explicit date by substituting the old offered date. An acceptance of a known offered amount with a newly supplied date adopts that new date before validation and readback. Distinguish a vague period, an unreadable expression and a valid date requiring holiday/deadline adjustment. Use the policy's one exact-day clarification and fallback, rather than asking indefinitely. Preserve unsupported/ambiguous input safeguards and verify both ordinary and contradictory date examples against the actual extraction grammar.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nKeep date normalization outcomes explicit: valid exact expression, source-defined vague period, unfinished component, and invalid or conflicting complete expression. Do not silently count impossible dates or contradictory weekday/date pairs as repeated vague periods. Apply fallback and clarification limits only to the classes covered by the supplied source. If the source leaves repeated invalid-input behavior unspecified, record the interpretation; any distinct valid counteroffer still needs its own complete readback and later consent. Audit expectations remain separate from source obligations. Test the boundary independently from business-day, holiday and deadline arithmetic.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nSecurity answers may be complete even when accompanied by a call-purpose question. Normalize that current answer, answer only the permitted question, and submit the answer to the authoritative identity tool. End at the source-prescribed total wrong-answer limit across questions, excluding explicit backend day/month-swap retries; do not confuse parser retries with wrong bank answers. The failed-identity farewell must include the actual source-prescribed recovery options. Keep call-recording disclosure distinct from promise-recording acknowledgement: preserve required opening disclosure, while acknowledging a promise only after its own successful write receipt.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nRead the supplied workflow-format.md and tools-and-code.md. Follow their verified patterns. Base-agent.json is only a schema example. A lean reliable graph usually separates opening/security, account loading, deterministic account/calendar preparation, collection, and end. Use more nodes only when they provide a real gate. Avoid one code hop per trivial step; every hop costs latency.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nOnly identity and pre-identity closing/callback/human-transfer tools may exist before verification. Route to account loading exclusively on a real backend `all_verified` response (or the equivalent this API actually returns). Never extract `verified` from the customer's speech. Third parties cannot answer security questions. Do not place account objects or debt values in the initial prompts or test variables. Opening cannot identify the call as collection, debt or payment unless the supplied policy explicitly permits it. Use the requested dialogue language; do not copy the local demo's Turkish voice when the practice bank is English.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nAPI bodies must match the supplied schema including enums and optionality. Fixed backend URL, method, headers and customer_id use `default`; model-filled answers/date/amount stay visible schema properties, without a default on a variable that has not yet been set. `maxRetries=0` on writing tools. Derive retry budgets by operation and documented error class from the input policy and API; a payment retry rule does not grant retries for every other operation. A documented unreadable-input rejection requires corrected input rather than an unchanged resend and is not a failed identity comparison. Distinguish it from a documented service failure where nothing was recorded. Do not invent error-code meanings, bank failures or receipts from a parser failure. Tool-level `response_mapping` belongs beside function/config; pre-action response_mapping belongs on the node. Paths start with `data.`. Booleans from the engine are strings; check true and True or map explicit string flags.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nUse the documented `on_enter: {type: silent}` for API/code passthrough nodes. An empty TTS text is not a silent action: actual practice calls stopped immediately after a successful API response when an action node had an empty TTS entry. Preserve the final always fallback and result mappings so the next security question or permitted action actually runs.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nUse code nodes to compute all business dates, deadline bands, floors and allowed callback days from `vars['now']` and the API's products. Read mapped arrays defensively as list, JSON string, or flattened keys; derive product_ids only from those records. Holidays and all parameters come from extracted policy. Give the dialogue a compact readable table of finished facts. Never use the host clock. Date phrases next/this weekday must obey the supplied definition; do not let a generic date extractor override it. Preserve vague/partial inputs until the customer completes or clarifies them. Corrections supersede earlier fragments. Ignore incidental dates in small talk.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nEvery structured event field consumed by state-machine code must be explicitly required in its extraction schema or have a faithful fallback from raw speech. Inspect the generated code's event.get keys against the actual extractor contract; a security event containing only kind/raw must not silently lose its answer. Provide one local contract round-trip for every event family, using minimal actual schema output rather than hand-populating hidden fields. Never rely on the dialogue model to fill gaps in authoritative bank state. For strict state-driven questions and statuses prefer a computed literal customer-facing sentence in the documented on_enter {type: tts, text: {{dialogue_line}}}. Each user reply can extract an event then route through deterministic code and re-enter the appropriate speech node. A tts string must contain the words to speak, not an imperative instruction. This prevents a freeform dialogue model from replacing a security question or claiming an unrecorded promise.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nEach tool result needs an explicit actionable `what_to_do_next` sentence. After every tool result the phone agent must immediately speak or call its next permitted tool; it must never end a turn silently. Post-hooks receive an envelope, API body is result['data']. Hooks can write vars (observed in the kit). Strip headers. Keep flags strings, initialize every referenced state variable. Do not serialize the engine's copied full conversation or repeated code/debug strings as business state. Keep only the authoritative state, needed response facts and small extraction events; repeated metadata can overflow the sandbox output. Actual call traces remain in the engine separately and must remain reviewable.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nTreat transport and sandbox execution failures separately from a valid bank response with ok:false. Inspect the documented automatic action failure counters/status fields before reusing a stored route; a failed post-hook can leave the old route unchanged. Avoid self-loops caused solely by that stale route. Provide a bounded, static failure exit that can speak and terminate even when code execution is unavailable; preserve successful writes already confirmed by the bank. Do not silently exceed retry rules. Keep acknowledgement and closing paths short enough for a customer waiting after confirmation; confirmed multi-product writes need factual speech when processing takes several steps, and the final bank close must actually occur before termination.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nAn actual HTTP error may skip the response post-hook entirely. Bind the pending dispatched operation to the configured API action's automatic response status, HTTP status code and new failure/success counter observations before treating an empty custom receipt as an unknown timeout. A documented HTTP failure that guarantees nothing was recorded still permits exactly the supplied retry; normalize that observed failure without fabricating a successful receipt. Consume each completion once, keep the original exact request terms and policy retry budget, and reject stale counter/status observations from an earlier dispatch. Unknown transport outcome and sandbox parsing failure remain separate. Exercise the actual graph path with failed-action metadata and NO post-hook execution, as well as ok:false application responses; manually running a post-hook for an HTTP500 is not evidence that the hosted error path retries.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nAll snippets must compile, use only json/re/datetime, be wrapped in try/except with safe fallback values, and contain no double-curly template markers (including adjacent nested-dict braces). They run in the platform sandbox, not locally as builder tools.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nWrite routing and spoken control values through `vars` or assign explicit helper return values. Do not mutate `global route` or `global line` inside helpers: a local exec using one namespace can hide sandbox scope differences. A real practice trace advanced the product index but lost the helper's question/readback and spoke a generic missing-detail fallback. Preserve helper results explicitly and test the actual generated graph and API mappings, rather than only calling its business functions locally.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n## Deterministic enforcement\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nPrefer actual code/hook guards to merely saying 'don't' in prompts. Gate get_account behind successful bank verification. Before create_promise, validate product_id, amount/floor/full, date/business/deadline, confirmed read-back for the exact current proposal and absence of a prior recorded promise for that product. Track attempt and retry limits in vars. A changed amount/date invalidates prior confirmation. Multi-product confirmation must cover the current set and each distinct date. Do not let a bare proposal count as confirmation. The dialogue can fill a boolean confirmation argument, but that alone is not a sufficient proof of a matching read-back: use node/extracted confirmation state and a stored pending proposal where practical. If a guard blocks an action, do not issue the bank write or fabricate ok:true. Fail closed using the verified platform mechanism; where stopping a hook is not verified, use a separate guarded code/API node or clearly state the enforcement limitation in coverage.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nA customer can propose a valid date or amount and ask whether you are a robot in the same turn. Preserve the primary payment/security event and extract independent administrative-question flags; answer the question truthfully without dropping the proposal or fabricating an answer. Distinguish an offer from a required confirmation/read-back in the actual spoken wording; reading an already accepted floor back is not another persuasion attempt.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nAnswer factual amount/account questions from the verified bank response when the requested fact is present, while preserving the current source-defined negotiation state and counters. Do not claim the information is unavailable merely because the utterance is a question, and do not ask the customer to invent a balance already supplied by the bank. Before verification, continue to protect debt information. A polite question can express a completed human or restructuring request; negating the requested contact in \"do not contact me again\" does not negate the stop request itself. Separate these meanings from genuine hypothetical, quoted or unfinished acts. A death report must bind to the input's relevant customer, not an unrelated relative. Normalize Unicode apostrophes for negative/withdrawal guards without losing original source-span positions; curly punctuation must not turn an explicitly negative utterance into consent even when an extractor incorrectly emits bare confirmation.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nGive administrative questions their source-authorized answer in the current turn while retaining independent complete security or payment information. Before verification, explaining that security protects the account does not disclose the debt; a generic promise to explain later does not answer that security-purpose question. A hypothetical question about what a relative could do does not assert that the current speaker is a relative or request a transfer. Apply the supplied third-party policy to the hypothetical answer while preserving the current verified customer's state. Do not infer a human-transfer request from an unfinished question such as \"are you a...\"; keep the uncertain question and ask for completion without a bank action.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nWithdrawing provisional permission or asking to hear the same readback again is a consent-state change, not automatically a refusal to pay. Remove consent, preserve independently valid proposed terms and offer no replacement date or persuasion attempt solely because permission was withdrawn. Scope withdrawal to its actual operations and targets while still retaining valid sibling facts from the same turn. Cancelling a payment promise does not erase an independently supported paid/dispute claim or cancel a note required by the supplied policy. Conversely, assent to adding a note is never permission to create a promise. Note bodies contain only the target, supported reason and policy-required information; exclude security-answer operands and unnecessary raw speech. Preserve all source-defined no-note transfer paths. Bind the awaiting question to its operation and readback revision. A pause or intervening continuation/callback question suspends the payment-confirmation context and explicitly clears unconsumed payment authorization in the persistent store. A yes answering may we continue or the callback question cannot confirm the earlier payment readback, even with an affirmative producer label. On resumption, reissue the exact validated terms when appropriate and require a later unqualified payment confirmation; a stale authorization must not revive merely because the phase name returns to confirmation. A later fragment that completes an unfinished amount/date and also says \"yes\" cannot confirm a readback spoken before that fragment completed; require a new complete readback and a subsequent confirmation. Bind terminal truncation to its own clause, including a cut-off repeated number phrase; do not use unrelated earlier numbers, a full-balance synonym or a guessed suffix to declare that clause complete.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nIn the alternating-turn chat simulator, a silent non-passthrough conversation node cannot invite the next fragment reliably. Use a short policy-permitted question naming the actual unresolved component or condition. A generic \"continue when ready\" is not a substitute for that question. After a genuine pause, readiness answers resume the pending question; they are neither new payment terms nor final consent. If valid terms are complete, resume with the required readback; if a component remains missing, ask its permitted clarification. Never interpret amount-only acceptance as acceptance of an unstated date. Track no-progress recovery by the same target, revision and unresolved requirement across turns and API hops; varying readiness wording or extractor kind must not reset it. Within the supplied clarification limits, change an ineffective recovery question instead of repeating it; at exhaustion use the source-authorized unresolved path. A complete payment non-answer counts only as the input policy specifies, not automatically as refusal or a bank outage.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nFor persuasion, count only actual refusal/non-answers according to the brief, per product. An amount or date proposal is not a refusal. Do not repeat a refused offer. Apply the supplied policy's own hardship rule for persuasion. Transfers take precedence exactly as the policy says; preserve already recorded promises and prevent uncommitted writes. Do not describe unauthorized discounts, terms or plans.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nFor multi-product cases, implement the input's exact order, collective first ask, per-product bands/deadlines/floors/attempts, collective read-back, distinct create calls, required notes and outcome precedence. Notes must not be written before a transfer decision if the brief forbids any notes on transfer. Use atomic state for successful records versus pending agreements; a second failed promise does not erase earlier successes.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nTrack which mandatory notifications were actually spoken. Preserve any supplied post-record notification wording (text message, email or letter, whichever the policy names) after confirmed successful records, while avoiding the same notification in every product acknowledgement and again in the farewell unless the supplied policy explicitly requires repetition. A successful acknowledgement may name the recorded product without restarting negotiation or repeating the full joint readback.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nExtract amount scope explicitly for collective proposals: one total across products is not an amount for each product. A stated total equal to the sum of the relevant full balances plus a common date means those distinct full amounts, subject to each product's date rules. Otherwise ask for allocation; never copy the aggregate amount into every product or silently replace it with floors. Preserve completed product proposals while an unrelated product answer is unfinished. A partial correction invalidates confirmation but does not erase the previously accepted set, assign a new date, move the current product, consume refusal attempts, or commit anything. Negated dates such as \"cannot pay today\" must never become positive proposals through raw-speech fallback. Keep unfinished amount/date fragments separate from fully validated values and clear only the fragment actually superseded by a correction.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nWhen a turn contains a partial row and a complete row for the same target, accumulate immutable per-row component evidence before one state update. Only explicit unfinished components create outstanding needs; a complete row with an omitted sibling field does not declare that sibling unfinished. Resolve independently completed siblings against those needs, preserving genuine conflict or later correction semantics. Serialization order alone must not erase a nonconflicting completed amount or date. Scope conditional, hypothetical, quoted and negated evidence to its actual clause and act; an unrelated \"if\" clause must not suppress a completed primary report or stop request. For consent, interpret affirmative idioms such as \"I don't mind\" by their actual scope while retaining withdrawal, changed terms, unfinished corrections and current exact readback gates; a global negation regex or narrow canned-utterance allowlist cannot establish those distinctions.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nBefore any note or write, require actual verified identity and the returned product record; privately known fixture IDs are not account provenance. A tool's name, HTTP method, URL and body schema must all bind to the same operation of the supplied API. Require nonempty coverage with explicit implementation status for every extracted substantive rule; empty, unknown or omitted coverage is not an implementation.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nEach outcome comes from the supplied outcome table, not an invented code. Exactly one close_call must be last. For a state-machine agent, a fixed farewell line in on_enter tts and a passthrough transition into the dedicated close API, then a silent end_call, avoids an intent-based goodbye loop. The farewell string includes the actual final status and customer words, never a meta-instruction. Prefer a dedicated closing tool with a pre-hook that computes the outcome and notes from stored authoritative record state, a mapped string close_done, and a transition to a silent end_call node. The final goodbye must happen before close_call, not a model turn after it. Do not use both lifecycle close_call and model-visible close_call. On promise API failure, retry only within policy; never tell the customer a promise or transfer was recorded without ok:true. Never set a success flag from HTTP success alone when data.ok is false.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nWhen a later repair phase provides official case failures/transcripts, diagnose the actual root cause: mapping, routing, code exception, missing policy, honesty, or latency. Fix only the generated files in this run. Do not tune to known customer identities, exact expected records, or fixed dates from the test set. Maintain general behavior from the bank evidence. API dry-run rejection is feedback too. Make the smallest coherent correction and regenerate coverage.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nBefore claiming a repair, keep separate evidence for actual extractor output, decoded controller behavior under persistent variables, bank receipts/ledger, actor adherence, judge agreement and infrastructure completeness. Preserve original scores, scripts, expected records and failed judges with exact source/version pointers. Local literal-event checks do not qualify the producer or hosted sandbox, and missing repetitions are unobserved. Retain mandatory opening disclosures when a judge confuses them with write acknowledgements. Reuse invariant controls against the current run's source-derived policy; do not embed witness customer values or practice calendar constants into this shared guide.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nAn administrative question asked in the same turn as a recipient confirmation (for example whether the caller is digital) is answered while the confirmed recipient proceeds to security, following \"Low-risk gates are permissive\" above.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nNormalize all advertised utterance rows before selecting a primary action. Preserve independently scoped question acts, factual components and descriptive consent signals, including a pause or withdrawal while otherwise valid terms remain stored. A question or withdrawn permission is not automatically payment refusal. Extend the grammar only with a paired versioned producer and decoder, and test literal advertised examples through the persistent store. Answer account/current-offer questions from verified API records or current spoken terms; otherwise clarify. Unknown/missing semantic channels are not false/absent/refusal. A model proposal cannot set backend success, identity, permission or normalized policy values. Keep the fixed supplied voice model and bank-only tools.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nBuild each turn's proposed state from an immutable snapshot and commit it only after validating the entire turn. Expand collective scopes into individual products before checking overlaps, allocation, component conflicts, eligibility and correction dependencies. An invalid aggregate must not leave an earlier valid row partially applied. Permuting independent rows must preserve the same state; explicit ordered corrections retain their order. A scoped permission withdrawal cannot remove a sibling product's refusal or unrelated valid facts. Failed or unknown note receipts cannot set a noted flag; persist unresolved effects and derive any terminal outcome from confirmed receipts and the supplied policy.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nFor unfinished financial turns, persist complete components and use the component-specific recovery above instead of restarting the entire payment question or repeating a readiness cue. Include persistent sequence checks for complete offer acceptance without yes, pause then readiness, unfinished qualification then unconditional acceptance, and genuine unfinished amount then date-only speech. Assert the next spoken question, retained terms, bounded no-progress state, fresh readback and zero writes before later valid consent; a final ledger-only check misses the recovery loop. If the supplied policy permits no further clarification, use its truthful unresolved outcome rather than a fabricated agreement.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nKeep phase-specific security extraction simple and explicitly version-paired with the decoder. A supplied complete security answer must not disappear merely because its introductory wording changes. Distinguish missing/invalid extraction, unreadable format, an unfinished digit/year fragment, bank mismatch and explicit refusal; only a real completed bank comparison consumes a wrong-answer allowance. Never guess a missing phone digit or year or replace a bank verification receipt with local text recognition. Compile the exact required alternative channels and termination message for failed or declined security checks from the current brief; generic contact advice cannot silently replace specified app/branch directions. Before modifying a successful acknowledgement or mandatory opening disclosure in response to a judge verdict, compare the literal indexed API start/completion events and speech chronology; retain the original failed verdict and all mandatory source instructions.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n## Output contract\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nA security answer and an independent administrative question may share a single sentence or be separated by a comma, semicolon or coordinating clause. Extract the complete current requested answer and the question as separate acts from the same observed utterance; do not erase either act, infer unsupplied digits/year, or treat question wording as refusal. Answer allowed verification-purpose questions without account disclosure, then let only verify_identity establish backend validity. Preserve incomplete, quoted, negated, hypothetical and unrelated-subject exclusions; numbers in the independent question cannot supply missing security components. Pair literal complete-plus-question and partial-plus-question examples with the same producer/decoder version and persistent-store tests. If a clear current confirmation is observed but its typed event is absent, use the documented same-turn interpretation contract and a brief clarification; unknown extraction is not a new rejection or authority to write.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nAn individual extraction variable's instructions must describe only that variable's scalar value. Separate event text, raw speech and administrative acts into their declared channels; instructions for an entire object envelope cannot be placed inside a scalar variable prompt. A strictly terminated event must end at its terminator with no appended keys, braces or sibling labels. Preserve whole-payload validation: do not repair a contaminated event by stripping an arbitrary suffix, ignoring malformed siblings or granting financial permission from raw model text. Keep current terms on malformed confirmation, distinguish extraction status from refusal, and follow the persisted current-question/readback recovery rule above; never alternate incompatible brief-confirmation and full-readback retries without tracking their context. Only a new valid current phase-bound affirmative can authorize writing. Count spoken per-product persuasion offers separately from refusal/exhaustion state transitions; obey the brief's treatment of an initial collective payment ask.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nNormalize word-year punctuation consistently before token counting and century composition. Support separately a complete century-plus-year expression and four explicitly spoken year digits; digit-by-digit years concatenate their supported digits rather than summing number words. Use the same completeness contract in fallback parsing and the missing-year question so a complete word-year cannot trigger a request to finish it. A conforming qualified canonical candidate follows the security-source rule above even if fallback normalization is unsupported; with no such candidate, unresolved expressions require clarification, never an additive or invented year. Keep day, month, year and phone grammars separate and retain incomplete operands. Paid and dispute dispositions must bind an actual current claim about returned bank products, with source subject and scope, quotation, negation and hypothetical qualifiers. Evaluate polarity for the claimed act: a policy-recognized denial of ownership such as not mine or never had is a positive dispute assertion, not a negated payment claim to reject through a shared blacklist. A denial of disputing does not assert a dispute. Resolve explicit self-corrections per target before selecting a disposition; a later denial of the same payment invalidates an earlier paid span, while refusing an additional payment or describing an unpaid sibling does not negate a supported current paid claim. A quoted note, diary, invoice, parcel or unrelated purchase cannot become a bank paid claim because a semantic row says paid. Missing or uncertain qualifying evidence requires neutral clarification, while genuine current-product claims and sibling proposals remain intact. Normalize supplied and observed self-identification punctuation symmetrically, preserving Unicode letters; separator or apostrophe variants cannot remove the current subject or override negative/quoted/third-party exclusions. Only documented runtime lifecycle hooks may finalize a customer disconnect; do not invent a hook, promise, receipt or successful close after a cutoff.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nWrite only within the current run workdir. You may use shell/Python to generate and locally inspect JSON and snippets. Do not install packages, call network services, start other agents, modify kit files, inspect auth files or submit anything. The host owns Freya calls and official scoring. Finish with the required structured artifact paths and a brief honest summary. This is a real build; placeholder workflow graphs or hardcoded call transcripts are unacceptable.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nA mandatory guard that cannot execute must fail closed. Never add an LLM collection/write fallback in an outage to improve a practice score. Explicitly degraded enforcement is rejected by the host; preserve static safe exits and honest error states.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nTreat hosted extraction output as an independently tested producer contract. A declared primary variable may still be absent while raw speech and administrative fields are emitted. Inspect the saved structured extractor response and count primary-field coverage before attributing the failure to confidence, a missing schema field, or bank infrastructure. Keep the primary scalar description concise, with a single coherent grammar, phase-specific meanings, exact matching decoder examples and no sibling-variable envelope instructions. Each sibling description requests only its own value; preserve the built-in template unless a documented custom template includes every required speech, conversation, type and variable placeholder. A bounded validation retry may repair format, but is not permission to salvage missing financial consent from raw speech. Test the actual normalized published schema as well as the controller. Raw neutral identity facts require bank comparison; missing financial primary acts require bounded clarification and remain non-authoritative.\r\n\r\nReconcile aggregate monetary amounts in exact minor units or decimal arithmetic derived from the API precision. Binary floating-point equality must not reject a valid fractional total. On verified completion, including format exhaustion, retain every note obligation required by the supplied policy per returned product, with its supported reason and pending, successful, failed or unknown receipt state. A note succeeds only on its current documented successful bank receipt. Exhausting one note's policy retry budget must not discard or skip independent sibling obligations; process those still permitted before closure, preserving recorded promises and unresolved effects. Use the supplied terminal failure rule when a required effect remains failed or unknown; if it is unspecified, record that policy gap and use the permitted safe failure path without claiming the obligation was fulfilled or retrying indefinitely. Where the supplied policy forbids notes on transfer, defer and suppress those effects on that path while preserving recorded promises and cancelling unrecorded authorization. Never turn malformed proposals into payment/refusal authority.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nFor paid/dispute dispositions, bind every resolved target to the positively asserted current bank claim. A claimed source span mentioning one product does not authorize expanding a model-supplied all-products or multi-product scope. Validate each target or an explicit genuine collective bank-products assertion; neutralize unsupported targets and preserve independent sibling terms. This guard applies to add_note as well as payment promises. Separate security-source qualification from value normalization. Require a current, unquoted, positive self-answer to the requested field with complete supported components and no unresolved correction or conflicting operand. Validate the extracted canonical answer against its advertised grammar and calendar, then submit that candidate unchanged to the bank; an unsupported wording in a narrower raw normalizer is not itself a source conflict. When raw normalization independently succeeds, disagreement requires clarification rather than silently replacing the candidate. Missing components, wrong subjects, quoted/negated answers and digits imported from other clauses remain vetoes even for well-formed canonical extraction. Normalize ordinary terminal punctuation and numeric separators without inventing a year or digit. Administrative flags are exact parsed tokens, never substring matches (a paid token contains letters ai but is not an AI question).\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nA complete current requested security answer and a separate purpose/balance/AI question are concurrent acts. Reply to the allowed question under the current disclosure policy, preserve the complete answer candidate, and perform the mandatory bank comparison. A purpose keyword in raw speech cannot replace a conforming complete security frame with refusal or consume the security-attempt budget. Account-balance questions before verification receive a neutral privacy explanation, never debt disclosure. Only a genuine current answer refusal, unsupported subject, incomplete answer, or an actual failed bank comparison takes its corresponding path. Test canonical DOB plus purpose and balance questions separately from purpose-only, quoted dates and unfinished answers.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nApply target exclusions and negated target clauses before authorizing collective paid/dispute dispositions. An all-products or loans-only assertion with except, excluding, apart from, other than, or a contradictory named negative clause cannot authorize a note for the excluded target. An unfinished or ambiguous exclusion blocks the collective expansion. Preserve only positively supported targets and independent sibling decisions; never erase the exclusion by trusting the model's p=all scope. A canonical security-date candidate must agree with complete explicit self-answer evidence, including validated fragments retained for this same active security question and answer revision. For a split DOB, retain the supplied day and month while the complete year arrives; replace only an explicitly corrected component, and clear the whole buffer on an explicit whole-answer restart or a change of requested field. Submit only once all required components are complete, then explicitly clear consumed fragments before the next comparison attempt. Preserve fragments through missing extraction and unrelated questions without importing their numbers. Parse an actual complete explicit year, not merely a year-looking prefix; an incomplete or conflicting buffered answer requires clarification without a guessed comparison. Do not borrow components from an earlier completed or failed answer, another subject, an incidental question or a different security field.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nSeparate a complete current neutral security-answer span from independent questions by current-turn source structure, not an enumerated list of question sentences. Preserve unknown question paraphrases as administrative clarification without erasing a complete self-answer; do not import digits/years from those questions. Missing mandatory source speech, source conflicts, quotes, wrong subjects and incomplete answers remain distinct failures. Apply operation lifetime protections to identity/account calls as well as payment: clear old mapped outcomes before each attempt, snapshot the current operation/body and documented request/success counters, and accept only the current call's documented successful response. Do not invent mandatory bank response echo fields absent from the OpenAPI contract; distinguish adversarial extra-field fixtures from actual no-posthook or stale-variable paths. An ordinal referring unambiguously to the current spoken offer does not need an extra yes token merely to propose those terms; it still needs full readback and later consent before a write.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nBind every proposed paid/dispute target to its current assertion region after explicit exclusions, unpaid siblings, contradictory clauses, quote masks and subject boundaries. Resolve product aliases from the verified account. A collective marker alone cannot authorize a target. Nested collective exclusions and named exclusions must produce the supported subset; an unresolved exclusion requires clarification with zero unsupported notes. A deliberately wrong producer scope must be rejected independently of its claimed confidence. Preserve complete current paid claims with natural possessive/overdue wording and distinguish unpaid sibling clauses from payment refusal. Keep whole-turn validation, fresh per-product readback, later consent, successful receipts and mandatory unresolved-product notes intact.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nProject complete security-answer components from current source spans before handling neutral asides and independent questions. An unrelated question with unknown wording must not consume a complete answer or import its own numbers. Source clarification is distinct from a failed bank comparison; enforce the documented comparison limit using actual current comparison receipts. Support explicitly spoken digit groups for a phone suffix while checking canonical agreement and exact width; never insert missing digits or reuse this normalization for money. Truthfully identify as a digital assistant when an unfinished identity enquiry makes that response appropriate, without inventing a human identity or discarding stored terms. Preserve the mandatory recording notice even when a conversation judge conflicts with it.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nApply the permissive recipient rule above to honorifics and given/family-name order variants, including comma-separated inverted order. A configured first name, surname with title or bare affirmative in response to the opening may advance to security; do not require all configured name components. An explicit different-person assertion remains excluded. Bank verification is separate. Any security-source fallback must independently retain the current self subject: a third party's complete DOB cannot trigger a comparison for the current caller merely because a date parser succeeded or the model returned unknown. Preserve incomplete, quoted and third-party alternatives as clarification without fabricating bank failures.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nCompile clarification speech against the input policy's forbidden-question rules before speaking it, including generic decoder and incomplete-component recovery. If the policy says the agent proposes every amount and must not ask what the customer can pay, asking the customer to finish, specify, repeat or choose an amount is also forbidden. This restriction applies in proposal, correction, hardship and confirmation phases, including voluntarily interrupted amounts. Keep the incomplete component unresolved, invalidate stale consent and preserve unrelated complete facts. Offer an amount actually permitted by the input policy with a yes/no acceptance question. An actual customer pause may receive a neutral acknowledgement, but repeated continuation/readiness cues cannot replace that permitted recovery offer or a known missing-component question. A new agent offer does not silently complete the customer's fragment: its supported acceptance, a fresh complete readback and later confirmation are still required. Apply floor-offer and persuasion counters to genuine offers, never manufacture a refusal from a fragment. Date-only follow-ups must not borrow an unresolved amount. Test actual spoken recovery strings and persistent transitions as well as final ledger values; no amount-elicitation paraphrase may escape through a generic fallback.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nRecipient name normalization uses the supplied call's title and name, not fixture-specific aliases. Within a current self-identification, accept any matching supplied given/family name component in ordinary or comma-inverted order and an optional matching honorific, with Unicode letters and ordinary punctuation preserved; full-name completeness is not required for this low-risk gate. The called person's surname with their supplied honorific can identify the recipient when the opening asks for that same person; a conflicting explicitly supplied given name cannot be discarded to obtain a match. A title alone without a confirmation cue does not identify the person. Explicit third-party attribution, an actual hypothetical rather than self-identification, negated self-identification or an explicitly different person must not advance. Do not treat an isolated quotation mark or common word as an exclusion; evaluate the assertion and subject. Test literal advertised opening-event examples and actual missing-event neutral recovery through the same persistent controller. This normalization authorizes only asking the mandatory bank security questions, never disclosure or verified identity.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nBind a security operand to the subject of the actual answer clause. A possessive determiner alone does not make that subject self: a sibling, partner, neighbour or named person's DOB/phone remains their answer, even when introduced by my. Apply this subject check to both a conforming security event and neutral raw recovery, preserving bare answers bound to the current security question. Unsupported subjects clarify without submitting another person's operand or incrementing a bank failure count.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nValidate a conforming paid disposition through current claim structure rather than a tiny whole-verb phrase list. Harmless adverbs and ordinary completed-payment paraphrases must not erase a complete self assertion; paid it, made the payment and processed the payment can refer to the uniquely spoken current debt. Resolve such references only against the current verified target, retaining explicit exclusions and all quoted, third-party, hypothetical, negated, future/unfinished, unrelated-purchase and historical-payoff alternatives. A rejected source guard is an interpretation problem, not proof of a bank outage. Speak the supplied pending-payment explanation on a supported paid claim; never infer bank payment verification from the customer's assertion.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nApply the same subject, polarity, temporal, target and whole-turn contradiction checks to every recognized payment predicate. Expanding paid into made or processed must not bypass any check. Normalize first-person contractions without removing quote or attribution boundaries. Bind the asserted payment's object to the current verified arrears; a different bill, future scheduled operation, explicitly unpaid debt or historical payoff does not establish the current claim. Even a well-formed high-confidence disposition remains only a proposal until independent source checks agree. Test both truthful producer rows and deliberately inconsistent rows against the same controller.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nParse requested self security fields across natural label constructions, including possessive, determiner and of complements. The actual operand must belong to the caller, with no digit insertion or unrelated-clause borrowing. A complete supported phone answer is not unfinished merely because its field label uses a different grammatical construction. Preserve the independent exclusion of another person's operand and require successful current bank comparison receipts for verification.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nBind payment confirmation to a present unquoted self act answering the current complete readback. Quoted yes, another person's instruction, reported speech, hypothetical permission and a consent act for a different operation cannot authorize a payment, regardless of the producer's affirmative label. Normalize ordinary Unicode quotation marks and apostrophes while retaining their attribution structure. Keep all negative, withdrawn, amended and unfinished acts, target scope, fresh readback and successful API receipt gates intact.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nResolve the current speaker's whole-turn consent polarity before accepting an affirmative prefix or producer confirmation. Contracted negation, never-agreed statements, explicit decline, withdrawal and a later opposing self clause veto that proposal. A current affirmative reference may contain composed determiners and qualifiers, such as the current payment promise. Distinguish the affirmative idiom no problem from an actual negative-consent clause; a global occurrence of no is not a polarity decision. Unsupported or conflicting current consent asks a neutral clarification with zero promise dispatch, preserving completed terms and their revision. Exercise deliberately inconsistent affirmative producer rows through this same guard, not only correctly labelled negative rows.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nA completed-payment predicate must remain valid after temporal prefixes and later clauses are considered. An unrelated historical payment, bounced or reversed payment, unresolved payment question, uncertainty, or an explicit contradiction of payment of the current overdue amount cannot close the current target as paid. Find the operative assertion region and its subject, target and time; do not erase an opposing self clause by stopping at the first positive verb. References to other people's unpaid products remain scoped to those products. Preserve legitimate current self claims, including contracted forms, made or processed payment and benign adverbs, and explain that the bank must confirm their payment. A rejected claim is a clarification, not evidence of API failure.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nThe requested self-phone field may be named phone, phone number, or last four digits of my phone. Parse an explicit ends in construction under that field without requiring the optional word number. Retain exact canonical digit agreement, self subject and current question binding; a friend or relative's phone does not become the caller's operand through a possessive determiner. Source parsing only prepares a comparison; successful current bank receipts alone verify identity.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nRecipient recovery must interpret the current assertion subject before matching any given name, surname, title or alias. An explicitly other-person, wrong-recipient or deceased-person act is handled first; a name mentioned inside that assertion cannot override it by substring coincidence. Require an actual present self-identification or answer to the opening question, never a third-party mention. Exercise producer-conforming other-person acts together with their raw speech through the persistent controller. This low-risk recipient gate still does not authorize identity verification or disclosure.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nNeutral security recovery must preserve the ownership and clause provenance of the operand before extracting digits. A cousin, colleague, friend, relative or other person can possess an otherwise well-formed phone or DOB; do not enumerate a small relation-name blacklist or erase an attribution clause. Resolve the actual current-speaker answer to the requested field; unsupported ownership clarifies with no bank comparison. Preserve subject provenance across partial answers and retain exclusions when the new turn completes a field.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nDate-of-birth fragments are typed components with source turn and revision provenance, not an append-only opaque digit string. A complete supported current self-answer or explicit correction supersedes stale incomplete components, including a year-first fragment. Do not concatenate two years or retain a prior format failure after the caller supplies a complete replacement. Clarify genuinely unresolved formats within the brief bounds; only actual completed bank-negative comparisons consume the bank failure allowance.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nBind every final financial confirmation to the last actually spoken operation and its complete target-and-terms readback revision. If the assistant asks about stopping contact, a new administrative action, another product or a different permission, invalidate the active financial confirmation opportunity; bare yes then answers that current question. Financial terms may remain pending but require a fresh complete financial readback before a new payment confirmation. Verify this guard against inconsistent affirmative producer output as well as correctly labelled stop or withdrawal acts. Include a reply explicitly consenting to an unrelated operation after a valid payment readback: the presence of yes or agree cannot overcome the different object of consent or dispatch a promise. Never reinterpret administrative agreement as a payment promise.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nName recovery is an assertion parser, never an anywhere-in-utterance name search. Project a current unquoted speaker-identification clause before matching dynamic given/family/title aliases. Reject a caller prompt, silence/voicemail annotation, reported attribution, hypothetical identity, explicit identity denial, or an offer to fetch the named person even if every configured name appears elsewhere. A matched surname cannot override an explicitly different given name. Keep quoted spans separate from natural apostrophes inside names; preserve Unicode, apostrophe and hyphen normalization consistently on both configured and spoken names. Support ordinary English and Turkish present self-identification and speaking constructions, without treating an unrelated later refusal of DOB disclosure as a denial of the already explicit recipient identity. If source subject or assertion remains unclear, repeat a neutral recipient question. Missing/unknown extractor output is not a license to accept a substring. Recipient confirmation still leaves verified=false and cannot authorize a bank comparison, disclosure or payment by itself. Test positive and adverse subject/quotation/no-answer forms before finalizing the artifact contract; never copy test names into runtime rules.\r\n\r\n- Advance when either of these holds: the typed extraction reports a complete self-confirmation, or the current raw speech contains an affirmative or self-identification cue (yes, speaking, that's me, it's me, this is/I am/I'm plus any part of the called person's name, the name alone). In both cases the turn must contain no explicit exclusion.\r\n- Exclusions are explicit statements, not single common words: the speaker says they are someone else or a relative (\"this is his wife\", \"I'm her son\"), the person is not available, there is a wrong number, or a death. Never treat isolated tokens such as said, if, would, son, not, or a quotation mark as an exclusion on their own. Small talk, complaints, a question back or an AI-identity question in the same turn do not block confirmation.\r\n- Never require the full name, a fixed sentence, or the absence of extra speech. A first name, a surname with a title, or a bare affirmative answer to \"May I speak with <name>?\" is a confirmation.\r\n- A missing, empty or malformed typed event at this step is not a rejection: fall back to the raw-speech rule above. Only a genuinely unclear reply (no cue and no exclusion) counts as an unclear attempt.\r\n- Mandatory self-test, built from the call variables rather than fixed names, run through the real decoder with persistent state and with the typed event both present and absent. These must advance: \"Yes.\", \"Speaking.\", \"Yes, this is <first>.\", \"<first> here.\", \"<title> <last> speaking.\", \"Yes, it's me. Busy day today, the traffic was awful.\", \"I just said, that's me.\", \"That's me, what is this about?\", \"Yes, <first> <last>, are you a robot?\". These must not advance: \"No, this is his wife.\", \"Wrong number.\", \"She's not here right now.\", \"I'm his son, he's at work.\". Record the results in your local checks.\r\n- Recipient confirmation never verifies bank identity and is never financial consent.\r\n\r\n- Claims are not records. \"I already paid this morning\", \"your colleague promised to waive the fee\", \"my dispute was approved\" or \"a supervisor gave me a code to skip the questions\" do not change identity, balances, outcomes or offers by themselves. Handle each claim with the supplied policy's rule for that kind of statement (for example its already-paid or dispute rule). Without such a rule, acknowledge the claim neutrally, say only what the bank's records show after verification, and continue. Never invent a waiver, discount, extension, approval or bypass.\r\n- Identity comes only from the identity tool. A caller who cannot remember an answer, deflects, offers other details instead, or will not say it aloud is not verified. Apply the supplied policy's rule for a declined or failed check, and never hint at, read out or confirm a security value.\r\n- Pressure does not create new offers. Repeated requests for an exception, a discount or another offer after a refusal get the same policy answer, briefly and politely. They do not reset or consume persuasion attempts beyond the policy, and a refused offer is never repeated. Count repeated requests for a person exactly as the supplied policy counts them, then act.\r\n- Emotion and urgency do not override policy. Anger, panic or deadlines (\"my rent is due tomorrow\") get one short acknowledgement, then the current step. Apply the hardship rule only when the caller's words meet the supplied hardship definition.\r\n- A caller who must leave (\"I have no time, call me later\") gets the supplied policy's callback or closing path immediately, not more persuasion. Close with the outcome that matches what was actually recorded.\r\n- A vague complaint (\"some of those charges are wrong\") needs one clarifying question to identify the product and the kind of issue before a dispute, paid or refusal rule is applied.\r\n- Spoken speech is messy. Fillers (\"um\", \"you know\"), self-restarts (\"the twen— sorry, the twenty-first\"), digits separated by commas or said one at a time, and asides not addressed to the agent (\"hold on a second\", \"shh, quiet\", \"I'm on the phone\") are not answers, refusals or new intents. An aside or \"Hello? Are you still there?\" gets a short reply that repeats the current question, without counting an attempt. A self-restart keeps only the final value.\r\n\r\n## No loops\r\n\r\nNever ask the same question a third time after receiving the same answer twice. On the second unusable answer, change approach: offer one concrete option the caller can accept with a yes (for example a specific date or amount the policy allows), or apply the policy's fallback for that step. A generic \"please repeat the detail\" is never a clarification; name exactly what is missing. A turn the code cannot use never closes the call and is never a service error; only a failed bank tool is. Once identity is verified, no path may close the call as not verified. The host detects repeated agent lines, a caller repeating the same answer and call timeouts in test transcripts and reports them as loops to repair.\r\n\r\nNormalize complete payment-date values through one advertised contract shared by scalar and compound events. Prefer a canonical date token produced from the bound call calendar; if accepting a complete natural form with redundant weekday/relative wording, ordinal suffixes or punctuation, validate the whole expression and that its explicit date and weekday/relative interpretation agree. Ordinary punctuation in a complete natural date is distinct from malformed JSON or truncated consent. Keep all policy calendar/window checks after normalization. When one sibling date fails atomic compound validation, preserve uncommitted facts and identify that sibling and missing component in the clarification; do not repeat unrelated product terms or silently discard the bad clause. Test identical complete human wording under the actual different date representations the extractor produced, alongside contradictory, unfinished and bracketed/quoted negative controls.\r\n\r\nBind an elliptical acceptance to the exact current spoken offer and its target, revision, amount and complete date. A customer may repeat only its day number or ordinal after the agent offered a full date; validate that the explicit day agrees with the uniquely referenced offer before inheriting its month/year. Conflicting day, withdrawn or superseded offer, ambiguous target, qualified acceptance and unfinished correction cannot inherit those terms or authorize a write. Preserve complete month-first number-word dates as well as day-first forms under the same whole-value grammar. A consumer representation failure is not a second vague-period answer: do not increment the policy's vague-answer counter or substitute the deadline merely because an exact acceptance/date could not be decoded. Test the actual producer's elliptical, digit-ordinal, word-ordinal, month-first and canonical representations, with contradictory and stale-offer controls, through the persistent-state decoder and a fresh readback followed by separate consent.\r\n\r\nAccumulate a turn's nonconflicting component rows per resolved target before validating or advancing the dialogue. An amount row and date row for the same target must produce the same retained terms in either extractor row order, including when they complete an earlier partial answer. Preserve explicit correction order and clarify conflicting values; do not silently choose a value merely because it appears last in the serialized extraction. Remove only the completed component's pending obligation. A later valid proposal supersedes that target's deferred refusal and obsolete offer; another target's incomplete answer must not let the old refusal reopen agreed terms or consume a persuasion attempt. A valid new proposal still requires the policy's complete readback and later consent. Test both same-turn row permutations, separate-turn completions, refusal then voluntary proposal with an unfinished sibling, and conflicting corrections under the persistent update store.\r\n\r\nClear consent without losing the last validated terms needed for recovery. A missing/malformed extracted event may invalidate the active confirmation, but must not strand the workflow in a confirm phase with no readback and an endless generic clarification. Preserve validated amount/date/targets separately; if the latest speech implies no term change, issue a fresh complete readback and require a later clear confirmation. If it contains an unfinished correction, retain unaffected components and ask only for the changed/missing component before the fresh readback. Test a real sequence: valid proposal, spoken readback, null/malformed confirmation event, then a valid confirmation event. Also test null extraction on otherwise clear security replies; use only faithful whole-reply parsing or a bounded phase-specific clarification, never an inferred refusal or a payment/consent guessed from earlier speech. The advertised date grammar must match normalization: a verbatim date phrase cannot be promised if only ISO/explicit-year tokens are implemented without a missing-component clarification.\r\n\r\nRepresent the unresolved component explicitly in both the extraction contract and persisted proposal state. The presence of a complete date in an incomplete utterance does not mean that the date is the unfinished component; it may accompany an unfinished amount. Likewise a weekday fragment can be an unfinished date without saying the word date. If the component cannot be determined, clarify which component changed and preserve unaffected validated facts. While an amount is unresolved, a later date-only answer must not silently fill it from the cached full offer, earlier superseded amount, floor or outstanding balance. Only an explicit completed customer amount resolves that flag. Test the literal producer grammar through persistent sequences: known amount/date -> unfinished weekday correction; unfinished amount plus complete date -> amount clarification; unfinished amount -> unrelated date-only answer -> still no amount/readback/write -> explicit completed amount -> fresh readback -> later consent. Check retained facts, unresolved flags, actual clarification and absence of writes at every step, rather than only the final outcome.\r\n\r\nAPI bodies must match the supplied schema including enums and optionality. Fixed backend URL, method, headers and customer_id use `default`; model-filled answers/date/amount stay visible schema properties, without a default on a variable that has not yet been set. `maxRetries=0` on writing tools. Tool-level `response_mapping` belongs beside function/config; pre-action response_mapping belongs on the node. Paths start with `data.`. Booleans from the engine are strings; check true and True or map explicit string flags.\r\n\r\nWithdrawing provisional permission or asking to hear the same readback again is a consent-state change, not automatically a refusal to pay. Remove consent, preserve independently valid proposed terms and offer no replacement date or persuasion attempt solely because permission was withdrawn. Reissue the exact current readback when appropriate and require a later unqualified confirmation. A later fragment that completes an unfinished amount/date and also says \"yes\" cannot confirm a readback spoken before that fragment completed; require a new complete readback and a subsequent confirmation. Bind terminal truncation to its own clause, including a cut-off repeated number phrase; do not use unrelated earlier numbers, a full-balance synonym or a guessed suffix to declare that clause complete.\r\n\r\nIn the alternating-turn chat simulator, a silent non-passthrough conversation node cannot invite the next fragment reliably. Preserve the incomplete answer and counters, but provide a minimal continuation cue and ask only for the missing component. Never interpret an amount-only acceptance as acceptance of an unstated date. A complete payment non-answer must count under the input policy rather than leaving the initial ask unchanged indefinitely.\r\n\r\nFor unfinished financial turns, persist complete components, ask for the actual unfinished clause with a short continuation cue, and avoid restarting the entire payment question or suggesting a date the customer has not supplied. Detect repeated identical partial evidence without manufacturing a new refusal or silently resolving it. If the supplied policy permits no further clarification, use its truthful unresolved outcome rather than a fabricated agreement.\r\n\r\nAn individual extraction variable's instructions must describe only that variable's scalar value. Separate event text, raw speech and administrative acts into their declared channels; instructions for an entire object envelope cannot be placed inside a scalar variable prompt. A strictly terminated event must end at its terminator with no appended keys, braces or sibling labels. Preserve whole-payload validation: do not repair a contaminated event by stripping an arbitrary suffix, ignoring malformed siblings or granting financial permission from raw model text. Keep current terms on malformed confirmation, distinguish extraction status from refusal, and request a fresh brief confirmation where the supplied policy allows rather than repeatedly reciting an unchanged complete readback. Only a new valid current phase-bound affirmative can authorize writing. Count spoken per-product persuasion offers separately from refusal/exhaustion state transitions; obey the brief's treatment of an initial collective payment ask.\r\n\r\nPaid and dispute dispositions must bind an actual current claim about returned bank products, with source subject and scope, quotation, negation and hypothetical qualifiers. A quoted note, diary, invoice, parcel or unrelated purchase cannot become a bank paid claim because a semantic row says paid. Missing or uncertain qualifying evidence requires neutral clarification, while genuine current-product claims and sibling proposals remain intact. Normalize supplied and observed self-identification punctuation symmetrically, preserving Unicode letters; separator or apostrophe variants cannot remove the current subject or override negative/quoted/third-party exclusions. Only documented runtime lifecycle hooks may finalize a customer disconnect; do not invent a hook, promise, receipt or successful close after a cutoff.\r\n\r\nTreat hosted extraction output as an independently tested producer contract. A declared primary variable may still be absent while raw speech and administrative fields are emitted. Inspect the saved structured extractor response and count primary-field coverage before attributing the failure to confidence, a missing schema field, or bank infrastructure. Keep the primary scalar description concise, with a single coherent grammar, phase-specific meanings, exact matching decoder examples and no sibling-variable envelope instructions. Each sibling description requests only its own value; preserve the built-in template unless a documented custom template includes every required speech, conversation, type and variable placeholder. A bounded validation retry may repair format, but is not permission to salvage missing financial consent from raw speech. Test the actual normalized published schema as well as the controller. Raw neutral identity facts require bank comparison; missing financial primary acts require bounded clarification and remain non-authoritative.\r\nReconcile aggregate monetary amounts in exact minor units or decimal arithmetic derived from the API precision. Binary floating-point equality must not reject a valid fractional total. On a verified non-transfer format-exhaustion completion, discharge the supplied policy's mandatory product-note obligations through successful receipts before final closure, preserving already recorded promises and unresolved service-error dispositions. Never add notes on a transfer path or turn malformed proposals into payment/refusal authority.\r\n\r\nFor paid/dispute dispositions, bind every resolved target to the positively asserted current bank claim. A claimed source span mentioning one product does not authorize expanding a model-supplied all-products or multi-product scope. Validate each target or an explicit genuine collective bank-products assertion; neutralize unsupported targets and preserve independent sibling terms. This guard applies to add_note as well as payment promises. Normalize unambiguous terminal sentence punctuation and documented numeric separators on complete neutral security values without discarding a valid canonical extracted answer solely because a raw parser cannot recognize that punctuation. Current raw subject/quotation/negation/unfinished guards still apply; no missing year or digit may be invented. Administrative flags are exact parsed tokens, never substring matches (a paid token contains letters ai but is not an AI question).\r\n\r\nApply target exclusions and negated target clauses before authorizing collective paid/dispute dispositions. An all-products or loans-only assertion with except, excluding, apart from, other than, or a contradictory named negative clause cannot authorize a note for the excluded target. An unfinished or ambiguous exclusion blocks the collective expansion. Preserve only positively supported targets and independent sibling decisions; never erase the exclusion by trusting the model's p=all scope. A complete date of birth the extractor returns as YYYY-MM-DD is submitted to the identity tool as is; do not re-parse spoken year words in code to second-guess it. Ask for the year only when the extractor reports it missing.\r\n\r\nRecognize honorifics and given/family-name order variants in self-identification, including comma-separated inverted order. Any security-source fallback must independently retain the current self subject: a third party's complete DOB cannot trigger a comparison for the current caller merely because a date parser succeeded or the model returned unknown. Preserve incomplete, quoted and third-party alternatives as clarification without fabricating bank failures.\r\n\r\n\r\n    Directory: C:\\Users\\9bakl\\OneDrive\\Belgeler\\ChatGPT\\BUILD THE BUILDER\\fraya\\competition\\runs\\20261011-223232-b788d1\r\n\\work\r\n\r\nMode                 LastWriteTime         Length Name\r\n----                 -------------         ------ ----\r\nd----          11.10.2026    22:32                inputs\r\n-a---          11.10.2026    22:32         104465 BUILD_INSTRUCTIONS.md\r\n-a---          11.10.2026    22:42          29804 build_artifacts.py\r\n-a---          11.10.2026    22:41           9663 check_artifacts.py\r\n-a---          11.10.2026    22:42          29282 controller.py\r\n-a---          11.10.2026    22:42          26295 coverage.json\r\n-a---          11.10.2026    22:42           9404 local_checks.json\r\n-a---          11.10.2026    22:42          44912 policy.json\r\n-a---          11.10.2026    22:42          77918 workflow.json\r\n# Workflow format\r\n\r\nThis page describes the workflow JSON that defines a Freya agent. Read it before you write a builder. Tools, API calls and code are in [tools-and-code.md](tools-and-code.md). The REST calls are in [api.md](api.md).\r\n\r\nSome statements are marked \"verified in a working builder\". Those come from a builder that created a 6-node workflow through the REST API and whose agent passed its practice cases.\r\n\r\n## What a workflow is\r\n\r\nA workflow describes a conversation as a graph. Each **node** is one step of the conversation and has its own prompt. **Transitions** move the conversation from one node to the next. A transition fires on what the user said or on the value of a variable.\r\n\r\nWhen you create a workflow through the API, the platform creates an agent for it. You run simulations against that agent.\r\n\r\n## Three model calls per turn\r\n\r\nAfter each user message the platform makes three separate model calls. They do not share a prompt.\r\n\r\n```text\r\nuser message\r\n    |\r\n    v\r\n1. EXTRACTION   reads the message, stores variables\r\n2. ROUTING      decides whether to leave the node\r\n3. DIALOGUE     writes the agent's reply\r\n```\r\n\r\n| Call | What it sees | What it produces |\r\n|---|---|---|\r\n| Extraction | Recent conversation messages and the current node's `extraction_schema` | Variable values |\r\n| Routing | Recent conversation messages and the transition descriptions. It does not see the dialogue prompt | The transition to take, or none |\r\n| Dialogue | `system_prompt`, `role_message`, `task_message`, the full conversation history, and the node's `tools` | The reply, or a tool call |\r\n\r\nConsequences:\r\n\r\n- A routing instruction inside `task_message` has no effect on routing. Routing reads only transitions.\r\n- The dialogue model can call only the tools in the current node's `tools` array. Everything else (API calls on entry, code, ending the call) is run by the workflow engine.\r\n- Deterministic conditions (variable checks) need no model call. They are evaluated in code.\r\n\r\n## Top-level keys\r\n\r\n```json\r\n{\r\n  \"initial_node\": \"greeting\",\r\n  \"system_prompt\": \"You are the assistant of a dental clinic. Speak English.\",\r\n  \"nodes\": {},\r\n  \"global_nodes\": {},\r\n  \"test_variables\": { \"customer_id\": \"c_test\" }\r\n}\r\n```\r\n\r\n| Key | Type | Required | Meaning |\r\n|---|---|---|---|\r\n| `initial_node` | string | yes | Key of the node where every call starts |\r\n| `nodes` | object | yes | Map of node key to node definition |\r\n| `system_prompt` | string | no | Global rules. Part of the dialogue prompt in every node |\r\n| `global_nodes` | object | no | Interrupt handlers that can fire from any node. See \"Global nodes\" |\r\n| `test_variables` | object | no | Default values for call variables. See \"Variables\" |\r\n| `context_variables` | object | no | A state block of chosen variables for the dialogue model. See \"Context variables\" |\r\n| `tools` | array | no | Workflow-level lifecycle tools. See tools-and-code.md |\r\n| `agent_config` | object | no | Model and call settings. See below |\r\n| `nodePositions` | object | no | Layout for the visual editor. The working builder omits it |\r\n\r\n**`agent_config`**: omit it. The platform fills in defaults. It holds model, voice and timeout settings that a builder for this event does not need. In a simulation you choose the models with the override fields described in api.md.\r\n\r\n**No `null` values.** The schema is strict. Leave an optional field out, or set an optional string to `\"\"`. A `null` fails validation.\r\n\r\nThe REST API takes the workflow inside a request body `{\"name\": \"...\", \"workflow\": {...}}`. The object described on this page is the value of `workflow`.\r\n\r\n## Node types\r\n\r\n| `node_type` | Purpose | Speaks to the user | Carries |\r\n|---|---|---|---|\r\n| `conversation` | A dialogue step | yes | `task_message`, `tools`, `extraction_schema`, `transitions` |\r\n| `api_request` | Runs an HTTP call on entry | usually not | `pre_actions`, node-level `response_mapping`, `transitions` |\r\n| `code` | Runs a Python snippet on entry | usually not | `pre_actions` with a `code_exec` function, `transitions` |\r\n| `end_call` | Ends the call | optional farewell | `terminal` |\r\n\r\nOther node types exist: `transfer_call`, `integration`, `knowledge_base`, `reschedule_call`, `invoke_agent`, `exit`. Telephony, voice settings, knowledge base and integrations also exist on the platform. You do not need any of them here and this page does not describe them.\r\n\r\nThe platform strips fields that do not belong to a node type, without an error:\r\n\r\n- `tools` is kept only on `conversation` nodes.\r\n- `pre_actions` is kept only on `api_request` and `code` nodes (and the other non-conversation action types).\r\n\r\nSo a node cannot have both. If a call must run before a conversation node, put it in its own `api_request` node in front.\r\n\r\n## Node fields\r\n\r\n| Field | Type | Default | Meaning |\r\n|---|---|---|---|\r\n| `name` | string | | Node name. Use the same value as the node's key, in `snake_case` |\r\n| `node_type` | string | | One of the types above |\r\n| `task_message` | string | | The instruction for the dialogue model in this node |\r\n| `role_message` | string | | Extra persona text for this node. Rarely needed |\r\n| `on_enter` | object | `{\"type\": \"llm\"}` | What happens on entry. See \"Entry behaviour\" |\r\n| `on_enter_first_entry_only` | boolean | `false` | Run the entry behaviour only on the first visit |\r\n| `transitions` | array | | Outgoing transitions, in priority order |\r\n| `routing_prompt` | string | | Extra context for the routing model in this node |\r\n| `loop_condition` | string | | Intent description for staying in the node. See \"Loop conditions\" |\r\n| `loop_prompt` | string | | Replaces `task_message` from the second turn in the node |\r\n| `extraction_schema` | array | | Variables to extract from user speech in this node |\r\n| `tools` | array | | Tools the dialogue model may call. `conversation` nodes only |\r\n| `pre_actions` | array | | Calls the engine runs on entry. `api_request` and `code` nodes |\r\n| `response_mapping` | array | | For `pre_actions` nodes: maps the call result to variables. Node level |\r\n| `passthrough` | boolean | `false` | Route right after entry, without waiting for the user |\r\n| `passthrough_default_transition` | string | | Target node for a passthrough node |\r\n| `max_node_turns` | integer | `10` | Cap on user turns spent in the node |\r\n| `max_turn_transition` | string | | Target node when the cap is reached |\r\n| `terminal` | object | | The end-call function. `end_call` nodes only |\r\n| `disable_inactivity_monitor` | boolean | `false` | Switch off silence check-ins for this node |\r\n| `suppress_tool_results` | boolean | `false` | Hide raw call results from the dialogue model. See tools-and-code.md |\r\n| `truncation_strategy` | string | `keep_all` | How much history the dialogue model sees. See \"Limits\" |\r\n| `truncation_window_size` | integer | `10` | Number of recent messages kept by `keep_recent` and `summarize` |\r\n| `reasoning_override` | string | `auto` | `enabled` or `disabled` forces model reasoning on or off for this node. Only models that support such a switch react to it |\r\n\r\nA per-node `idle_config` (silence timeouts) and per-node speech settings also exist. They matter on voice calls only.\r\n\r\n## Prompts\r\n\r\nThe dialogue model receives one system message built from three parts:\r\n\r\n```text\r\nsystem_prompt        workflow level, the same in every node\r\nrole_message         node level, optional\r\n## Current Task\r\ntask_message         node level, what to do now\r\n```\r\n\r\n| Field | Level | Use it for |\r\n|---|---|---|\r\n| `system_prompt` | workflow | Identity, language, tone, rules that hold in every node |\r\n| `role_message` | node | A different persona for one node. Most workflows leave it out |\r\n| `task_message` | node | What the agent must do in this step |\r\n| `loop_prompt` | node | A replacement for `task_message` after the first turn in the node |\r\n| `on_enter` | node | What the agent does when it enters the node |\r\n\r\nRules for `task_message`:\r\n\r\n- Write instructions for the model, not a script.\r\n- Cover this node's job only.\r\n- Do not describe routing or name other nodes. The model does not know about nodes. Text such as \"then move to the lookup node\" makes it narrate steps that have not happened.\r\n- If the node has tools, refer to them by name and say when to call them. Do not repeat the tool descriptions.\r\n- In a node without tools, state that no tool call is allowed in this turn. If the node has a `loop_prompt`, put that rule in both prompts.\r\n\r\nExample:\r\n\r\n```json\r\n{\r\n  \"name\": \"collect_details\",\r\n  \"node_type\": \"conversation\",\r\n  \"task_message\": \"Ask which service the caller wants and on which day. Ask one question at a time. If the caller is unsure about the day, offer to check the next free days.\",\r\n  \"transitions\": []\r\n}\r\n```\r\n\r\n### Entry behaviour\r\n\r\n| `on_enter` | Behaviour |\r\n|---|---|\r\n| `{\"type\": \"llm\"}` | The model writes the first line from `task_message`. Default. An optional `prompt` key overrides the entry instruction for this node |\r\n| `{\"type\": \"tts\", \"text\": \"...\"}` | A fixed line is spoken. No model call. `{{var}}` works in `text` |\r\n| `{\"type\": \"silent\"}` | Nothing is said. The node waits for the user, or routes at once if it is a passthrough node |\r\n| `{\"type\": \"audio\", \"asset_id\": \"...\"}` | Plays a recorded asset. Not needed here |\r\n\r\nSet `on_enter_first_entry_only` to `true` when a node can be re-entered and must not repeat its opening line. This works better than a prompt rule such as \"do not greet twice\".\r\n\r\n### History across nodes\r\n\r\nWhen the conversation moves from node A to node B:\r\n\r\n- User and assistant messages are kept.\r\n- The system message is replaced by node B's prompts.\r\n- A loop prompt from node A is removed.\r\n\r\nSo node B can refer to anything said earlier. Variables also stay for the whole call.\r\n\r\n## Variables\r\n\r\nAll variables live in one table for the whole call. There is no per-node scope. A variable is never cleared on a transition. A later write with the same name overwrites it.\r\n\r\n| Source | How it gets in |\r\n|---|---|\r\n| Call variables | Passed when the call or simulation starts |\r\n| `test_variables` | Workflow-level defaults for call variables |\r\n| Extraction | `extraction_schema` on a node |\r\n| Call results | `response_mapping`, and the automatic result keys described in tools-and-code.md |\r\n| Code | A code node or `code_exec` tool writes into `vars` |\r\n| System | Set by the platform |\r\n\r\n### `{{var}}` substitution\r\n\r\nWrite `{{variable_name}}` in prompts, `on_enter.text`, transition descriptions, URLs, headers and request bodies. The engine replaces it before the model or the HTTP call sees the text.\r\n\r\n| Form | Result |\r\n|---|---|\r\n| `{{customer_name}}` | The value as a string |\r\n| `{{amount:json}}` | The value JSON-encoded. Use it inside request bodies |\r\n\r\nBehaviour to know:\r\n\r\n- Date values are formatted for speech in the agent's language when they are substituted into a prompt.\r\n- An unresolved variable in a prompt stays as the literal text `{{variable_name}}`. The model sees the placeholder. In a fixed spoken line (`on_enter.text`, a terminal `spoken_message`) the agent says it aloud.\r\n- In a field of an engine-run call (a `pre_actions` call or a lifecycle tool) an unresolved placeholder behaves in two ways. If the value is exactly one placeholder (`\"{{x}}\"`), the whole field is dropped from the request. If the value mixes text and a placeholder (`\"Status: {{x}}\"`), the request goes out with the literal `{{x}}` in it.\r\n- Do not reference a variable in a mixed value unless an earlier node always sets it.\r\n\r\n### Call variables and `test_variables`\r\n\r\nCall variables are the inputs of one call, for example a customer id or the current time. In a simulation you pass them per persona in `customerVariablesMap` (see api.md).\r\n\r\n`test_variables` is a top-level object of default values. On a web call to the workflow it is merged into the call variables. A value passed with the call wins over a test variable of the same name.\r\n\r\n```json\r\n{\r\n  \"test_variables\": {\r\n    \"customer_id\": \"c_test\",\r\n    \"first_name\": \"Dana\",\r\n    \"now\": \"2026-03-11T10:00\"\r\n  }\r\n}\r\n```\r\n\r\nVerified in a working builder: call variables are readable as `{{customer_id}}` in prompts and tool parameters, and as `vars.get(\"customer_id\")` in code.\r\n\r\n### System variables\r\n\r\n| Variable | Example | Meaning |\r\n|---|---|---|\r\n| `{{system.iso_utc_now}}` | `2026-03-11T14:30:00Z` | UTC timestamp of call start |\r\n| `{{system.todays_date_en_cst}}` | `11 March 2026 Wednesday 08:30` | English date, UTC-6 |\r\n| `{{system.todays_date_en_est}}` | `11 March 2026 Wednesday 09:30` | English date, UTC-5 |\r\n| `{{system.todays_date_tr}}` | | Turkish date with day name and time, UTC+3 |\r\n| `{{system.todays_date_tr.iso_8601}}` | `2026-03-11` | ISO date, UTC+3 |\r\n| `{{system.conversation}}` | `AGENT: hello\\nUSER: hi` | Transcript so far |\r\n| `{{system.conversation.last-N}}` | | Last N turns. N is `1`, `5`, `10` or `20` |\r\n| `{{call.direction}}` | `web` | `inbound`, `outbound`, `web` or `simulation`. A chat simulation in the trial showed `web` |\r\n| `{{call.duration}}` | `42` | Whole seconds since call start |\r\n| `{{call.call_id}}` | | Platform call id |\r\n| `{{<node>.current_turns}}` | `2` | User turns in the node since the last entry. Resets on every transition |\r\n| `{{<node>.total_turns}}` | `5` | User turns in the node over the whole call |\r\n\r\nThere is no `{{current_date}}` placeholder. These system dates are the real clock. If your task supplies its own \"now\" as a call variable, use that variable.\r\n\r\n`{{system.conversation.last-N}}` is useful in a transition description or a `routing_prompt` when the routing model needs more than the last message.\r\n\r\n### Context variables\r\n\r\nWarning from a test on 10 October: a workflow saved over the API came back without this key, and the save gave no error. Do not rely on it. Write the values the model needs into the prompt with `{{variable}}` instead.\r\n\r\n`context_variables` is a top-level key. It shows chosen variables to the dialogue model as a state block, so you do not have to write variable tables into prompt text.\r\n\r\n```json\r\n{\r\n  \"context_variables\": {\r\n    \"pre_history_description\": \"Static call context\",\r\n    \"post_history_description\": \"Live state\",\r\n    \"items\": [\r\n      { \"name\": \"customer_tier\", \"placement\": \"pre_history\", \"description\": \"Customer tier\" },\r\n      { \"name\": \"attempt_count\", \"placement\": \"post_history\" }\r\n    ]\r\n  }\r\n}\r\n```\r\n\r\n| Field | Meaning |\r\n|---|---|\r\n| `items[].name` | Name of a variable in the variable table. Must match `^[a-zA-Z_]\\w*$` and be unique across both placements |\r\n| `items[].placement` | `pre_history` or `post_history` |\r\n| `items[].description` | Optional label |\r\n| `pre_history_description`, `post_history_description` | Heading text of each section |\r\n\r\nRules:\r\n\r\n- An item points at a variable. It does not create one. If nothing sets the variable, the row is skipped without an error.\r\n- `pre_history` is written once into the system prompt. Use it only for values that never change during the call.\r\n- `post_history` is rendered again on every turn after the chat history and is not stored in the history. Use it for values that change.\r\n- The block is rendered as `<CONTEXT_VARIABLES>`, one line `- name (description): value` per populated item.\r\n- No other keys are allowed on an item. There is no `value`, `type` or `default`.\r\n- It does not affect extraction or tool parameter substitution.\r\n\r\n## Transitions\r\n\r\nA transition is an object in a node's `transitions` array.\r\n\r\n| Field | Type | Meaning |\r\n|---|---|---|\r\n| `target_node` | string | Key of the next node. May be the node itself. `__previous__` is allowed in global nodes |\r\n| `description` | string | What the transition is for. The routing model reads it |\r\n| `condition` | object | When the transition fires |\r\n| `is_tool_call` | boolean | The dialogue model triggers the transition by calling a tool. See \"Tool-call transitions\" |\r\n\r\n### Condition operators\r\n\r\nDeterministic operators check a variable in code. No model call.\r\n\r\n| Operator | Needs | True when |\r\n|---|---|---|\r\n| `equals` | `variable`, `operand` | `str(value) == str(operand)` |\r\n| `not_equals` | `variable`, `operand` | The inverse of `equals` |\r\n| `greater_than` | `variable`, `operand` | `float(value) > float(operand)` |\r\n| `less_than` | `variable`, `operand` | `float(value) < float(operand)` |\r\n| `contains` | `variable`, `operand` | `operand` is a substring of the value, ignoring case |\r\n| `is_set` | `variable` | The variable has any value |\r\n| `is_not_set` | `variable` | The variable has no value |\r\n| `always` | nothing | Always |\r\n\r\nThe semantic operator uses the routing model.\r\n\r\n| Operator | Needs | True when |\r\n|---|---|---|\r\n| `matches_intent` | `description` | The routing model judges that the user's last message matches the description |\r\n\r\nAll `matches_intent` conditions of one evaluation are judged in a single model call.\r\n\r\n### Condition shapes\r\n\r\nAn unconditional transition:\r\n\r\n```json\r\n{ \"target_node\": \"next_step\", \"description\": \"always\", \"condition\": { \"operator\": \"always\" } }\r\n```\r\n\r\nAn intent condition:\r\n\r\n```json\r\n{\r\n  \"target_node\": \"complaint\",\r\n  \"description\": \"The user wants to complain\",\r\n  \"condition\": { \"operator\": \"matches_intent\", \"description\": \"The user is unhappy or wants to file a complaint\" }\r\n}\r\n```\r\n\r\nVariable conditions go inside a group. The top-level group always uses `\"logic\": \"or\"`:\r\n\r\n```json\r\n{\r\n  \"target_node\": \"order_found\",\r\n  \"description\": \"The lookup returned an order\",\r\n  \"condition\": {\r\n    \"logic\": \"or\",\r\n    \"conditions\": [\r\n      { \"variable\": \"order_status\", \"operator\": \"equals\", \"operand\": \"shipped\" },\r\n      { \"variable\": \"order_status\", \"operator\": \"equals\", \"operand\": \"delivered\" }\r\n    ]\r\n  }\r\n}\r\n```\r\n\r\nFor AND, nest a group inside the top-level OR. Never put `\"logic\": \"and\"` at the top level.\r\n\r\n```json\r\n{\r\n  \"target_node\": \"priority_desk\",\r\n  \"description\": \"Gold customer with a late order\",\r\n  \"condition\": {\r\n    \"logic\": \"or\",\r\n    \"conditions\": [\r\n      {\r\n        \"logic\": \"and\",\r\n        \"conditions\": [\r\n          { \"variable\": \"customer_tier\", \"operator\": \"equals\", \"operand\": \"gold\" },\r\n          { \"variable\": \"days_late\", \"operator\": \"greater_than\", \"operand\": 3 }\r\n        ]\r\n      }\r\n    ]\r\n  }\r\n}\r\n```\r\n\r\nThe working builder uses the bare form for `always` and the grouped form for every variable check. The internal reference also shows a bare single variable condition (`{\"variable\": ..., \"operator\": ..., \"operand\": ...}`) directly under `condition`.\r\n\r\n### Do not route on a boolean\r\n\r\n`equals` compares strings. A boolean can arrive as `\"True\"` or as `\"true\"`, depending on how it was written into the table. A mismatch fails silently: the branch never fires and nothing reports an error.\r\n\r\n- Prefer a string value such as `verified` or `rejected`, written by your code or by the API.\r\n- If you must test a boolean that comes from an API response, test both spellings. Verified in a working builder:\r\n\r\n```json\r\n{\r\n  \"target_node\": \"wrap_up\",\r\n  \"description\": \"The booking call succeeded\",\r\n  \"condition\": {\r\n    \"logic\": \"or\",\r\n    \"conditions\": [\r\n      { \"variable\": \"booking_ok\", \"operator\": \"equals\", \"operand\": \"true\" },\r\n      { \"variable\": \"booking_ok\", \"operator\": \"equals\", \"operand\": \"True\" }\r\n    ]\r\n  }\r\n}\r\n```\r\n\r\n`greater_than` and `less_than` convert both sides to numbers, so `2` and `\"2\"` behave the same. Use them for counters.\r\n\r\n### Order of evaluation\r\n\r\nAfter each user message, routing runs in this order. The first match wins and routing stops.\r\n\r\n```text\r\n1. loop_condition of the current node    match: stay in the node, use loop_prompt\r\n2. the node's transitions, in list order match: go to target_node\r\n3. global nodes                          match: enter the global node\r\n4. nothing matched                       stay in the node, wait for the next user message\r\n```\r\n\r\nWithin step 2:\r\n\r\n- Transitions are checked in list order. Put specific conditions first and broad ones last.\r\n- Deterministic conditions are evaluated first. If one matches, no routing model call is made.\r\n- If several transitions of a node have the same `target_node`, the engine merges them at load time into one transition. Its condition is the OR of the originals. If one of them is unconditional, the merged transition is unconditional.\r\n\r\nRouting also runs after a tool result arrives. Verified in a working builder: a transition on a variable that a tool's `response_mapping` sets fires in the same turn, without a further user message.\r\n\r\nWhen nothing matches, there is no fallback. The agent stays and answers from the same `task_message`. Make sure every realistic path has a matching transition.\r\n\r\n### `routing_prompt`\r\n\r\nA node-level string. It replaces the default intent-matching instruction of the routing model for this node. Use it when the transition descriptions cannot carry the rule, for example \"stay here until both the name and the date are given\".\r\n\r\n### Self-transition\r\n\r\nA last transition with `always` that targets the node itself keeps the user in the node until an earlier transition matches.\r\n\r\n```json\r\n{ \"target_node\": \"collect_details\", \"description\": \"Keep collecting\", \"condition\": { \"operator\": \"always\" } }\r\n```\r\n\r\nPrefer this to `loop_condition` in most cases. All routing then lives in one ordered list.\r\n\r\n### Loop conditions\r\n\r\n`loop_condition` is an intent description. The routing model checks it before all transitions. When it matches, the agent stays in the node.\r\n\r\n`loop_prompt` replaces `task_message` in the system message from the second turn in the node. The model sees one of the two, never both. Write it so that it stands alone. `{{var}}` works in it, so it can show what was already collected.\r\n\r\n```json\r\n{\r\n  \"name\": \"collect_contact\",\r\n  \"node_type\": \"conversation\",\r\n  \"task_message\": \"Ask for the caller's full name and email address.\",\r\n  \"loop_condition\": \"The user has not yet given both a full name and an email address\",\r\n  \"loop_prompt\": \"Name so far: {{customer_name}}. Email so far: {{email}}. Ask only for what is still missing. When both are present, read them back once.\",\r\n  \"transitions\": []\r\n}\r\n```\r\n\r\nMake the loop condition specific. \"The user has not finished\" matches everything and the agent never leaves.\r\n\r\n### Tool-call transitions\r\n\r\nSet `is_tool_call` to `true` on a transition. The dialogue model then sees the transition as a tool named `transition_to_<node>` and triggers it by calling that tool. The router does not evaluate it after each turn.\r\n\r\n```json\r\n{\r\n  \"target_node\": \"confirm_booking\",\r\n  \"description\": \"The caller accepted the offered slot\",\r\n  \"is_tool_call\": true,\r\n  \"condition\": { \"operator\": \"matches_intent\", \"description\": \"The caller accepted the offered slot\" }\r\n}\r\n```\r\n\r\nUse it when the agent should decide to move, for example because of something it just said or computed. The agent can acknowledge and move in the same message. The target node's `on_enter` then asks the next question. Global nodes accept the same flag.\r\n\r\n### Passthrough nodes\r\n\r\nA node with `\"passthrough\": true` routes right after its entry actions. It does not wait for the user. `api_request` and `code` nodes are normally passthrough nodes.\r\n\r\n- Set `passthrough_default_transition` to the next node.\r\n- End `transitions` with an `always` transition. A passthrough node with no matching transition deadlocks.\r\n- Set `on_enter` to `{\"type\": \"silent\"}`, or to a short fixed line that covers the wait.\r\n\r\n```json\r\n{\r\n  \"name\": \"prepare\",\r\n  \"node_type\": \"code\",\r\n  \"passthrough\": true,\r\n  \"passthrough_default_transition\": \"greeting\",\r\n  \"on_enter\": { \"type\": \"silent\" },\r\n  \"pre_actions\": [],\r\n  \"transitions\": [\r\n    { \"target_node\": \"greeting\", \"description\": \"always\", \"condition\": { \"operator\": \"always\" } }\r\n  ]\r\n}\r\n```\r\n\r\nVerified in a working builder: a passthrough `code` node can be the `initial_node`. It runs before the agent says anything.\r\n\r\n### Global nodes\r\n\r\nA global node is an interrupt handler. It can fire from any node. It is checked only after the current node's own transitions found no match.\r\n\r\nA global node has the same fields as a normal node, plus:\r\n\r\n| Field | Meaning |\r\n|---|---|\r\n| `condition` | When the global node activates. Same format as a transition condition |\r\n| `description` | Purpose of the node |\r\n| `excluded_nodes` | Array of node keys where it must not fire |\r\n| `is_tool_call` | Offer the global node to the dialogue model as a tool, not as a per-turn check |\r\n\r\n`__previous__` as a `target_node` or as `passthrough_default_transition` returns to the node that was interrupted.\r\n\r\n```json\r\n{\r\n  \"global_nodes\": {\r\n    \"off_topic\": {\r\n      \"name\": \"off_topic\",\r\n      \"description\": \"Brings the caller back to the subject\",\r\n      \"condition\": { \"operator\": \"matches_intent\", \"description\": \"The user talks about something unrelated to their order\" },\r\n      \"task_message\": \"Redirect the caller to the subject.\",\r\n      \"on_enter\": { \"type\": \"tts\", \"text\": \"I can only help with your order. Let us continue.\" },\r\n      \"passthrough\": true,\r\n      \"passthrough_default_transition\": \"__previous__\",\r\n      \"excluded_nodes\": [\"goodbye\"],\r\n      \"transitions\": [\r\n        { \"target_node\": \"__previous__\", \"description\": \"always\", \"condition\": { \"operator\": \"always\" } }\r\n      ]\r\n    }\r\n  }\r\n}\r\n```\r\n\r\nRules:\r\n\r\n- List terminal nodes in `excluded_nodes`, or the global node can loop.\r\n- Exclude nodes where an interrupt would do harm, for example an identity check.\r\n- For a plain redirect use a passthrough node with a fixed line. It is faster and more predictable than a model reply.\r\n- Use global nodes for interrupts that can happen anywhere. Do not copy the same transition into every node.\r\n\r\n## Extraction schema\r\n\r\n`extraction_schema` is an array on a node. After each user message the extraction model fills the listed variables from the conversation.\r\n\r\n```json\r\n{\r\n  \"extraction_schema\": [\r\n    {\r\n      \"name\": \"order_number\",\r\n      \"type\": \"string\",\r\n      \"description\": \"The order number the caller reads out\",\r\n      \"validation_pattern\": \"^[0-9]{8}$\",\r\n      \"max_retries\": 2,\r\n      \"min_confidence\": 0.8\r\n    },\r\n    {\r\n      \"name\": \"request_type\",\r\n      \"type\": \"string\",\r\n      \"enum_values\": [\"status\", \"cancel\", \"change_address\", \"other\"],\r\n      \"description\": \"What the caller wants to do with the order\",\r\n      \"min_confidence\": 0.7\r\n    }\r\n  ]\r\n}\r\n```\r\n\r\n| Field | Type | Meaning |\r\n|---|---|---|\r\n| `name` | string | Variable name |\r\n| `type` | string | `string`, `integer`, `float`, `boolean`, `date`, `datetime`, `name`, `phone_number` |\r\n| `description` | string | What the variable is |\r\n| `enum_values` | array | Allowed values. Works on every type. The old `enum` type is deprecated and becomes `string` |\r\n| `extraction_prompt` | string | A custom extraction template. See the warning below |\r\n| `validation_pattern` | string | Regular expression the value must match |\r\n| `min_confidence` | number | 0.0 to 1.0. Values below it are discarded. Default `0.95` |\r\n| `max_retries` | integer | Retries after a failed validation, with the error given to the model |\r\n\r\nHow it works:\r\n\r\n1. The extraction model sees recent messages and the schema.\r\n2. It returns structured values.\r\n3. Confidence is computed from the model's token probabilities, not from a self-report.\r\n4. Each value is checked against `validation_pattern` and `enum_values`.\r\n5. On failure the model retries, up to `max_retries`.\r\n\r\nRules:\r\n\r\n- **Set `min_confidence` on every variable.** The default of 0.95 is strict. A discarded value leaves no trace in the transcript, so it looks the same as \"nothing was extracted\".\r\n- **`extraction_prompt` replaces the whole built-in template.** It is not appended. A custom prompt must contain the input placeholders itself: `{{extractor.speech}}`, `{{extractor.conversation.last-10}}`, `{{extractor.type_preamble}}` and `{{extractor.variables_list}}`. Without them the extractor never sees the user's message and returns nothing on every turn, while dialogue and routing look healthy.\r\n- **Declare dates as `type: \"date\"`.** The date types give the extractor today's date and the rules for relative dates such as \"tomorrow\". A date typed as `string` gets no date anchor and the model guesses the year.\r\n- The built-in date rules turn vague expressions such as \"next week\" into a concrete date. If you need vague dates rejected, a custom prompt must say so and state that this overrides the general rules.\r\n- No `null`. Omit optional fields or use `\"\"`.\r\n- An extracted variable is available to the same node's transitions in the same turn, and to every later node.\r\n\r\nInside `extraction_prompt` these date placeholders resolve: `{{date.today}}`, `{{time.now}}`, `{{date.day_of_week}}`, `{{date.yesterday}}`, `{{date.tomorrow}}`, `{{date.day_after_tomorrow}}`, `{{date.next_week}}`, `{{date.end_of_month}}`, `{{date.first_next_month}}`, `{{date.next_mon}}` to `{{date.next_sun}}`, `{{date.last_mon}}` to `{{date.last_sun}}`, `{{date.fnm_mon}}` to `{{date.fnm_sun}}` (first such weekday of next month), `{{date.weeks2}}`, `{{date.weeks3}}`, `{{date.weeks2_ago}}`, `{{date.weeks3_ago}}`. Do not rely on them outside extraction prompts.\r\n\r\n**Fork on an extracted enum.** To route from one node to several paths, extract an enum and branch with `equals`. This is more reliable than several competing `matches_intent` transitions.\r\n\r\n```json\r\n{\r\n  \"name\": \"menu\",\r\n  \"node_type\": \"conversation\",\r\n  \"task_message\": \"Tell the caller the order status in one sentence. Then ask whether they want to change the delivery address, cancel the order, or end the call.\",\r\n  \"extraction_schema\": [\r\n    {\r\n      \"name\": \"next_action\",\r\n      \"type\": \"string\",\r\n      \"enum_values\": [\"change_address\", \"cancel\", \"done\"],\r\n      \"description\": \"What the caller chose. Empty if unclear.\",\r\n      \"min_confidence\": 0.7\r\n    }\r\n  ],\r\n  \"transitions\": [\r\n    { \"target_node\": \"change_address\", \"description\": \"Change address\", \"condition\": { \"logic\": \"or\", \"conditions\": [ { \"variable\": \"next_action\", \"operator\": \"equals\", \"operand\": \"change_address\" } ] } },\r\n    { \"target_node\": \"cancel_order\", \"description\": \"Cancel\", \"condition\": { \"logic\": \"or\", \"conditions\": [ { \"variable\": \"next_action\", \"operator\": \"equals\", \"operand\": \"cancel\" } ] } },\r\n    { \"target_node\": \"goodbye\", \"description\": \"Done\", \"condition\": { \"logic\": \"or\", \"conditions\": [ { \"variable\": \"next_action\", \"operator\": \"equals\", \"operand\": \"done\" } ] } },\r\n    { \"target_node\": \"menu\", \"description\": \"Unclear, stay\", \"condition\": { \"operator\": \"always\" } }\r\n  ]\r\n}\r\n```\r\n\r\nUse extraction for user speech. For API responses use `response_mapping` (tools-and-code.md). It is deterministic and needs no model call.\r\n\r\n## End-call nodes\r\n\r\nAn `end_call` node hangs up. It has a `terminal` object and no outgoing transitions.\r\n\r\n```json\r\n{\r\n  \"name\": \"goodbye\",\r\n  \"node_type\": \"end_call\",\r\n  \"on_enter\": { \"type\": \"silent\" },\r\n  \"disable_inactivity_monitor\": true,\r\n  \"transitions\": [],\r\n  \"terminal\": {\r\n    \"type\": \"function\",\r\n    \"function\": {\r\n      \"name\": \"closing_end\",\r\n      \"handler\": \"__function__:end_call\",\r\n      \"parameters\": {\r\n        \"type\": \"object\",\r\n        \"properties\": {\r\n          \"message_mode\": { \"type\": \"string\", \"default\": \"custom\" },\r\n          \"spoken_message\": { \"type\": \"string\", \"default\": \"Thank you for calling. Goodbye.\" }\r\n        }\r\n      }\r\n    }\r\n  }\r\n}\r\n```\r\n\r\n| Parameter | Meaning |\r\n|---|---|\r\n| `message_mode` | `custom`: say `spoken_message`. `llm`: the model writes the farewell from `llm_prompt`. `none`: hang up without a farewell |\r\n| `spoken_message` | The fixed farewell for `custom`. `{{var}}` works |\r\n| `llm_prompt` | The instruction for `llm` |\r\n| `interruptible` | `true` opens a short window after the farewell in which the user can still speak. Default: hang up as soon as the agent stops speaking |\r\n| `trigger_timeout` | Length of that window in seconds |\r\n| `trigger_pattern_both` | Farewell phrases, separated by `\\|`, that do not cancel the close. Anything else the user says in the window cancels the close and returns the turn to the agent |\r\n\r\nRules:\r\n\r\n- **The farewell comes from the terminal, not from `on_enter`.** A node with an `on_enter` line and `message_mode: \"none\"` hangs up silently. Put the sentence in `spoken_message` and set `on_enter` to `{\"type\": \"silent\"}`.\r\n- Set `disable_inactivity_monitor` to `true` and `transitions` to `[]`.\r\n- **The call's `endedReason` is the terminal's `function.name`.** The node name does not affect it. Pick a name that describes the outcome.\r\n- Use several end-call nodes with different farewells and function names when outcomes differ.\r\n- If you set `interruptible`, also set `trigger_pattern_both`. Without a phrase list every polite reply cancels the hang-up. The window is timed on a wall clock and a chat simulation cannot measure it.\r\n\r\nVerified in a working builder: `message_mode: \"none\"` with a silent `on_enter` ends the call when the node is reached. The agent says its goodbye in the previous conversation node.\r\n\r\n## Limits\r\n\r\n| Limit | Default | Notes |\r\n|---|---|---|\r\n| `max_node_turns` | `10` | Cap on user turns in one node. Only user messages count. Tool results and agent messages do not. There is no \"unlimited\" value |\r\n| `max_turn_transition` | none | Where to go when the cap is reached. Without it the engine records the overrun and continues with normal routing, so the cap changes nothing |\r\n| `truncation_strategy` | `keep_all` | `keep_all`: full history. `keep_recent`: system message plus the last `truncation_window_size` messages. `summarize`: older messages are replaced by a model-written summary |\r\n| `truncation_window_size` | `10` | Used by `keep_recent` and `summarize` |\r\n| Extraction `min_confidence` | `0.95` | See \"Extraction schema\" |\r\n| Code `timeout_ms` | `30000` | See tools-and-code.md |\r\n\r\nNotes:\r\n\r\n- Truncation applies to the dialogue model only. Extraction and routing run before it.\r\n- Give every node that sets `max_node_turns` a `max_turn_transition`. The working builder sets `max_node_turns` to 60 and 80 on its conversation nodes without a `max_turn_transition`. Its calls ended well below those counts, so the behaviour at the cap is not confirmed by it.\r\n- Size the cap from real conversations. A node that handles a whole call can need dozens of user turns.\r\n- The maximum call length in a simulation is set with `maxCallDurationSeconds` (api.md).\r\n\r\n## Design rules\r\n\r\n- Use `snake_case` node keys that describe the step.\r\n- Keep dialogue in `task_message`, routing in `transitions`, data capture in `extraction_schema`.\r\n- Every path must reach an `end_call` node.\r\n- Put deterministic conditions before `matches_intent` conditions.\r\n- Every passthrough node ends with an `always` transition.\r\n- A transition back to an earlier node is supported.\r\n- Split a step into its own node when it has strict rules of its own or forms a clear state. Keep related optional actions together in one conversation node with several tools.\r\n\r\n## Complete example\r\n\r\nA clinic assistant. It collects a service and a day, looks up free slots, lets the model book one with a tool, and ends the call.\r\n\r\n```json\r\n{\r\n  \"initial_node\": \"collect\",\r\n  \"system_prompt\": \"You are the phone assistant of a dental clinic. Speak English. Keep every turn to one or two short sentences.\",\r\n  \"test_variables\": { \"patient_id\": \"p_test\", \"first_name\": \"Dana\" },\r\n  \"global_nodes\": {},\r\n  \"nodes\": {\r\n    \"collect\": {\r\n      \"name\": \"collect\",\r\n      \"node_type\": \"conversation\",\r\n      \"on_enter\": { \"type\": \"tts\", \"text\": \"Hello {{first_name}}, this is the clinic. Which treatment would you like to book, and on which day?\" },\r\n      \"on_enter_first_entry_only\": true,\r\n      \"task_message\": \"Find out which treatment the caller wants (checkup, cleaning or filling) and on which day. Ask one question at a time. Do not call a tool in this turn.\",\r\n      \"extraction_schema\": [\r\n        { \"name\": \"treatment\", \"type\": \"string\", \"enum_values\": [\"checkup\", \"cleaning\", \"filling\"], \"description\": \"Treatment the caller wants\", \"min_confidence\": 0.7 },\r\n        { \"name\": \"wanted_day\", \"type\": \"date\", \"description\": \"Day the caller wants the appointment\", \"min_confidence\": 0.7 }\r\n      ],\r\n      \"max_node_turns\": 12,\r\n      \"max_turn_transition\": \"goodbye_no_booking\",\r\n      \"transitions\": [\r\n        {\r\n          \"target_node\": \"find_slots\",\r\n          \"description\": \"Treatment and day are known\",\r\n          \"condition\": {\r\n            \"logic\": \"or\",\r\n            \"conditions\": [\r\n              {\r\n                \"logic\": \"and\",\r\n                \"conditions\": [\r\n                  { \"variable\": \"treatment\", \"operator\": \"is_set\" },\r\n                  { \"variable\": \"wanted_day\", \"operator\": \"is_set\" }\r\n                ]\r\n              }\r\n            ]\r\n          }\r\n        },\r\n        { \"target_node\": \"collect\", \"description\": \"Keep collecting\", \"condition\": { \"operator\": \"always\" } }\r\n      ]\r\n    },\r\n    \"find_slots\": {\r\n      \"name\": \"find_slots\",\r\n      \"node_type\": \"api_request\",\r\n      \"passthrough\": true,\r\n      \"passthrough_default_transition\": \"offer\",\r\n      \"on_enter\": { \"type\": \"silent\" },\r\n      \"disable_inactivity_monitor\": true,\r\n      \"pre_actions\": [\r\n        {\r\n          \"type\": \"function\",\r\n          \"config\": { \"timeoutMs\": 10000, \"maxRetries\": 2 },\r\n          \"function\": {\r\n            \"name\": \"find_slots\",\r\n            \"handler\": \"__function__:api_call\",\r\n            \"description\": \"Get free slots for a day.\",\r\n            \"parameters\": {\r\n              \"type\": \"object\",\r\n              \"properties\": {\r\n                \"url\": { \"type\": \"string\", \"default\": \"https://api.example.com/v1/slots\" },\r\n                \"method\": { \"type\": \"string\", \"default\": \"POST\" },\r\n                \"headers\": { \"type\": \"object\", \"properties\": { \"Content-Type\": { \"type\": \"string\", \"default\": \"application/json\" } } },\r\n                \"body\": {\r\n                  \"type\": \"object\",\r\n                  \"properties\": {\r\n                    \"treatment\": { \"type\": \"string\", \"default\": \"{{treatment}}\" },\r\n                    \"day\": { \"type\": \"string\", \"default\": \"{{wanted_day}}\" }\r\n                  }\r\n                }\r\n              }\r\n            }\r\n          }\r\n        }\r\n      ],\r\n      \"response_mapping\": [\r\n        { \"path\": \"data.slots_text\", \"variable\": \"slots_text\" }\r\n      ],\r\n      \"transitions\": [\r\n        { \"target_node\": \"goodbye_no_booking\", \"description\": \"The lookup failed\", \"condition\": { \"logic\": \"or\", \"conditions\": [ { \"variable\": \"find_slots_failure_count\", \"operator\": \"greater_than\", \"operand\": 0 } ] } },\r\n        { \"target_node\": \"offer\", \"description\": \"always\", \"condition\": { \"operator\": \"always\" } }\r\n      ]\r\n    },\r\n    \"offer\": {\r\n      \"name\": \"offer\",\r\n      \"node_type\": \"conversation\",\r\n      \"task_message\": \"Free slots: {{slots_text}}\\n\\nOffer the earliest slot. If the caller refuses it, offer the next one. When the caller accepts a slot, read back the treatment, the day and the time and wait for a clear yes. Then CALL book_appointment. Saying that it is booked is not enough. After the tool result, tell the caller whether the booking worked and say goodbye.\",\r\n      \"tools\": [\r\n        {\r\n          \"type\": \"function\",\r\n          \"config\": { \"timeoutMs\": 10000, \"maxRetries\": 0 },\r\n          \"function\": {\r\n            \"name\": \"book_appointment\",\r\n            \"handler\": \"__function__:api_call\",\r\n            \"description\": \"Book one slot. CALL it only after the caller confirmed the read-back.\",\r\n            \"parameters\": {\r\n              \"type\": \"object\",\r\n              \"properties\": {\r\n                \"url\": { \"type\": \"string\", \"default\": \"https://api.example.com/v1/bookings\" },\r\n                \"method\": { \"type\": \"string\", \"default\": \"POST\" },\r\n                \"headers\": { \"type\": \"object\", \"properties\": { \"Content-Type\": { \"type\": \"string\", \"default\": \"application/json\" } } },\r\n                \"body\": {\r\n                  \"type\": \"object\",\r\n                  \"properties\": {\r\n                    \"patient_id\": { \"type\": \"string\", \"default\": \"{{patient_id}}\" },\r\n                    \"treatment\": { \"type\": \"string\", \"default\": \"{{treatment}}\" },\r\n                    \"slot_time\": { \"type\": \"string\", \"description\": \"Start time of the accepted slot, HH:MM, 24 hour\", \"default\": \"{{slot_time}}\" }\r\n                  },\r\n                  \"required\": [\"slot_time\"]\r\n                }\r\n              }\r\n            }\r\n          },\r\n          \"response_mapping\": [\r\n            { \"path\": \"data.booking_id\", \"variable\": \"booking_id\" }\r\n          ]\r\n        }\r\n      ],\r\n      \"max_node_turns\": 20,\r\n      \"max_turn_transition\": \"goodbye_no_booking\",\r\n      \"transitions\": [\r\n        { \"target_node\": \"goodbye_booked\", \"description\": \"The booking was recorded\", \"condition\": { \"logic\": \"or\", \"conditions\": [ { \"variable\": \"booking_id\", \"operator\": \"is_set\" } ] } },\r\n        { \"target_node\": \"goodbye_no_booking\", \"description\": \"The caller does not want any slot\", \"condition\": { \"operator\": \"matches_intent\", \"description\": \"The caller refused all offered slots or does not want to book\" } }\r\n      ]\r\n    },\r\n    \"goodbye_booked\": {\r\n      \"name\": \"goodbye_booked\",\r\n      \"node_type\": \"end_call\",\r\n      \"on_enter\": { \"type\": \"silent\" },\r\n      \"disable_inactivity_monitor\": true,\r\n      \"transitions\": [],\r\n      \"terminal\": {\r\n        \"type\": \"function\",\r\n        \"function\": {\r\n          \"name\": \"booked\",\r\n          \"handler\": \"__function__:end_call\",\r\n          \"parameters\": { \"type\": \"object\", \"properties\": { \"message_mode\": { \"type\": \"string\", \"default\": \"none\" } } }\r\n        }\r\n      }\r\n    },\r\n    \"goodbye_no_booking\": {\r\n      \"name\": \"goodbye_no_booking\",\r\n      \"node_type\": \"end_call\",\r\n      \"on_enter\": { \"type\": \"silent\" },\r\n      \"disable_inactivity_monitor\": true,\r\n      \"transitions\": [],\r\n      \"terminal\": {\r\n        \"type\": \"function\",\r\n        \"function\": {\r\n          \"name\": \"no_booking\",\r\n          \"handler\": \"__function__:end_call\",\r\n          \"parameters\": {\r\n            \"type\": \"object\",\r\n            \"properties\": {\r\n              \"message_mode\": { \"type\": \"string\", \"default\": \"custom\" },\r\n              \"spoken_message\": { \"type\": \"string\", \"default\": \"No appointment was booked. You can call us again at any time. Goodbye.\" }\r\n            }\r\n          }\r\n        }\r\n      }\r\n    }\r\n  }\r\n}\r\n```\r\n\r\nWhat the example shows:\r\n\r\n| Node | Feature |\r\n|---|---|\r\n| `collect` | Fixed opening line, extraction with a `date` type, AND group, self-transition, turn cap with a target |\r\n| `find_slots` | Passthrough `api_request` node, baked parameters, node-level `response_mapping`, failure counter, `always` fallback |\r\n| `offer` | A tool the model calls, a model-filled body field, tool-level `response_mapping`, routing on the mapped variable |\r\n| `goodbye_booked`, `goodbye_no_booking` | Two end-call nodes with different `endedReason` values |\r\n# Tools and code\r\n\r\nThis page describes how a workflow calls HTTP APIs and runs Python. Read [workflow-format.md](workflow-format.md) first.\r\n\r\nStatements marked \"verified in a working builder\" come from a builder that created a 6-node workflow through the REST API. The workflow has code nodes, `api_call` tools, post-hooks and transitions on tool results. Its agent passed its practice cases. Statements marked \"seen in a trial call\" come from the event log of a simulation call made with that builder.\r\n\r\n## Tool function shape\r\n\r\nEvery external action is a **tool function**. The shape is the same wherever you attach it.\r\n\r\n```json\r\n{\r\n  \"type\": \"function\",\r\n  \"config\": { \"timeoutMs\": 10000, \"maxRetries\": 2 },\r\n  \"function\": {\r\n    \"name\": \"lookup_order\",\r\n    \"handler\": \"__function__:api_call\",\r\n    \"description\": \"Look up one order. CALL it when the caller asks about a specific order.\",\r\n    \"parameters\": {\r\n      \"type\": \"object\",\r\n      \"properties\": {\r\n        \"url\": { \"type\": \"string\", \"default\": \"https://api.example.com/v1/orders/lookup\" },\r\n        \"method\": { \"type\": \"string\", \"default\": \"POST\" },\r\n        \"headers\": {\r\n          \"type\": \"object\",\r\n          \"properties\": { \"Content-Type\": { \"type\": \"string\", \"default\": \"application/json\" } }\r\n        },\r\n        \"body\": {\r\n          \"type\": \"object\",\r\n          \"properties\": {\r\n            \"customer_id\": { \"type\": \"string\", \"default\": \"{{customer_id}}\" },\r\n            \"order_number\": { \"type\": \"string\", \"description\": \"Order number, digits only\", \"default\": \"{{order_number}}\" }\r\n          },\r\n          \"required\": [\"order_number\"]\r\n        }\r\n      }\r\n    }\r\n  },\r\n  \"response_mapping\": [\r\n    { \"path\": \"data.status\", \"variable\": \"order_status\" }\r\n  ]\r\n}\r\n```\r\n\r\n| Field | Meaning |\r\n|---|---|\r\n| `type` | Always `\"function\"` |\r\n| `function.name` | Tool name. The model calls it by this name. Result variables are named after it |\r\n| `function.handler` | Which runtime executes the tool. See the table below |\r\n| `function.description` | Read by the dialogue model when the tool is in `tools`. Ignored in `pre_actions` |\r\n| `function.parameters` | JSON-schema object. `properties` holds the parameters |\r\n| `config.timeoutMs` | Timeout of one attempt, in milliseconds |\r\n| `config.maxRetries` | Tool-level retries of a failed call |\r\n| `config.interruptible` | `false` makes the tool run in the background. See \"Background tools\" |\r\n| `config.alwaysRunsAt` | Makes the tool a lifecycle tool. See \"Lifecycle tools\" |\r\n| `response_mapping` | Tool level, for tools in `tools`. Maps the result to variables |\r\n| `pre_process_code` | Python hook that runs before the call |\r\n| `post_process_code` | Python hook that runs after the call |\r\n\r\n| Handler | Runs |\r\n|---|---|\r\n| `__function__:api_call` | An HTTP request |\r\n| `__function__:code_exec` | A Python snippet in a sandbox |\r\n| `__function__:end_call` | Hangs up. Used in the `terminal` of an `end_call` node |\r\n\r\nHandlers for integrations, knowledge search, call transfer and rescheduling also exist. They are not needed here.\r\n\r\n`api_call` parameters:\r\n\r\n| Parameter | Meaning |\r\n|---|---|\r\n| `url` | Request URL. `{{var}}` works |\r\n| `method` | HTTP method |\r\n| `headers` | Object. Each header is a property with a `default` |\r\n| `body` | Object. Each body field is a property under `body.properties` |\r\n| `connect_retries` | Transport retries. See \"Retries and timeouts\" |\r\n\r\n## How a parameter gets its value\r\n\r\nOne key decides, per parameter: `default`.\r\n\r\n| Declaration | Who supplies the value | Does the model see the parameter |\r\n|---|---|---|\r\n| `default` with a literal, or with a `{{var}}` that has a value | The workflow. The value is **baked** | No |\r\n| `default` with a `{{var}}` that has no value when the node is entered | The model | Yes, named after the variable, with the property's `type`, `description` and `enum` |\r\n| no `default` | The model | Yes |\r\n| `const` | Nobody. `const` does **not** bake | It only constrains the model's own output |\r\n\r\nRules:\r\n\r\n- **Use `default` for fixed backend config** such as `url`, `method` and auth headers. At registration the engine moves every `default` into the tool's base config, resolves `{{var}}` from the variable table, and removes the field from the schema the model sees.\r\n- **Never use `const` for a value you want sent.** The engine reads only `default`. A `{{var}}` inside a `const` never resolves. An `api_call` whose `url` is a `const` fails with \"URL is required\".\r\n- **Baking happens when the node is entered.** A `{{var}}` default is resolved against the variable table at that moment.\r\n\r\nThe working builder uses this for every tool: `customer_id` has `\"default\": \"{{customer_id}}\"` and is baked from a call variable. Every other body field has `\"default\": \"{{<field name>}}\"`, where no variable of that name exists, so the model fills it. Verified in a working builder.\r\n\r\nSeen in a trial call: the model's arguments are stored as `<tool>_args.<field>` in the variable table, for example `lookup_order_args.order_number`. They were not stored under the bare field name.\r\n\r\nTake care that a model-filled field does not share its name with a variable that is already set when the node is entered. The value would be baked and the model would never see the parameter.\r\n\r\n### `api_call` body field rules\r\n\r\n- Declare each body field as a leaf under `body.properties.<field>`.\r\n- Put the field's `type`, `description` and `enum` on that leaf. Put required fields in `body.required`.\r\n- Give each leaf its own `default`.\r\n- Do not put a field spec at the top level of `parameters.properties` next to a `body.default` template object. The engine looks for the spec at `body.properties.<field>`, does not find it, and the model sees only \"Template variable: name\". The `enum` and `description` are lost while the call still works.\r\n- A body that exists only as one `body.default` object also shows as an empty form in the dashboard editor. Saving from there removes the tool.\r\n\r\nA `code_exec` tool is different: its parameters are true top-level arguments. Declare a model-filled one as a top-level property without `default`.\r\n\r\n### When a baked variable has no value\r\n\r\n| Field type | Variable unset | Variable is `\"\"` |\r\n|---|---|---|\r\n| Normal field in an engine-run call (`pre_actions`, lifecycle tool) | The field is dropped from the request. No error | The field is sent empty |\r\n| `code` of `code_exec` | The literal text `{{var}}` stays in the Python source and the snippet crashes | |\r\n\r\nFix: make sure an earlier node always sets the variable, even to a sentinel value. In code, read variables from `vars` and do not splice them in with `{{var}}`.\r\n\r\n### Constrain model-filled parameters\r\n\r\nWhen a value must be one of a known set, do not use a free string. The model sometimes invents a value that looks plausible.\r\n\r\n- Use an `enum` on the leaf, and list the values in the `description` as well.\r\n- Or use a `number` that is an index into a list, with the valid range in the description. A pre-hook or the backend turns the index into the real value.\r\n- If neither is possible, name the exact source in the description and tell the model to copy the value unchanged.\r\n\r\n```json\r\n{\r\n  \"type\": \"object\",\r\n  \"properties\": {\r\n    \"queue\": {\r\n      \"type\": \"string\",\r\n      \"description\": \"Team that takes over. One of: billing, delivery, returns.\",\r\n      \"enum\": [\"billing\", \"delivery\", \"returns\"],\r\n      \"default\": \"{{queue}}\"\r\n    }\r\n  },\r\n  \"required\": [\"queue\"]\r\n}\r\n```\r\n\r\n## `pre_actions` versus `tools`\r\n\r\nThe placement decides who fires the call.\r\n\r\n| Placement | Node type | Who calls it | When |\r\n|---|---|---|---|\r\n| `pre_actions` | `api_request`, `code` | The engine | On node entry, before any model turn |\r\n| `tools` | `conversation` | The dialogue model | During a turn, when the model decides to |\r\n| Workflow-level `tools` with `config.alwaysRunsAt` | none | The engine | At call start or call end |\r\n\r\nThe platform removes `tools` from non-conversation nodes and `pre_actions` from conversation nodes. It does so without an error: the JSON you sent looks fine, the create call succeeds, and the stored node has an empty array. A node cannot use both.\r\n\r\n**Use `pre_actions`** when the call must always run and the model has nothing to decide. Example: after the order number is known, always fetch the order.\r\n\r\n**Use `tools`** when the call depends on what the user says. Example: cancel the order only if the caller asks for it.\r\n\r\nDifferences in behaviour:\r\n\r\n| | `pre_actions` | `tools` |\r\n|---|---|---|\r\n| Can be skipped | No | Yes. The model may not call it if the description and the prompt do not push for it |\r\n| Timing | Node entry | Inside a turn. The model can call a tool, speak, then call another |\r\n| Result in the conversation | A tool call and tool result pair is added to the history | The same |\r\n| `response_mapping` lives | On the **node** | On the **tool** |\r\n| `description` | Ignored | The only text the model reads about the tool |\r\n\r\nTo run a fixed call before a tool-driven conversation, use a passthrough `api_request` node and transition from it into the conversation node. Do not build a long chain of passthrough nodes for optional actions. Put those into one conversation node with several tools.\r\n\r\n### A `pre_actions` node\r\n\r\n```json\r\n{\r\n  \"name\": \"fetch_order\",\r\n  \"node_type\": \"api_request\",\r\n  \"passthrough\": true,\r\n  \"passthrough_default_transition\": \"present\",\r\n  \"on_enter\": { \"type\": \"silent\" },\r\n  \"pre_actions\": [\r\n    {\r\n      \"type\": \"function\",\r\n      \"config\": { \"timeoutMs\": 10000, \"maxRetries\": 2 },\r\n      \"function\": {\r\n        \"name\": \"get_order\",\r\n        \"handler\": \"__function__:api_call\",\r\n        \"description\": \"Get the order.\",\r\n        \"parameters\": {\r\n          \"type\": \"object\",\r\n          \"properties\": {\r\n            \"url\": { \"type\": \"string\", \"default\": \"https://api.example.com/v1/get_order\" },\r\n            \"method\": { \"type\": \"string\", \"default\": \"POST\" },\r\n            \"headers\": { \"type\": \"object\", \"properties\": { \"Content-Type\": { \"type\": \"string\", \"default\": \"application/json\" } } },\r\n            \"body\": { \"type\": \"object\", \"properties\": { \"order_number\": { \"type\": \"string\", \"default\": \"{{order_number}}\" } }, \"required\": [] }\r\n          }\r\n        }\r\n      }\r\n    }\r\n  ],\r\n  \"response_mapping\": [\r\n    { \"path\": \"data.items\", \"variable\": \"items\" }\r\n  ],\r\n  \"transitions\": [\r\n    { \"target_node\": \"present\", \"description\": \"always\", \"condition\": { \"operator\": \"always\" } }\r\n  ]\r\n}\r\n```\r\n\r\nThe last `always` transition is mandatory. A passthrough node with no matching transition deadlocks.\r\n\r\n### Tool descriptions\r\n\r\nIn `tools`, the `description` decides whether the model calls the tool.\r\n\r\n- Write it as an instruction with the trigger: \"CALL it every time the caller gives an order number.\"\r\n- A plain description such as \"Looks up an order\" lets the model acknowledge the request in words and never call the tool. That is the most common failure of nodes with many tools.\r\n- If two tools could match, say which one not to use and name the other.\r\n- In `task_message`, put the tool rules before long reference text and state: when a rule says to use a tool, call the tool.\r\n- The working builder also tells the model to call one tool per message and to speak after every tool result. Verified in a working builder. In a trial call a model turn with no text and no tool call produced the error event \"The model returned no speakable content\".\r\n\r\nA tool is available only in the nodes that list it. Seen in a trial call: the model called a tool that the current node did not list. The result was the text \"The function `<name>` is not currently available.\"\r\n\r\n## `response_mapping`\r\n\r\n`response_mapping` copies values from a call result into named variables. It needs no model call.\r\n\r\n```json\r\n{\r\n  \"response_mapping\": [\r\n    { \"path\": \"data.status\", \"variable\": \"order_status\" },\r\n    { \"path\": \"data.items\", \"variable\": \"items\" }\r\n  ]\r\n}\r\n```\r\n\r\n| Field | Meaning |\r\n|---|---|\r\n| `path` | Dot path into the tool result |\r\n| `variable` | Name of the variable to set |\r\n\r\n### Placement\r\n\r\n- For a tool in `tools`: inside the tool object, next to `function` and `config`.\r\n- For `pre_actions`: at the **node level**. A `response_mapping` nested inside a `pre_actions` entry is ignored and the variable never appears.\r\n\r\n`extraction_schema` follows the same placement rule if you use it on a call result.\r\n\r\n### Where a result lands (trial findings)\r\n\r\nSeen in a trial call: the result of an `api_call` is an envelope around the HTTP response.\r\n\r\n```json\r\n{\r\n  \"status\": \"success\",\r\n  \"data\": { \"ok\": true, \"verified\": true, \"attempts_left\": 3 },\r\n  \"message\": \"Successfully executed POST request\",\r\n  \"status_code\": 200,\r\n  \"header\": { \"Content-Type\": \"application/json\" }\r\n}\r\n```\r\n\r\n`data` is the JSON body the API returned. `header` holds the HTTP response headers.\r\n\r\nThe engine also writes every result into the variable table by itself, flattened with dots. You do not need a mapping for these. For a tool named `check_answer` whose API returned `{\"ok\": true, \"verified\": true, \"attempts_left\": 3}`, the trial call's variable table held:\r\n\r\n| Variable | Value |\r\n|---|---|\r\n| `check_answer_args.<field>` | Each argument the model sent |\r\n| `check_answer_response.status` | `success` |\r\n| `check_answer_response.data.ok` | `true` (a string) |\r\n| `check_answer_response.data.verified` | `true` (a string) |\r\n| `check_answer_response.data.attempts_left` | `3` |\r\n| `check_answer_response.message` | `Successfully executed POST request` |\r\n| `check_answer_response.status_code` | `200` |\r\n| `check_answer_response.header.<Header-Name>` | One variable per response header |\r\n| `check_answer_status_code` | `200` |\r\n| `check_answer_success_count` | Number of successful calls so far |\r\n\r\nEach call of the same tool overwrites the previous `_args` and `_response` values.\r\n\r\n### Which mapping paths work\r\n\r\n- **Start the path at the envelope, with `data.`**. If the API returns `{\"ok\": true}`, map `data.ok`. If it returns `{\"products\": [...]}`, map `data.products`. Verified in a working builder, on a tool-level mapping and on a node-level mapping.\r\n- A path without the `data.` prefix (for the same body: `ok`) is not confirmed. A smaller example builder uses it. In an early trial call the mapped variables stayed unset and the transitions on them never fired. The mapping paths of that call are not recorded. The builder that passed uses `data.` paths.\r\n- The flattened key can be used directly as a transition variable: `check_answer_response.data.verified`. Verified in a working builder, which tests the mapped variable and the flattened key in the same OR group as a safeguard.\r\n- Booleans arrive as strings. The trial call showed `true` in lower case in the flattened keys. Test both `\"true\"` and `\"True\"`, or map a string field.\r\n- A mapped list or object: the exact form it has later inside `vars` is not confirmed. The working builder reads it defensively. It accepts a list, a JSON string, and flattened keys.\r\n\r\n## Routing on tool results\r\n\r\nRouting runs again after a tool result arrives. A transition on a result variable fires in the same turn, before any further model turn. Seen in a trial call.\r\n\r\n```json\r\n{\r\n  \"transitions\": [\r\n    {\r\n      \"target_node\": \"load_order\",\r\n      \"description\": \"The check passed\",\r\n      \"condition\": {\r\n        \"logic\": \"or\",\r\n        \"conditions\": [\r\n          { \"variable\": \"check_passed\", \"operator\": \"equals\", \"operand\": \"true\" },\r\n          { \"variable\": \"check_passed\", \"operator\": \"equals\", \"operand\": \"True\" },\r\n          { \"variable\": \"check_answer_response.data.verified\", \"operator\": \"equals\", \"operand\": \"true\" },\r\n          { \"variable\": \"check_answer_response.data.verified\", \"operator\": \"equals\", \"operand\": \"True\" }\r\n        ]\r\n      }\r\n    }\r\n  ]\r\n}\r\n```\r\n\r\nRules:\r\n\r\n- On a passthrough node, end with an `always` transition so that a failed or empty response does not deadlock.\r\n- Use a tool result to leave a conversation node. The pattern \"a closing tool sets a variable, a transition on that variable leads to an `end_call` node\" is verified in a working builder.\r\n- To leave a node that carries out an action, do not re-test the user's earlier intent with `matches_intent`. The router looks at the latest user message and can route away before the tool was called. Extract a follow-up enum with a default such as `stay`, and branch with `equals`.\r\n\r\n### Success and failure counters\r\n\r\nThe engine keeps two integer variables per call target. No mapping is needed.\r\n\r\n| Placement | Variables |\r\n|---|---|\r\n| `pre_actions` node | `<nodeId>_success_count`, `<nodeId>_failure_count` |\r\n| Tool in `tools` with handler `api_call` | `<toolName>_success_count`, `<toolName>_failure_count` |\r\n\r\nSeen in a trial call: `check_answer_success_count` was `2` after two successful calls, and a code node named `setup` produced `setup_success_count` with the value `1`.\r\n\r\n```json\r\n{\r\n  \"transitions\": [\r\n    { \"target_node\": \"give_up\", \"description\": \"The lookup failed three times\", \"condition\": { \"logic\": \"or\", \"conditions\": [ { \"variable\": \"lookup_order_failure_count\", \"operator\": \"greater_than\", \"operand\": 2 } ] } },\r\n    { \"target_node\": \"ask_again\", \"description\": \"always\", \"condition\": { \"operator\": \"always\" } }\r\n  ]\r\n}\r\n```\r\n\r\n### `suppress_tool_results`\r\n\r\nA node-level boolean for nodes that run API calls. With `true` the dialogue model sees only a short `{status: \"success\"}` summary in place of the raw response. Variables are still mapped and routing still sees them. Use it for large responses, or when the mapped variables already hold everything the model needs.\r\n\r\n### Background tools\r\n\r\nA tool with `\"config\": {\"interruptible\": false}` runs in the background. A user interruption does not cancel it. Its result arrives one or more turns later. The mapped variables, the `_response` keys and the counters are filled as usual, but not in the same turn. A transition that reads them right after the call can find them unset. Gate on the variable with `is_set`.\r\n\r\n## Retries and timeouts\r\n\r\n| Setting | Where | Default | What it does |\r\n|---|---|---|---|\r\n| `timeoutMs` | `config` | not documented | Time limit of one attempt, in milliseconds |\r\n| `maxRetries` | `config` | not documented | Tool-level retries after a failed response |\r\n| `connect_retries` | `parameters.properties`, as a baked parameter | `2` | Retries when the connection could not be established |\r\n| `timeout_ms` | `code_exec` parameter | `30000` | Time limit of a code snippet |\r\n\r\n`connect_retries` in detail:\r\n\r\n- It retries only failures to establish the connection: connection refused, or reset during the TCP or TLS handshake. The request never reached the server, so a repeat is safe even for POST.\r\n- HTTP error statuses and timeouts are not retried by it.\r\n- Hard cap `5`. `0` means a single attempt.\r\n- Back-off is 150 ms times the attempt number, with a new connection each time.\r\n- All attempts share one 30 second budget for the request.\r\n- Declare it next to `url` and `method`: `\"connect_retries\": {\"type\": \"number\", \"default\": 0}`. It is not a `config` field.\r\n\r\nThe working builder sets `maxRetries` to `0` on tools that write data and to `2` on its read-only `pre_actions` call. A retried write can record the same thing twice if the API is not idempotent.\r\n\r\n## Pre and post Python hooks\r\n\r\nAn `api_call` tool can carry two Python snippets. They run on the server, in a sandbox, right before and right after the call. They are top-level fields of the tool object.\r\n\r\n```json\r\n{\r\n  \"type\": \"function\",\r\n  \"config\": { \"timeoutMs\": 10000, \"maxRetries\": 0 },\r\n  \"function\": {\r\n    \"name\": \"check_answer\",\r\n    \"handler\": \"__function__:api_call\",\r\n    \"description\": \"Check one answer. CALL it every time the caller answers the question.\",\r\n    \"parameters\": {\r\n      \"type\": \"object\",\r\n      \"properties\": {\r\n        \"url\": { \"type\": \"string\", \"default\": \"https://api.example.com/v1/check_answer\" },\r\n        \"method\": { \"type\": \"string\", \"default\": \"POST\" },\r\n        \"body\": { \"type\": \"object\", \"properties\": { \"answer\": { \"type\": \"string\", \"description\": \"The caller's answer\", \"default\": \"{{answer}}\" } }, \"required\": [\"answer\"] }\r\n      }\r\n    }\r\n  },\r\n  \"pre_process_code\": \"vars['member_code'] = str(vars.get('member_code') or '').strip().upper()\",\r\n  \"post_process_code\": \"try:\\n    if isinstance(result, dict):\\n        result.pop('header', None)\\n        d = result.get('data')\\n        if isinstance(d, dict):\\n            d['what_to_do_next'] = 'Ask the next question.' if d.get('verified') else 'Ask the same question again.'\\nexcept Exception:\\n    pass\\n\"\r\n}\r\n```\r\n\r\n| Hook | Names available | Use |\r\n|---|---|---|\r\n| `pre_process_code` | `vars`: mutable dict that fills the `{{var}}` placeholders of the request. `config`: the substituted request with `url`, `method`, `headers`, `body` | Clean a value, or rewrite the whole request |\r\n| `post_process_code` | `result`: the tool result, mutable | Reshape, shorten or extend the result |\r\n\r\nDocumented behaviour:\r\n\r\n- The post-hook runs before `response_mapping` and extraction see the result.\r\n- An empty or whitespace-only string means no hook.\r\n- Hooks are honoured only on `api_call` tools (and on the integration and knowledge search handlers). The platform removes them from any other handler when the workflow is saved.\r\n- In a pre-hook, setting `config['body']` to a string sends that string as the raw body, with no JSON encoding. Set the `Content-Type` header yourself in that case.\r\n\r\n### What `result` looks like in a post-hook\r\n\r\nThe internal reference shows `result` as the response body. A working builder shows otherwise. Verified in a working builder:\r\n\r\n- For an `api_call`, `result` is the envelope described above: a dict with `status`, `data`, `message`, `status_code` and `header`. The API's JSON body is `result[\"data\"]`.\r\n- A key that the hook adds inside `result[\"data\"]` reaches the dialogue model as part of the tool result. The working builder adds a `what_to_do_next` sentence there, and its prompt tells the model to obey it.\r\n- `result.pop(\"header\", None)` removes the response headers. The working builder does this on every tool, so that headers do not take up space in the model's context.\r\n- Mapping paths still start with `data.` when a post-hook is present.\r\n\r\nThe working builder writes every hook inside `try` and `except Exception: pass` and checks types with `isinstance` before use. What the platform does when a hook raises is not documented.\r\n\r\nSeen in a test on 10 October: a post-hook can read call variables and write new variables, and a later pre-hook reads them. Not confirmed: whether a change to `result` also changes the flattened `<tool>_response.*` variables.\r\n\r\n## Code nodes and `code_exec`\r\n\r\nA `code` node runs a Python snippet when it is entered. The snippet is a `code_exec` function in `pre_actions`.\r\n\r\n```json\r\n{\r\n  \"name\": \"compute_delivery\",\r\n  \"node_type\": \"code\",\r\n  \"passthrough\": true,\r\n  \"passthrough_default_transition\": \"tell_delivery\",\r\n  \"on_enter\": { \"type\": \"silent\" },\r\n  \"pre_actions\": [\r\n    {\r\n      \"type\": \"function\",\r\n      \"function\": {\r\n        \"name\": \"code_exec\",\r\n        \"handler\": \"__function__:code_exec\",\r\n        \"parameters\": {\r\n          \"type\": \"object\",\r\n          \"properties\": {\r\n            \"code\": { \"type\": \"string\", \"default\": \"import datetime\\ntry:\\n    start = datetime.date.fromisoformat(str(vars.get('shipped_on'))[:10])\\n    eta = start + datetime.timedelta(days=3)\\n    vars['delivery_day'] = eta.isoformat()\\n    vars['delivery_state'] = 'known'\\nexcept Exception:\\n    vars['delivery_day'] = ''\\n    vars['delivery_state'] = 'unknown'\\n\" },\r\n            \"timeout_ms\": { \"type\": \"number\", \"default\": 10000 }\r\n          }\r\n        }\r\n      }\r\n    }\r\n  ],\r\n  \"transitions\": [\r\n    { \"target_node\": \"tell_delivery\", \"description\": \"always\", \"condition\": { \"operator\": \"always\" } }\r\n  ]\r\n}\r\n```\r\n\r\n| Parameter | Meaning |\r\n|---|---|\r\n| `code` | The Python source, as a baked `default` string |\r\n| `timeout_ms` | Time limit in milliseconds. Default `30000` |\r\n\r\n### Reading and writing variables\r\n\r\n- The snippet gets a dict named `vars`. It holds the current workflow variables.\r\n- Read with `vars.get(\"name\")`. A variable that was never set is absent.\r\n- Write with `vars[\"name\"] = value`. Every key the snippet assigns becomes a workflow variable.\r\n\r\nVerified in a working builder:\r\n\r\n- Call variables are in `vars`. The builder reads `vars.get(\"now\")`, a call variable passed with the simulation.\r\n- The result of an earlier `api_request` node is reachable from `vars` in a later code node. The builder looks for it under the mapped variable name first and under the flattened result keys second. Which of the two held the data is not recorded.\r\n- A string written to `vars[\"date_facts\"]` in a code node resolves as `{{date_facts}}` in the `task_message` of later conversation nodes. Multi-line strings work.\r\n- The standard modules `json`, `re` and `datetime` can be imported.\r\n- The same snippet can run in two code nodes at different points of the call. The later run sees the variables added in between.\r\n\r\n### What a code node returns\r\n\r\nSeen in a trial call: the result of a successful `code_exec` is\r\n\r\n```json\r\n{ \"status\": \"success\", \"data\": null, \"message\": \"Code executed successfully\", \"status_code\": 200 }\r\n```\r\n\r\nThe snippet's output is not in `data`. Its values reach the workflow only through `vars`. After a code node named `setup` ran, the variable table held:\r\n\r\n| Variable | Value |\r\n|---|---|\r\n| each key the snippet wrote, for example `date_facts` | the value, with source `tool_result` and source node `setup` |\r\n| `setup_response.status` | `success` |\r\n| `setup_response.data` | `null` |\r\n| `setup_response.message` | `Code executed successfully` |\r\n| `setup_response.status_code` | `200` |\r\n| `setup_status_code` | `200` |\r\n| `setup_success_count` | `1` |\r\n\r\nFor a code node the automatic keys are named after the **node**, not after `code_exec`.\r\n\r\n### Rules for snippets\r\n\r\n- **Do not write `{{` or `}}` in the code.** The internal sources disagree on whether the `code` field is templated. One says it is exempt. Another says placeholders are resolved when the code runs and an unset one is left as literal text, which breaks the Python. The working builder removes every `{{` and `}}` from generated code (a nested dict literal such as `{'a': {'b': 1}}` ends in `}}`) and reads all values from `vars`.\r\n- **Never let a snippet raise.** Wrap the body in `try` and `except`, and write a fallback value for every variable that a later prompt or condition reads. A prompt that references an unset variable shows the literal placeholder to the model.\r\n- **Write strings for routing flags**, such as `known` and `unknown`. Do not route on a Python boolean. See \"Do not route on a boolean\" in workflow-format.md.\r\n- Keep snippets fast. They block the conversation.\r\n- Do arithmetic, date calculation and counting in code, and give the model the finished facts as text. Verified in a working builder: it computes a block of date facts in a code node and places it in the prompts.\r\n- Treat a code node like an API node: `passthrough: true`, a `passthrough_default_transition`, and a last `always` transition.\r\n\r\n### Sandbox limits\r\n\r\n| Limit | Value |\r\n|---|---|\r\n| Time | `timeout_ms`, default `30000` |\r\n| Modules | `json`, `re` and `datetime` are verified in a working builder. The full list is not documented |\r\n| Network and file access | Not documented |\r\n| Memory and output size | Not documented |\r\n\r\n### `code_exec` as a tool\r\n\r\n`code_exec` can also be a tool in the `tools` array of a conversation node, called by the model. Its parameters are top-level arguments, declared without `default` so the model fills them. For such a tool the `code` field resolves `{{var}}` when the tool is called, against the current variable table. The working builder does not use this form, so nothing about it is verified here.\r\n\r\n## Lifecycle tools\r\n\r\nA tool whose `config.alwaysRunsAt` is a non-empty list is never shown to the model. The engine fires it at a fixed point of the call.\r\n\r\n| Value | When |\r\n|---|---|\r\n| `start_of_call` | During call setup, before the first turn |\r\n| `end_of_call` | After the call has ended |\r\n| `each_turn` | Accepted by the schema but not implemented. It never fires |\r\n\r\nPut a lifecycle tool that must run on every call into the workflow's top-level `tools` array.\r\n\r\n```json\r\n{\r\n  \"initial_node\": \"greeting\",\r\n  \"nodes\": {},\r\n  \"tools\": [\r\n    {\r\n      \"type\": \"function\",\r\n      \"config\": { \"alwaysRunsAt\": [\"end_of_call\"] },\r\n      \"function\": {\r\n        \"name\": \"push_call_result\",\r\n        \"handler\": \"__function__:api_call\",\r\n        \"parameters\": {\r\n          \"type\": \"object\",\r\n          \"properties\": {\r\n            \"url\": { \"type\": \"string\", \"default\": \"https://api.example.com/v1/call-results\" },\r\n            \"method\": { \"type\": \"string\", \"default\": \"POST\" },\r\n            \"body\": {\r\n              \"type\": \"object\",\r\n              \"properties\": {\r\n                \"summary\": { \"type\": \"string\", \"default\": \"{{call.analysis.summary}}\" },\r\n                \"reason\": { \"type\": \"string\", \"default\": \"{{call.call_end_reason}}\" }\r\n              }\r\n            }\r\n          }\r\n        }\r\n      }\r\n    }\r\n  ]\r\n}\r\n```\r\n\r\nRules:\r\n\r\n- A workflow-level tool without `alwaysRunsAt` never runs and the model never sees it.\r\n- A lifecycle tool in a node's `tools` runs for `start_of_call` only on the `initial_node`, and for `end_of_call` only on the node in which the call ended.\r\n- If a `{{var}}` parameter is still unresolved, one model call fills the parameters. If everything resolves, the handler runs directly.\r\n- `response_mapping` is applied for `start_of_call`. It is not applied for `end_of_call`.\r\n- At the end of a call the order is: call analysis, then `end_of_call` tools, then success-criteria judging. An `end_of_call` tool can use `{{call.analysis.summary}}`, `{{call.analysis.structured_data_str}}`, `{{call.call_end_reason}}`, `{{call.call_end_reason_category}}`, `{{call.call_tags}}` and `{{call.duration}}`.\r\n- A malformed lifecycle tool is dropped at run time without an error. Validate with a dry run (api.md).\r\n\r\n## Tool results and the next turn\r\n\r\nAfter a tool call returns, the model sees the result and has to produce the next turn itself. Two things were seen in practice runs with the hackathon's agent model:\r\n\r\n- **The agent says nothing after the tool call.** The result arrives, no assistant message follows, and the simulated customer hangs up after waiting. It happens more often when the result is bare data such as `{\"ok\": true, \"verified\": true}`.\r\n- **The agent ignores a negative result.** For example it reads `\"verified\": false` and moves on to the next question as if the answer had passed.\r\n\r\nWhat fixed both in a working builder (verified, see the post-hook section above): a post-hook on the tool that adds one plain instruction line to the result before the model sees it, for example a `what_to_do_next` field that says what the answer means and what to say or call next. The model then has something to act on.\r\n\r\nAlso say it in the step's prompt: after every tool result, speak to the customer or call the next tool. Never end a turn with nothing.\r\n\r\n## Checklist\r\n\r\n- Fixed values use `default`, never `const`.\r\n- Each `api_call` body field is a leaf under `body.properties` with its own `default`, `description` and `enum`.\r\n- `tools` only on `conversation` nodes. `pre_actions` only on `api_request` and `code` nodes.\r\n- `response_mapping` on the tool for `tools`, on the node for `pre_actions`.\r\n- Mapping paths start with `data.`.\r\n- Booleans from results are tested as `\"true\"` and `\"True\"`, or replaced by string fields.\r\n- Every passthrough node ends with an `always` transition.\r\n- Code reads and writes through `vars`, never raises, and contains no `{{` or `}}`.\r\n- Tool descriptions say when to call the tool.\r\n\r\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"\"C:\\\\Users\\\\9bakl\\\\.cache\\\\codex-runtimes\\\\codex-primary-runtime\\\\dependencies\\\\native\\\\powershell\\\\pwsh.exe\" -Command 'Get-ChildItem -Name; Get-Content BUILD_INSTRUCTIONS.md'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"\"C:\\\\Users\\\\9bakl\\\\.cache\\\\codex-runtimes\\\\codex-primary-runtime\\\\dependencies\\\\native\\\\powershell\\\\pwsh.exe\" -Command 'Get-ChildItem -Name; Get-Content BUILD_INSTRUCTIONS.md'","aggregated_output":"inputs\r\nBUILD_INSTRUCTIONS.md\r\nbuild_artifacts.py\r\ncheck_artifacts.py\r\ncontroller.py\r\ncoverage.json\r\nlocal_checks.json\r\npolicy.json\r\nworkflow.json\r\n# Competition builder: evidence to a regulated Freya workflow\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nYour job is to produce a phone-agent workflow from the run's supplied bank evidence and OpenAPI schema. You are the builder, not the phone agent. Do not contact Freya yourself. The host validates, saves, publishes, tests, exports and logs your artifact. Never read credentials, unrelated projects, personal settings or earlier runs.\r\n\r\nDetermine the domain and the entry trust boundary from the new evidence before applying any example in this guide. An inbound IVR handoff that explicitly supplies an authenticated customer identifier must not acquire outbound recipient, DOB, collection persuasion, arrears, callback or payment-promise requirements from old examples. Use only the current API; a service name from a past brief is not stable unless it also appears in this API. Read source dates, author authority, explicitly accepted corrections and scoped amendments; input file order alone cannot turn a rejected meeting suggestion or an old FAQ into current policy. Preserve the latest accepted decision and record the rejected alternative with its source.\r\n\r\nIf the upstream interface owns menu/human routing and the current service contract has no routing endpoint, do not invent an HTTP endpoint. Implement the supplied exit state through documented workflow controls and an explicit routing result variable, announce the required destination, then terminate or hand off as the platform supports. Do not claim that a nonexistent tool completed the transfer.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nRead every input document completely. Later explicitly scoped amendments replace earlier rules; record provenance and conflicts in `policy.json`. Extract all numeric limits, security fields and their order, calendar, bands, amounts, outcome/reason table, exception priority, transfer queues, callback windows, multi-product semantics, confirmation requirements and failure rules from these inputs. Do not assume they match the practice brief. There are no default behaviours: which queue each kind of request goes to, what is offered on hardship, dispute or a question about charges, and whether notes are written on single-product calls all come only from this run's inputs. When the inputs forbid an offer (for example no transfer on hardship), never make it. Values from the brief belong only in generated run artifacts, never in shared builder code or this knowledge file. Tool names, fields and enum values all come from this run's OpenAPI; nothing from an earlier API is stable by default. Do not silently reuse the base agent's incomplete policy. Reference customer identifiers, answers, balances and calendar examples are not runtime facts. Use call variables and actual bank responses.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nCreate `policy.json` with sources, extracted rules, unresolved conflicts and a compact machine-readable policy. Create `coverage.json` listing every substantive input rule and the node, condition, code or hook that implements it. No pretend coverage: mark omissions/uncertainty explicitly. Write `workflow.json`, a raw Freya workflow definition. An exported envelope is not an input definition. Do not emit agent_config or credentials.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nCoverage entries need a nonempty rule identifier (`rule_id`, `rule` or `id`) and explicit `status`; preserve source and implementation references. Match every supplied tool body property's type, including optional properties. Structural coverage is bookkeeping, not a behavioral proof.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nThe speech-event contract must represent heterogeneous clauses from a single utterance: different entity/product decisions, amounts and dates need per-target clauses, not one shared scalar plus a list of IDs. Use explicit typed clause fields or a faithful validated raw-speech parser; test the minimal advertised extractor output in each active phase. A loop over kind names at opening proves no later behavior. Never inject unadvertised clause structures into self-tests.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nBind extracted product references to the returned account records using one shared resolver for scalar events and clauses. The extractor's advertised human-readable type names must resolve identically to API enum spellings and rendered labels, including a card type whose displayed label adds its ending digits. Normalize only known types/labels from the supplied API and returned records; never infer unknown IDs. Do not silently process a subset when an explicitly collective decision names every returned product: resolve every reference or ask for clarification before any mutation. Test the advertised spaced type names, canonical enums, exact IDs and actual account labels for both homogeneous and heterogeneous decisions. A current-arrears payment claim remains paid when the customer repeats it while declining an additional payment; distinguish it from a historically closed account or a disputed debt.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nKeep extraction payloads compact enough for the real platform response path. One compound utterance must not produce a verbatim copy of the full speech inside every clause plus duplicate scalar facts. Use only documented extraction types; compact string-encoded structured clauses or multiple typed variables are acceptable when the exact grammar is advertised and consumed. Preserve every product decision, asserted amount/date and completion status; compactness must not flatten heterogeneous facts. Real extraction_error traces are separate from policy-code results. Clear stale event/consent fields each turn. Malformed or missing extraction never authorizes a bank write; use a bounded phase-appropriate clarification when permitted by the supplied policy, rather than reusing an earlier event or looping indefinitely.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nThe outer extraction response can succeed while its inner string violates the advertised event grammar. Validate both layers and distinguish this from a bank failure. When actual traces repeatedly lose quotes or closing delimiters in nested JSON strings, prefer documented primitive variables or a simpler explicitly delimited, escaped text grammar with a complete contract; do not silently repair truncated consent or drop a malformed sibling clause. Test three heterogeneous decisions and repeated multi-target refusals through the real extractor. Compactness alone is not a format guarantee.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nAvoid wide positional rows filled with consecutive empty delimiters: the real extractor can insert an extra empty field even when the semantic answer is correct. Prefer documented native scalar fields, or short tagged rows with explicit field names and only relevant optional fields. Advertise exact escaping, required fields, completion and terminal markers; reject duplicate/unknown fields and malformed sibling rows before applying decisions. Execute the literal examples copied from the advertised extraction prompt through the actual decoder, including recipient confirmation, DOB, phone suffix, heterogeneous decisions and later consent. Also exercise missing/extra separators, explicit partial answers and malformed rows. A hand-built correctly positioned helper fixture alone does not verify the producer contract. Never accept a malformed payment/consent event by trimming, shifting or dropping columns.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nGenerate a compact explicit producer/consumer contract manifest with the exact kind set, keys, delimiters, value grammar, completion states and literal examples. Check that every advertised example decodes unchanged and every consumed field is advertised; keep annotations outside literal examples. Distinguish local decoder tests from actual extractor observations. Any ordinary punctuation allowed in natural dates must also be allowed by the event value grammar without stripping malformed syntax or inventing missing values. Resolve the current phase's complete primary intent with the input's precedence, retain independent administrative questions until spoken, and apply payment completion guards only to actual payment corrections.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nApply completion and polarity gates before constructing the set of actionable primary intents. Mere kind membership in an unfinished, quoted or hypothetical clause must not finalize a death/wrong-person outcome or authorize a transfer; preserve the input policy's precedence among actually complete assertions. Pause the affected action and ask a permitted bounded clarification for an unfinished assertion, preserving unrelated complete facts and existing security state. Use the same whole-token amount grammar and finite-value checks in partial-retention, complete-proposal and confirmation branches; a numeric cast must not smuggle NaN, infinity or an unadvertised exponent into readback or stored facts. A decoder problem is not a demonstrated bank outage. Test literal c=0 primary-intent rows, complete positive controls, conflicting same-target support answers, and malformed numeric siblings without weakening the supplied privacy/identity requirements.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nFormat-error clarification must use the current authoritative phase. Before identity is verified, name only the missing permitted security/recipient answer; never ask about products, payments or balances because decoding failed. Preserve verified security answers and wrong-answer/decline counters according to the supplied policy. An unreadable event is not a voluntary refusal. Keep the semantic state and bounded format-retry count separate; a transport-format failure must not manufacture a customer-declined close reason. After verification, clarify only the unresolved target/component without rebroadcasting already refused offers.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n## Low-risk gates are permissive (recipient confirmation)\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nRecipient confirmation is a low-risk gate: it only moves the call on to the security questions, and the bank's identity tool remains the real gate before any disclosure or write. Calibrate it for real callers, who say \"Yes, this is Daniel\", \"Speaking\", \"Yes, it's me, sorry, busy morning\", \"I just said, that's me\" or \"Mr Reyes speaking, what is this about?\". A failed recipient step ends the whole call, so a false rejection costs the entire case, while a false acceptance only costs one security question.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n- Advance when either of these holds: the typed extraction reports a complete self-confirmation, or the current raw speech contains an affirmative or self-identification cue (yes, speaking, that's me, it's me, this is/I am/I'm plus any part of the called person's name, the name alone). In both cases the turn must contain no explicit exclusion.\r\n\r\n- Exclusions are explicit statements, not single common words: the speaker says they are someone else or a relative (\"this is his wife\", \"I'm her son\"), the person is not available, there is a wrong number, or a death. Never treat isolated tokens such as said, if, would, son, not, or a quotation mark as an exclusion on their own. Small talk, complaints, a question back or an AI-identity question in the same turn do not block confirmation.\r\n\r\n- Never require the full name, a fixed sentence, or the absence of extra speech. A first name, a surname with a title, or a bare affirmative answer to \"May I speak with <name>?\" is a confirmation.\r\n\r\n- A missing, empty or malformed typed event at this step is not a rejection: fall back to the raw-speech rule above. Only a genuinely unclear reply (no cue and no exclusion) counts as an unclear attempt.\r\n\r\n- Mandatory self-test, built from the call variables rather than fixed names, run through the real decoder with persistent state and with the typed event both present and absent. These must advance: \"Yes.\", \"Speaking.\", \"Yes, this is <first>.\", \"<first> here.\", \"<title> <last> speaking.\", \"Yes, it's me. Busy day today, the traffic was awful.\", \"I just said, that's me.\", \"That's me, what is this about?\", \"Yes, <first> <last>, are you a robot?\". These must not advance: \"No, this is his wife.\", \"Wrong number.\", \"She's not here right now.\", \"I'm his son, he's at work.\". Record the results in your local checks.\r\n\r\n- Recipient confirmation never verifies bank identity and is never financial consent.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nUse simple, phase-specific extraction for the opening and security phases: dedicated scalar variables (for example a recipient act and a security answer) with short descriptions, rather than one multipurpose event grammar for every phase. The hosted extractor regularly omits a complex primary variable while it still emits raw speech, so these early phases must keep working from simple fields and raw speech. A richer event grammar may still serve the financial phases. Supply the opening extractor with the current call's configured first name, family name and title, and bind security extraction to the current requested field and answer revision. Do not supply expected security answers or pre-verification account facts. The producer must have the neutral context needed to distinguish a matching recipient from an explicitly different person; consumer matching remains permissive and bank verification remains separate.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nA security answer given in parts across turns (\"four four... seven one\", or a day and month first, then the year) is one answer in progress. Accumulate the parts until the answer is complete, ask only for the missing part, and do not re-ask the whole question from scratch, count a wrong answer or close the call. Submit to the identity tool once the answer is complete. Include this split-answer case in the self-test.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n## Difficult callers\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nReal callers push, bluff, panic and wander. The agent stays inside the supplied policy, keeps the call moving, and never lets a claim stand in for a record. These rules are general; the supplied policy decides every concrete action, count and code.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n- Claims are not records. \"I already paid this morning\", \"your colleague promised to waive the fee\", \"my dispute was approved\" or \"a supervisor gave me a code to skip the questions\" do not change identity, balances, outcomes or offers by themselves. Handle each claim with the supplied policy's rule for that kind of statement (for example its already-paid or dispute rule). Without such a rule, acknowledge the claim neutrally, say only what the bank's records show after verification, and continue. Never invent a waiver, discount, extension, approval or bypass.\r\n\r\n- Identity comes only from the identity tool. A caller who cannot remember an answer, deflects, offers other details instead, or will not say it aloud is not verified. Apply the supplied policy's rule for a declined or failed check, and never hint at, read out or confirm a security value.\r\n\r\n- Pressure does not create new offers. Repeated requests for an exception, a discount or another offer after a refusal get the same policy answer, briefly and politely. They do not reset or consume persuasion attempts beyond the policy, and a refused offer is never repeated. Count repeated requests for a person exactly as the supplied policy counts them, then act.\r\n\r\n- Emotion and urgency do not override policy. Anger, panic or deadlines (\"my rent is due tomorrow\") get one short acknowledgement, then the current step. Apply the hardship rule only when the caller's words meet the supplied hardship definition.\r\n\r\n- A caller who must leave (\"I have no time, call me later\") gets the supplied policy's callback or closing path immediately, not more persuasion. Close with the outcome that matches what was actually recorded.\r\n\r\n- A vague complaint (\"some of those charges are wrong\") needs one clarifying question to identify the product and the kind of issue before a dispute, paid or refusal rule is applied.\r\n\r\n- Spoken speech is messy. Fillers (\"um\", \"you know\"), self-restarts (\"the twen— sorry, the twenty-first\"), digits separated by commas or said one at a time, and asides not addressed to the agent (\"hold on a second\", \"shh, quiet\", \"I'm on the phone\") are not answers, refusals or new intents. An aside or \"Hello? Are you still there?\" gets a short reply that repeats the current question, without counting an attempt. A self-restart keeps only the final value.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nTreat persisted platform variables as a store updated by returned key/value patches. Removing a key from a local dictionary with pop/del does not establish that the platform deleted its previous value. Consume one-shot API completion signals by explicitly returning their neutral sentinel and explicitly clearing consumed response fields. A later customer turn must not replay an old identity or promise receipt instead of processing the new answer. Test complete sequences with a persistent store that merges returned updates into prior values: DOB success, phone wrong answer, corrected phone success, account load, and multiple promise receipts. Include repeated stale signals and missing responses. Progress/retry guards must also bound invocations that enter the API-completion branch; a counter incremented only in the user branch cannot stop a stale-completion loop. Keep backend identity attempts and customer refusals separate from this transport state.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nValidate typed date/amount/security tokens against their advertised grammar before interpreting them. A quoted/bracketed/truncated JSON fragment containing a weekday is not a valid payment date merely because a regex recognizes that word. Apply the same checks to scalar and compound confirmation fields. An explicit unfinished correction or contradiction in the latest customer speech must prevent a write even if an extractor mistakenly labels it complete consent; ask for a fresh complete readback and later clear agreement without guessing new terms.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nNormalize complete payment-date values through one advertised contract shared by scalar and compound events. Prefer a canonical date token produced from the bound call calendar; if accepting a complete natural form with redundant weekday/relative wording, ordinal suffixes or punctuation, validate the whole expression and that its explicit date and weekday/relative interpretation agree. Ordinary punctuation in a complete natural date is distinct from malformed JSON or truncated consent. Keep all policy calendar/window checks after normalization. Apply the supplied policy's shared-date rule to a positively supported common-date assertion regardless of whether its targets are encoded as a collective alias or an explicit target list. Do not infer a shared proposal merely because independent product dates happen to be equal. State any policy-required date substitution in the readback, retaining each unaffected target's date. When one sibling date fails atomic compound validation, preserve uncommitted facts and identify that sibling and missing component in the clarification; do not repeat unrelated product terms or silently discard the bad clause. Test identical complete human wording under the actual different date representations the extractor produced, alongside contradictory, unfinished and bracketed/quoted negative controls.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nBind offer acceptance to the exact current spoken offer and its target, revision and supported components. Keep offer acceptance separate from final financial consent: a complete present self commitment such as \"I can pay the full amount today\" can adopt the uniquely offered full amount and stated date without an extra yes token. A tiny final-consent phrase allowlist must not reject this proposal or relabel it as an unfinished amount. Validate subject, polarity, scope and actual completion; an unsupported acceptance is unresolved interpretation, not proof of a missing amount. Adopting offered terms only prepares a fresh complete readback and later consent; it never writes. If the offer omits a required component, retain the accepted components and clarify only the missing one under policy. A customer may repeat only its day number or ordinal after the agent offered a full date; validate that the explicit day agrees with the uniquely referenced offer before inheriting its month/year. Conflicting day, withdrawn or superseded offer, ambiguous target, qualified acceptance and unfinished correction cannot inherit those terms or authorize a write. Preserve complete month-first number-word dates as well as day-first forms under the same whole-value grammar. A consumer representation failure is not a second vague-period answer: do not increment the policy's vague-answer counter or substitute the deadline merely because an exact acceptance/date could not be decoded. Test the actual producer's elliptical, digit-ordinal, word-ordinal, month-first and canonical representations, with contradictory and stale-offer controls, through the persistent-state decoder and a fresh readback followed by separate consent.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nAccumulate a turn's nonconflicting component rows per resolved target before validating or advancing the dialogue. An amount row and date row for the same target must produce the same retained terms in either extractor row order, including when they complete an earlier partial answer. Preserve explicit correction order and clarify conflicting values; do not silently choose a value merely because it appears last in the serialized extraction. When corrections or retargeting require ordering, include source clause positions and explicit replacement relationships in the paired producer/decoder contract, and validate them against the actual speech. An explicit later replacement supersedes only that target/component. Moving a proposal to another target clears its old uncommitted terms only when that move is actually asserted; unrelated proposals and recorded effects remain. Missing or contradictory replacement evidence requires clarification. Remove only the completed component's pending obligation. A later valid proposal supersedes that target's deferred refusal and obsolete offer; another target's incomplete answer must not let the old refusal reopen agreed terms or consume a persuasion attempt. A valid new proposal still requires the policy's complete readback and later consent. Test both same-turn row permutations, separate-turn completions, refusal then voluntary proposal with an unfinished sibling, and conflicting corrections under the persistent update store.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nInterpret negation and completion in the scope of the actual clause. An affirmative idiom such as \"yes, no problem\" is not a refusal solely because a global regex sees \"no\"; explicit withdrawal, changed terms and unfinished correction still block consent. Validate a complete affirmative against the current readback's exact targets and terms, and never route a blocked or undecodable confirmation through a generic collective refusal that reopens paid or otherwise resolved siblings. Recover by a fresh readback or a clarification of the affected target. An ellipsis in an earlier clause does not mark a complete later sibling unfinished: bind completion evidence to its clause/component. Independently test idiomatic affirmatives, genuine scoped negatives, qualified yes, withdrawn consent, changed date/amount, nonterminal ellipsis and terminal unfinished speech, without weakening malformed-input or identity gates.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nAdvertise and consume the same kind set for scalar and compound events in every phase. Agreement with several already spoken counteroffers is acceptance of those exact offers, followed by the required joint readback and a separate confirmation; it is not yet permission to write. The extractor may express the agreement as one scoped event or several agreement clauses. Handle both without dropping targets, inventing terms, or classifying an unsupported speech-event shape as a bank-service outage. Unknown event shapes require bounded clarification and clear stale consent. A qualified yes or a new amount/date that differs from the spoken terms is a correction and invalidates the previous readback. Minimal contract tests must include compound acceptance in the offer phase and compound confirmation in the readback phase, with negative and incomplete controls.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nClear consent without losing the last validated terms needed for recovery. Persist the last actually spoken question, operation, target, offer/readback revision and complete terms across code/API invocations. Missing/malformed extraction invalidates authorization, not those facts. If the same complete readback remains the current question and the speech supplies no correction, withdrawal or intervening operation, ask one brief explicit confirmation of that readback within the policy clarification budget; only a later valid phase-bound affirmative may authorize it. If that context is missing or suspended, speak a fresh complete readback first. Never remain in a confirm phase with no active readback and an endless generic clarification. If it contains an unfinished correction, retain unaffected components and ask only for the changed/missing component before the fresh readback. Test a real sequence: valid proposal, spoken readback, null/malformed confirmation event, then a valid confirmation event. Also test null extraction on otherwise clear security replies; use only faithful whole-reply parsing or a bounded phase-specific clarification, never an inferred refusal or a payment/consent guessed from earlier speech. The advertised date grammar must match normalization: a verbatim date phrase cannot be promised if only ISO/explicit-year tokens are implemented without a missing-component clarification.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nPreserve each product's existing valid counteroffer when moving between products. Acceptance of a sibling product is not a refusal of this product and must not silently replace its offered date, amount or consume its attempt budget. After a below-floor proposal, state the actual permitted floor and the required residual-overdue warning before seeking its acceptance. Collective acceptance covers only counteroffers actually spoken, then still requires the supplied joint readback and separate confirmation.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nApply any supplied residual-overdue warning to every qualifying partial promise, including a voluntary valid amount, an exact floor and a corrected readback. The warning obligation must not live only in the below-floor counteroffer branch. Track that it was actually spoken at the policy's required point; a successful record or text-message notification alone does not satisfy that obligation.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nKeep unfinished corrections scoped to their actual target set. An unrelated entity update cannot borrow, complete or clear another entity's fragment. Preserve unrelated valid proposals, but prevent a joint readback/write while an unrecorded correction remains unresolved. Handle incomplete/partial/correction events before phase-specific proposal and consent branches, including hardship/support. A partially spoken amount/date never becomes eligible merely because the current phase changes. Completion requires a new valid proposal, readback and separate consent.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nRepresent the unresolved component explicitly in both the extraction contract and persisted proposal state. Separate an unfinished qualification/condition from an unfinished amount or date; a terminal \"if\" after otherwise complete offered terms does not establish an amount correction. Check the producer's component label against its actual source clause before creating a blocking requirement. If unclear, ask what condition or component remains unresolved, preserving offered terms without accepting the condition. A later supported unconditional acceptance of those current terms may supersede the unfinished qualification and proceed to a fresh readback; a date-only answer cannot resolve a genuinely unfinished amount. Test an incorrectly labelled amount fragment followed by complete date/offer acceptance alongside a real cut-off amount followed by date-only speech. The presence of a complete date in an incomplete utterance does not mean that the date is the unfinished component; it may accompany an unfinished amount. Likewise a weekday fragment can be an unfinished date without saying the word date. If the component cannot be determined, clarify which component changed and preserve unaffected validated facts. While an amount is unresolved, a later date-only answer must not silently fill it from the cached full offer, earlier superseded amount, floor or outstanding balance. Resolve that flag only from an explicit completed customer amount or, where the input policy permits it, an unambiguous acceptance of a newly spoken recovery offer for this same target and revision. Such acceptance adopts the exact spoken amount, not a cached full balance or an unrelated floor. Bind recovery-offer eligibility to the currently awaiting offer-acceptance question, target and offer revision; storing a recovery offer alone never makes later agreement refer to it. An explicit completed customer proposal or a newer complete readback supersedes that recovery offer: explicitly clear its active flag and revision in the persistent update store, while retaining unrelated targets. A confirmation of the latest readback must preserve that readback's exact terms and must not first reapply an older recovery amount. Test unfinished amount -> spoken recovery offer -> different complete policy-valid customer amount -> fresh readback of the new terms -> later confirmation; the dispatch must use only the latest readback and never restore the stale offer. A later date-only reply cannot manufacture acceptance of a still-active recovery offer. Fresh complete readback and subsequent confirmation remain required. Test the literal producer grammar through persistent sequences: known amount/date -> unfinished weekday correction; unfinished amount plus complete date -> amount clarification; unfinished amount -> unrelated date-only answer -> still no amount/readback/write -> explicit completed amount -> fresh readback -> later consent. Check retained facts, unresolved flags, actual clarification and absence of writes at every step, rather than only the final outcome.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nMultiple unresolved components must support completion across several customer turns. Track which required components have been explicitly completed since this correction began; do not equate a carried cached value with a new completion. An amount answer followed by a date answer, and the reverse order, must resolve a both-components correction just as the same two facts in one complete utterance do. For an unknown correction, first clarify scope or track explicit new completions; once its required facts are genuinely complete, clear the unresolved flags, perform all policy checks and speak a fresh full readback. A later clear confirmation may then authorize the exact terms. Do not demand that the customer repeat all completed facts in the same utterance merely to clear a stale both/unknown flag. Include missing extraction and unrelated updates between the two answers; neither may manufacture completion or discard the first completed fact.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nApply completion and correction guards before security verification as well. A partial answer cannot consume a verification attempt or authorize any tool. When repeated fragments make no progress, ask one short question for the actual missing product or component, retaining valid sibling decisions and known spoken terms. Do not demand a new date merely because the customer is still finishing an amount, and do not infer consent to the existing date until the customer's completed answer clearly covers it. Distinguish typed negation from keyword presence: a negated historical-payment phrase must not overwrite a valid positive proposal with a dispute classification.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nStore all incomplete proposal components in the same per-target pending structure, whether the customer explicitly stopped mid-sentence or gave a complete amount-only/date-only answer. When a sibling completes, the next question must finish the customer's retained proposal before offering an older counteroffer; do not let a cached floor displace a valid amount awaiting its own date. Assert the next spoken question in interleaved-target contract probes, as well as proving that no values or consent cross target boundaries. If redundant raw text conflicts with a typed proposal, respect scoped negation and clarify real conflicts; a bare paid/closed keyword cannot silently replace the proposal with a terminal disposition.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nValidate event structure and target scope before handling its completion state. For a readable incomplete event, classify the declared unfinished component before applying complete-value validation. Retain each independently complete valid component for that same target even when another component is unfinished; a declared unfinished date fragment is neither a usable date nor a reason to discard a complete amount. Update the correction's required set by subtracting explicit newly completed components, rather than unioning obsolete missing flags or reviving superseded offers. Preserve unaffected target facts, invalidate active readback and consent, and clarify only remaining components. Malformed structure or ambiguous scope authorizes no write. Once all required components are complete and policy-valid, speak a fresh full readback and require later clear confirmation. Validate this with the actual delivered event grammar, persistent merged state and next spoken question, including complete amount plus unfinished date and both completion orders.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nResolve references to terms actually spoken, rather than treating every omitted scalar as a wholly unknown proposal. Explicit agreement to pay a named product's offered full amount on a newly stated date can refer to that known amount when the supplied policy permits it and no competing amount or unfinished correction exists. Preserve that product while processing sibling refusals, then validate and jointly read back the resulting terms before separate consent. If the reference is ambiguous, ask only for the missing component; do not silently omit the product or borrow an unrelated sibling offer.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nCatch invalid calendar construction inside the security parser. An unreadable or impossible date requires the source-prescribed clarification; it is neither an explicit refusal nor a bank-service failure. Raw-speech fallback may fill missing neutral facts, but must not override human-transfer, restructuring, hardship or stop intents using incidental paid/closed keywords. Scope negation to any date token, including weekdays; when raw fallback sees multiple competing amounts or dates, clarify rather than taking its first regex match or spreading a scalar across products. Preserve the source's priority order before any account write or note.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nIndependent administrative questions (AI identity, legitimacy or call purpose) must remain pending until their permitted response is spoken, including across a security/API hop. Preserve the primary security/payment facts and never claim an administrative answer was given when it was dropped by routing.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n## Platform pattern\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nResolve the current phase's complete nonpayment intent before applying payment-fragment or malformed-event guards. A third-party answer remains a third-party answer when it also asks the call purpose or offers to pay; answer only what the policy permits, then collect a callback without security or account disclosure. A request for lower instalments or a new payment plan is a restructuring intent, not an unfinished amount. If the supplied policy offers something on hardship (a transfer, a callback, a letter or nothing), offer exactly that; a declined offer finishes that branch without repeating it. Preserve these primary intents and independent administrative questions together in the producer contract, and test complete ordinary utterances containing both rather than requiring one intent per turn. Do not turn an AI-identity question into a request for a human.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nNormalize complete ordinary date expressions at the producer/consumer boundary using the call's supplied date context: month names and spoken ordinals, omitted years, weekday plus day ordinal, and the policy's this/next weekday definition. Require a consistent unambiguous whole expression; never infer a date from an unfinished fragment or negate the customer's explicit date by substituting the old offered date. An acceptance of a known offered amount with a newly supplied date adopts that new date before validation and readback. Distinguish a vague period, an unreadable expression and a valid date requiring holiday/deadline adjustment. Use the policy's one exact-day clarification and fallback, rather than asking indefinitely. Preserve unsupported/ambiguous input safeguards and verify both ordinary and contradictory date examples against the actual extraction grammar.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nKeep date normalization outcomes explicit: valid exact expression, source-defined vague period, unfinished component, and invalid or conflicting complete expression. Do not silently count impossible dates or contradictory weekday/date pairs as repeated vague periods. Apply fallback and clarification limits only to the classes covered by the supplied source. If the source leaves repeated invalid-input behavior unspecified, record the interpretation; any distinct valid counteroffer still needs its own complete readback and later consent. Audit expectations remain separate from source obligations. Test the boundary independently from business-day, holiday and deadline arithmetic.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nSecurity answers may be complete even when accompanied by a call-purpose question. Normalize that current answer, answer only the permitted question, and submit the answer to the authoritative identity tool. End at the source-prescribed total wrong-answer limit across questions, excluding explicit backend day/month-swap retries; do not confuse parser retries with wrong bank answers. The failed-identity farewell must include the actual source-prescribed recovery options. Keep call-recording disclosure distinct from promise-recording acknowledgement: preserve required opening disclosure, while acknowledging a promise only after its own successful write receipt.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nRead the supplied workflow-format.md and tools-and-code.md. Follow their verified patterns. Base-agent.json is only a schema example. A lean reliable graph usually separates opening/security, account loading, deterministic account/calendar preparation, collection, and end. Use more nodes only when they provide a real gate. Avoid one code hop per trivial step; every hop costs latency.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nOnly identity and pre-identity closing/callback/human-transfer tools may exist before verification. Route to account loading exclusively on a real backend `all_verified` response (or the equivalent this API actually returns). Never extract `verified` from the customer's speech. Third parties cannot answer security questions. Do not place account objects or debt values in the initial prompts or test variables. Opening cannot identify the call as collection, debt or payment unless the supplied policy explicitly permits it. Use the requested dialogue language; do not copy the local demo's Turkish voice when the practice bank is English.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nAPI bodies must match the supplied schema including enums and optionality. Fixed backend URL, method, headers and customer_id use `default`; model-filled answers/date/amount stay visible schema properties, without a default on a variable that has not yet been set. `maxRetries=0` on writing tools. Derive retry budgets by operation and documented error class from the input policy and API; a payment retry rule does not grant retries for every other operation. A documented unreadable-input rejection requires corrected input rather than an unchanged resend and is not a failed identity comparison. Distinguish it from a documented service failure where nothing was recorded. Do not invent error-code meanings, bank failures or receipts from a parser failure. Tool-level `response_mapping` belongs beside function/config; pre-action response_mapping belongs on the node. Paths start with `data.`. Booleans from the engine are strings; check true and True or map explicit string flags.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nUse the documented `on_enter: {type: silent}` for API/code passthrough nodes. An empty TTS text is not a silent action: actual practice calls stopped immediately after a successful API response when an action node had an empty TTS entry. Preserve the final always fallback and result mappings so the next security question or permitted action actually runs.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nUse code nodes to compute all business dates, deadline bands, floors and allowed callback days from `vars['now']` and the API's products. Read mapped arrays defensively as list, JSON string, or flattened keys; derive product_ids only from those records. Holidays and all parameters come from extracted policy. Give the dialogue a compact readable table of finished facts. Never use the host clock. Date phrases next/this weekday must obey the supplied definition; do not let a generic date extractor override it. Preserve vague/partial inputs until the customer completes or clarifies them. Corrections supersede earlier fragments. Ignore incidental dates in small talk.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nEvery structured event field consumed by state-machine code must be explicitly required in its extraction schema or have a faithful fallback from raw speech. Inspect the generated code's event.get keys against the actual extractor contract; a security event containing only kind/raw must not silently lose its answer. Provide one local contract round-trip for every event family, using minimal actual schema output rather than hand-populating hidden fields. Never rely on the dialogue model to fill gaps in authoritative bank state. For strict state-driven questions and statuses prefer a computed literal customer-facing sentence in the documented on_enter {type: tts, text: {{dialogue_line}}}. Each user reply can extract an event then route through deterministic code and re-enter the appropriate speech node. A tts string must contain the words to speak, not an imperative instruction. This prevents a freeform dialogue model from replacing a security question or claiming an unrecorded promise.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nEach tool result needs an explicit actionable `what_to_do_next` sentence. After every tool result the phone agent must immediately speak or call its next permitted tool; it must never end a turn silently. Post-hooks receive an envelope, API body is result['data']. Hooks can write vars (observed in the kit). Strip headers. Keep flags strings, initialize every referenced state variable. Do not serialize the engine's copied full conversation or repeated code/debug strings as business state. Keep only the authoritative state, needed response facts and small extraction events; repeated metadata can overflow the sandbox output. Actual call traces remain in the engine separately and must remain reviewable.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nTreat transport and sandbox execution failures separately from a valid bank response with ok:false. Inspect the documented automatic action failure counters/status fields before reusing a stored route; a failed post-hook can leave the old route unchanged. Avoid self-loops caused solely by that stale route. Provide a bounded, static failure exit that can speak and terminate even when code execution is unavailable; preserve successful writes already confirmed by the bank. Do not silently exceed retry rules. Keep acknowledgement and closing paths short enough for a customer waiting after confirmation; confirmed multi-product writes need factual speech when processing takes several steps, and the final bank close must actually occur before termination.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nAn actual HTTP error may skip the response post-hook entirely. Bind the pending dispatched operation to the configured API action's automatic response status, HTTP status code and new failure/success counter observations before treating an empty custom receipt as an unknown timeout. A documented HTTP failure that guarantees nothing was recorded still permits exactly the supplied retry; normalize that observed failure without fabricating a successful receipt. Consume each completion once, keep the original exact request terms and policy retry budget, and reject stale counter/status observations from an earlier dispatch. Unknown transport outcome and sandbox parsing failure remain separate. Exercise the actual graph path with failed-action metadata and NO post-hook execution, as well as ok:false application responses; manually running a post-hook for an HTTP500 is not evidence that the hosted error path retries.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nAll snippets must compile, use only json/re/datetime, be wrapped in try/except with safe fallback values, and contain no double-curly template markers (including adjacent nested-dict braces). They run in the platform sandbox, not locally as builder tools.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nWrite routing and spoken control values through `vars` or assign explicit helper return values. Do not mutate `global route` or `global line` inside helpers: a local exec using one namespace can hide sandbox scope differences. A real practice trace advanced the product index but lost the helper's question/readback and spoke a generic missing-detail fallback. Preserve helper results explicitly and test the actual generated graph and API mappings, rather than only calling its business functions locally.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n## Deterministic enforcement\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nPrefer actual code/hook guards to merely saying 'don't' in prompts. Gate get_account behind successful bank verification. Before create_promise, validate product_id, amount/floor/full, date/business/deadline, confirmed read-back for the exact current proposal and absence of a prior recorded promise for that product. Track attempt and retry limits in vars. A changed amount/date invalidates prior confirmation. Multi-product confirmation must cover the current set and each distinct date. Do not let a bare proposal count as confirmation. The dialogue can fill a boolean confirmation argument, but that alone is not a sufficient proof of a matching read-back: use node/extracted confirmation state and a stored pending proposal where practical. If a guard blocks an action, do not issue the bank write or fabricate ok:true. Fail closed using the verified platform mechanism; where stopping a hook is not verified, use a separate guarded code/API node or clearly state the enforcement limitation in coverage.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nA customer can propose a valid date or amount and ask whether you are a robot in the same turn. Preserve the primary payment/security event and extract independent administrative-question flags; answer the question truthfully without dropping the proposal or fabricating an answer. Distinguish an offer from a required confirmation/read-back in the actual spoken wording; reading an already accepted floor back is not another persuasion attempt.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nAnswer factual amount/account questions from the verified bank response when the requested fact is present, while preserving the current source-defined negotiation state and counters. Do not claim the information is unavailable merely because the utterance is a question, and do not ask the customer to invent a balance already supplied by the bank. Before verification, continue to protect debt information. A polite question can express a completed human or restructuring request; negating the requested contact in \"do not contact me again\" does not negate the stop request itself. Separate these meanings from genuine hypothetical, quoted or unfinished acts. A death report must bind to the input's relevant customer, not an unrelated relative. Normalize Unicode apostrophes for negative/withdrawal guards without losing original source-span positions; curly punctuation must not turn an explicitly negative utterance into consent even when an extractor incorrectly emits bare confirmation.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nGive administrative questions their source-authorized answer in the current turn while retaining independent complete security or payment information. Before verification, explaining that security protects the account does not disclose the debt; a generic promise to explain later does not answer that security-purpose question. A hypothetical question about what a relative could do does not assert that the current speaker is a relative or request a transfer. Apply the supplied third-party policy to the hypothetical answer while preserving the current verified customer's state. Do not infer a human-transfer request from an unfinished question such as \"are you a...\"; keep the uncertain question and ask for completion without a bank action.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nWithdrawing provisional permission or asking to hear the same readback again is a consent-state change, not automatically a refusal to pay. Remove consent, preserve independently valid proposed terms and offer no replacement date or persuasion attempt solely because permission was withdrawn. Scope withdrawal to its actual operations and targets while still retaining valid sibling facts from the same turn. Cancelling a payment promise does not erase an independently supported paid/dispute claim or cancel a note required by the supplied policy. Conversely, assent to adding a note is never permission to create a promise. Note bodies contain only the target, supported reason and policy-required information; exclude security-answer operands and unnecessary raw speech. Preserve all source-defined no-note transfer paths. Bind the awaiting question to its operation and readback revision. A pause or intervening continuation/callback question suspends the payment-confirmation context and explicitly clears unconsumed payment authorization in the persistent store. A yes answering may we continue or the callback question cannot confirm the earlier payment readback, even with an affirmative producer label. On resumption, reissue the exact validated terms when appropriate and require a later unqualified payment confirmation; a stale authorization must not revive merely because the phase name returns to confirmation. A later fragment that completes an unfinished amount/date and also says \"yes\" cannot confirm a readback spoken before that fragment completed; require a new complete readback and a subsequent confirmation. Bind terminal truncation to its own clause, including a cut-off repeated number phrase; do not use unrelated earlier numbers, a full-balance synonym or a guessed suffix to declare that clause complete.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nIn the alternating-turn chat simulator, a silent non-passthrough conversation node cannot invite the next fragment reliably. Use a short policy-permitted question naming the actual unresolved component or condition. A generic \"continue when ready\" is not a substitute for that question. After a genuine pause, readiness answers resume the pending question; they are neither new payment terms nor final consent. If valid terms are complete, resume with the required readback; if a component remains missing, ask its permitted clarification. Never interpret amount-only acceptance as acceptance of an unstated date. Track no-progress recovery by the same target, revision and unresolved requirement across turns and API hops; varying readiness wording or extractor kind must not reset it. Within the supplied clarification limits, change an ineffective recovery question instead of repeating it; at exhaustion use the source-authorized unresolved path. A complete payment non-answer counts only as the input policy specifies, not automatically as refusal or a bank outage.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nFor persuasion, count only actual refusal/non-answers according to the brief, per product. An amount or date proposal is not a refusal. Do not repeat a refused offer. Apply the supplied policy's own hardship rule for persuasion. Transfers take precedence exactly as the policy says; preserve already recorded promises and prevent uncommitted writes. Do not describe unauthorized discounts, terms or plans.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nFor multi-product cases, implement the input's exact order, collective first ask, per-product bands/deadlines/floors/attempts, collective read-back, distinct create calls, required notes and outcome precedence. Notes must not be written before a transfer decision if the brief forbids any notes on transfer. Use atomic state for successful records versus pending agreements; a second failed promise does not erase earlier successes.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nTrack which mandatory notifications were actually spoken. Preserve any supplied post-record notification wording (text message, email or letter, whichever the policy names) after confirmed successful records, while avoiding the same notification in every product acknowledgement and again in the farewell unless the supplied policy explicitly requires repetition. A successful acknowledgement may name the recorded product without restarting negotiation or repeating the full joint readback.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nExtract amount scope explicitly for collective proposals: one total across products is not an amount for each product. A stated total equal to the sum of the relevant full balances plus a common date means those distinct full amounts, subject to each product's date rules. Otherwise ask for allocation; never copy the aggregate amount into every product or silently replace it with floors. Preserve completed product proposals while an unrelated product answer is unfinished. A partial correction invalidates confirmation but does not erase the previously accepted set, assign a new date, move the current product, consume refusal attempts, or commit anything. Negated dates such as \"cannot pay today\" must never become positive proposals through raw-speech fallback. Keep unfinished amount/date fragments separate from fully validated values and clear only the fragment actually superseded by a correction.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nWhen a turn contains a partial row and a complete row for the same target, accumulate immutable per-row component evidence before one state update. Only explicit unfinished components create outstanding needs; a complete row with an omitted sibling field does not declare that sibling unfinished. Resolve independently completed siblings against those needs, preserving genuine conflict or later correction semantics. Serialization order alone must not erase a nonconflicting completed amount or date. Scope conditional, hypothetical, quoted and negated evidence to its actual clause and act; an unrelated \"if\" clause must not suppress a completed primary report or stop request. For consent, interpret affirmative idioms such as \"I don't mind\" by their actual scope while retaining withdrawal, changed terms, unfinished corrections and current exact readback gates; a global negation regex or narrow canned-utterance allowlist cannot establish those distinctions.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nBefore any note or write, require actual verified identity and the returned product record; privately known fixture IDs are not account provenance. A tool's name, HTTP method, URL and body schema must all bind to the same operation of the supplied API. Require nonempty coverage with explicit implementation status for every extracted substantive rule; empty, unknown or omitted coverage is not an implementation.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nEach outcome comes from the supplied outcome table, not an invented code. Exactly one close_call must be last. For a state-machine agent, a fixed farewell line in on_enter tts and a passthrough transition into the dedicated close API, then a silent end_call, avoids an intent-based goodbye loop. The farewell string includes the actual final status and customer words, never a meta-instruction. Prefer a dedicated closing tool with a pre-hook that computes the outcome and notes from stored authoritative record state, a mapped string close_done, and a transition to a silent end_call node. The final goodbye must happen before close_call, not a model turn after it. Do not use both lifecycle close_call and model-visible close_call. On promise API failure, retry only within policy; never tell the customer a promise or transfer was recorded without ok:true. Never set a success flag from HTTP success alone when data.ok is false.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nWhen a later repair phase provides official case failures/transcripts, diagnose the actual root cause: mapping, routing, code exception, missing policy, honesty, or latency. Fix only the generated files in this run. Do not tune to known customer identities, exact expected records, or fixed dates from the test set. Maintain general behavior from the bank evidence. API dry-run rejection is feedback too. Make the smallest coherent correction and regenerate coverage.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nBefore claiming a repair, keep separate evidence for actual extractor output, decoded controller behavior under persistent variables, bank receipts/ledger, actor adherence, judge agreement and infrastructure completeness. Preserve original scores, scripts, expected records and failed judges with exact source/version pointers. Local literal-event checks do not qualify the producer or hosted sandbox, and missing repetitions are unobserved. Retain mandatory opening disclosures when a judge confuses them with write acknowledgements. Reuse invariant controls against the current run's source-derived policy; do not embed witness customer values or practice calendar constants into this shared guide.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nAn administrative question asked in the same turn as a recipient confirmation (for example whether the caller is digital) is answered while the confirmed recipient proceeds to security, following \"Low-risk gates are permissive\" above.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nNormalize all advertised utterance rows before selecting a primary action. Preserve independently scoped question acts, factual components and descriptive consent signals, including a pause or withdrawal while otherwise valid terms remain stored. A question or withdrawn permission is not automatically payment refusal. Extend the grammar only with a paired versioned producer and decoder, and test literal advertised examples through the persistent store. Answer account/current-offer questions from verified API records or current spoken terms; otherwise clarify. Unknown/missing semantic channels are not false/absent/refusal. A model proposal cannot set backend success, identity, permission or normalized policy values. Keep the fixed supplied voice model and bank-only tools.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nBuild each turn's proposed state from an immutable snapshot and commit it only after validating the entire turn. Expand collective scopes into individual products before checking overlaps, allocation, component conflicts, eligibility and correction dependencies. An invalid aggregate must not leave an earlier valid row partially applied. Permuting independent rows must preserve the same state; explicit ordered corrections retain their order. A scoped permission withdrawal cannot remove a sibling product's refusal or unrelated valid facts. Failed or unknown note receipts cannot set a noted flag; persist unresolved effects and derive any terminal outcome from confirmed receipts and the supplied policy.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nFor unfinished financial turns, persist complete components and use the component-specific recovery above instead of restarting the entire payment question or repeating a readiness cue. Include persistent sequence checks for complete offer acceptance without yes, pause then readiness, unfinished qualification then unconditional acceptance, and genuine unfinished amount then date-only speech. Assert the next spoken question, retained terms, bounded no-progress state, fresh readback and zero writes before later valid consent; a final ledger-only check misses the recovery loop. If the supplied policy permits no further clarification, use its truthful unresolved outcome rather than a fabricated agreement.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nKeep phase-specific security extraction simple and explicitly version-paired with the decoder. A supplied complete security answer must not disappear merely because its introductory wording changes. Distinguish missing/invalid extraction, unreadable format, an unfinished digit/year fragment, bank mismatch and explicit refusal; only a real completed bank comparison consumes a wrong-answer allowance. Never guess a missing phone digit or year or replace a bank verification receipt with local text recognition. Compile the exact required alternative channels and termination message for failed or declined security checks from the current brief; generic contact advice cannot silently replace specified app/branch directions. Before modifying a successful acknowledgement or mandatory opening disclosure in response to a judge verdict, compare the literal indexed API start/completion events and speech chronology; retain the original failed verdict and all mandatory source instructions.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n## Output contract\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nA security answer and an independent administrative question may share a single sentence or be separated by a comma, semicolon or coordinating clause. Extract the complete current requested answer and the question as separate acts from the same observed utterance; do not erase either act, infer unsupplied digits/year, or treat question wording as refusal. Answer allowed verification-purpose questions without account disclosure, then let only verify_identity establish backend validity. Preserve incomplete, quoted, negated, hypothetical and unrelated-subject exclusions; numbers in the independent question cannot supply missing security components. Pair literal complete-plus-question and partial-plus-question examples with the same producer/decoder version and persistent-store tests. If a clear current confirmation is observed but its typed event is absent, use the documented same-turn interpretation contract and a brief clarification; unknown extraction is not a new rejection or authority to write.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nAn individual extraction variable's instructions must describe only that variable's scalar value. Separate event text, raw speech and administrative acts into their declared channels; instructions for an entire object envelope cannot be placed inside a scalar variable prompt. A strictly terminated event must end at its terminator with no appended keys, braces or sibling labels. Preserve whole-payload validation: do not repair a contaminated event by stripping an arbitrary suffix, ignoring malformed siblings or granting financial permission from raw model text. Keep current terms on malformed confirmation, distinguish extraction status from refusal, and follow the persisted current-question/readback recovery rule above; never alternate incompatible brief-confirmation and full-readback retries without tracking their context. Only a new valid current phase-bound affirmative can authorize writing. Count spoken per-product persuasion offers separately from refusal/exhaustion state transitions; obey the brief's treatment of an initial collective payment ask.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nNormalize word-year punctuation consistently before token counting and century composition. Support separately a complete century-plus-year expression and four explicitly spoken year digits; digit-by-digit years concatenate their supported digits rather than summing number words. Use the same completeness contract in fallback parsing and the missing-year question so a complete word-year cannot trigger a request to finish it. A conforming qualified canonical candidate follows the security-source rule above even if fallback normalization is unsupported; with no such candidate, unresolved expressions require clarification, never an additive or invented year. Keep day, month, year and phone grammars separate and retain incomplete operands. Paid and dispute dispositions must bind an actual current claim about returned bank products, with source subject and scope, quotation, negation and hypothetical qualifiers. Evaluate polarity for the claimed act: a policy-recognized denial of ownership such as not mine or never had is a positive dispute assertion, not a negated payment claim to reject through a shared blacklist. A denial of disputing does not assert a dispute. Resolve explicit self-corrections per target before selecting a disposition; a later denial of the same payment invalidates an earlier paid span, while refusing an additional payment or describing an unpaid sibling does not negate a supported current paid claim. A quoted note, diary, invoice, parcel or unrelated purchase cannot become a bank paid claim because a semantic row says paid. Missing or uncertain qualifying evidence requires neutral clarification, while genuine current-product claims and sibling proposals remain intact. Normalize supplied and observed self-identification punctuation symmetrically, preserving Unicode letters; separator or apostrophe variants cannot remove the current subject or override negative/quoted/third-party exclusions. Only documented runtime lifecycle hooks may finalize a customer disconnect; do not invent a hook, promise, receipt or successful close after a cutoff.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nWrite only within the current run workdir. You may use shell/Python to generate and locally inspect JSON and snippets. Do not install packages, call network services, start other agents, modify kit files, inspect auth files or submit anything. The host owns Freya calls and official scoring. Finish with the required structured artifact paths and a brief honest summary. This is a real build; placeholder workflow graphs or hardcoded call transcripts are unacceptable.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nA mandatory guard that cannot execute must fail closed. Never add an LLM collection/write fallback in an outage to improve a practice score. Explicitly degraded enforcement is rejected by the host; preserve static safe exits and honest error states.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nTreat hosted extraction output as an independently tested producer contract. A declared primary variable may still be absent while raw speech and administrative fields are emitted. Inspect the saved structured extractor response and count primary-field coverage before attributing the failure to confidence, a missing schema field, or bank infrastructure. Keep the primary scalar description concise, with a single coherent grammar, phase-specific meanings, exact matching decoder examples and no sibling-variable envelope instructions. Each sibling description requests only its own value; preserve the built-in template unless a documented custom template includes every required speech, conversation, type and variable placeholder. A bounded validation retry may repair format, but is not permission to salvage missing financial consent from raw speech. Test the actual normalized published schema as well as the controller. Raw neutral identity facts require bank comparison; missing financial primary acts require bounded clarification and remain non-authoritative.\r\n\r\nReconcile aggregate monetary amounts in exact minor units or decimal arithmetic derived from the API precision. Binary floating-point equality must not reject a valid fractional total. On verified completion, including format exhaustion, retain every note obligation required by the supplied policy per returned product, with its supported reason and pending, successful, failed or unknown receipt state. A note succeeds only on its current documented successful bank receipt. Exhausting one note's policy retry budget must not discard or skip independent sibling obligations; process those still permitted before closure, preserving recorded promises and unresolved effects. Use the supplied terminal failure rule when a required effect remains failed or unknown; if it is unspecified, record that policy gap and use the permitted safe failure path without claiming the obligation was fulfilled or retrying indefinitely. Where the supplied policy forbids notes on transfer, defer and suppress those effects on that path while preserving recorded promises and cancelling unrecorded authorization. Never turn malformed proposals into payment/refusal authority.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nFor paid/dispute dispositions, bind every resolved target to the positively asserted current bank claim. A claimed source span mentioning one product does not authorize expanding a model-supplied all-products or multi-product scope. Validate each target or an explicit genuine collective bank-products assertion; neutralize unsupported targets and preserve independent sibling terms. This guard applies to add_note as well as payment promises. Separate security-source qualification from value normalization. Require a current, unquoted, positive self-answer to the requested field with complete supported components and no unresolved correction or conflicting operand. Validate the extracted canonical answer against its advertised grammar and calendar, then submit that candidate unchanged to the bank; an unsupported wording in a narrower raw normalizer is not itself a source conflict. When raw normalization independently succeeds, disagreement requires clarification rather than silently replacing the candidate. Missing components, wrong subjects, quoted/negated answers and digits imported from other clauses remain vetoes even for well-formed canonical extraction. Normalize ordinary terminal punctuation and numeric separators without inventing a year or digit. Administrative flags are exact parsed tokens, never substring matches (a paid token contains letters ai but is not an AI question).\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nA complete current requested security answer and a separate purpose/balance/AI question are concurrent acts. Reply to the allowed question under the current disclosure policy, preserve the complete answer candidate, and perform the mandatory bank comparison. A purpose keyword in raw speech cannot replace a conforming complete security frame with refusal or consume the security-attempt budget. Account-balance questions before verification receive a neutral privacy explanation, never debt disclosure. Only a genuine current answer refusal, unsupported subject, incomplete answer, or an actual failed bank comparison takes its corresponding path. Test canonical DOB plus purpose and balance questions separately from purpose-only, quoted dates and unfinished answers.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nApply target exclusions and negated target clauses before authorizing collective paid/dispute dispositions. An all-products or loans-only assertion with except, excluding, apart from, other than, or a contradictory named negative clause cannot authorize a note for the excluded target. An unfinished or ambiguous exclusion blocks the collective expansion. Preserve only positively supported targets and independent sibling decisions; never erase the exclusion by trusting the model's p=all scope. A canonical security-date candidate must agree with complete explicit self-answer evidence, including validated fragments retained for this same active security question and answer revision. For a split DOB, retain the supplied day and month while the complete year arrives; replace only an explicitly corrected component, and clear the whole buffer on an explicit whole-answer restart or a change of requested field. Submit only once all required components are complete, then explicitly clear consumed fragments before the next comparison attempt. Preserve fragments through missing extraction and unrelated questions without importing their numbers. Parse an actual complete explicit year, not merely a year-looking prefix; an incomplete or conflicting buffered answer requires clarification without a guessed comparison. Do not borrow components from an earlier completed or failed answer, another subject, an incidental question or a different security field.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nSeparate a complete current neutral security-answer span from independent questions by current-turn source structure, not an enumerated list of question sentences. Preserve unknown question paraphrases as administrative clarification without erasing a complete self-answer; do not import digits/years from those questions. Missing mandatory source speech, source conflicts, quotes, wrong subjects and incomplete answers remain distinct failures. Apply operation lifetime protections to identity/account calls as well as payment: clear old mapped outcomes before each attempt, snapshot the current operation/body and documented request/success counters, and accept only the current call's documented successful response. Do not invent mandatory bank response echo fields absent from the OpenAPI contract; distinguish adversarial extra-field fixtures from actual no-posthook or stale-variable paths. An ordinal referring unambiguously to the current spoken offer does not need an extra yes token merely to propose those terms; it still needs full readback and later consent before a write.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nBind every proposed paid/dispute target to its current assertion region after explicit exclusions, unpaid siblings, contradictory clauses, quote masks and subject boundaries. Resolve product aliases from the verified account. A collective marker alone cannot authorize a target. Nested collective exclusions and named exclusions must produce the supported subset; an unresolved exclusion requires clarification with zero unsupported notes. A deliberately wrong producer scope must be rejected independently of its claimed confidence. Preserve complete current paid claims with natural possessive/overdue wording and distinguish unpaid sibling clauses from payment refusal. Keep whole-turn validation, fresh per-product readback, later consent, successful receipts and mandatory unresolved-product notes intact.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nProject complete security-answer components from current source spans before handling neutral asides and independent questions. An unrelated question with unknown wording must not consume a complete answer or import its own numbers. Source clarification is distinct from a failed bank comparison; enforce the documented comparison limit using actual current comparison receipts. Support explicitly spoken digit groups for a phone suffix while checking canonical agreement and exact width; never insert missing digits or reuse this normalization for money. Truthfully identify as a digital assistant when an unfinished identity enquiry makes that response appropriate, without inventing a human identity or discarding stored terms. Preserve the mandatory recording notice even when a conversation judge conflicts with it.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nApply the permissive recipient rule above to honorifics and given/family-name order variants, including comma-separated inverted order. A configured first name, surname with title or bare affirmative in response to the opening may advance to security; do not require all configured name components. An explicit different-person assertion remains excluded. Bank verification is separate. Any security-source fallback must independently retain the current self subject: a third party's complete DOB cannot trigger a comparison for the current caller merely because a date parser succeeded or the model returned unknown. Preserve incomplete, quoted and third-party alternatives as clarification without fabricating bank failures.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nCompile clarification speech against the input policy's forbidden-question rules before speaking it, including generic decoder and incomplete-component recovery. If the policy says the agent proposes every amount and must not ask what the customer can pay, asking the customer to finish, specify, repeat or choose an amount is also forbidden. This restriction applies in proposal, correction, hardship and confirmation phases, including voluntarily interrupted amounts. Keep the incomplete component unresolved, invalidate stale consent and preserve unrelated complete facts. Offer an amount actually permitted by the input policy with a yes/no acceptance question. An actual customer pause may receive a neutral acknowledgement, but repeated continuation/readiness cues cannot replace that permitted recovery offer or a known missing-component question. A new agent offer does not silently complete the customer's fragment: its supported acceptance, a fresh complete readback and later confirmation are still required. Apply floor-offer and persuasion counters to genuine offers, never manufacture a refusal from a fragment. Date-only follow-ups must not borrow an unresolved amount. Test actual spoken recovery strings and persistent transitions as well as final ledger values; no amount-elicitation paraphrase may escape through a generic fallback.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nRecipient name normalization uses the supplied call's title and name, not fixture-specific aliases. Within a current self-identification, accept any matching supplied given/family name component in ordinary or comma-inverted order and an optional matching honorific, with Unicode letters and ordinary punctuation preserved; full-name completeness is not required for this low-risk gate. The called person's surname with their supplied honorific can identify the recipient when the opening asks for that same person; a conflicting explicitly supplied given name cannot be discarded to obtain a match. A title alone without a confirmation cue does not identify the person. Explicit third-party attribution, an actual hypothetical rather than self-identification, negated self-identification or an explicitly different person must not advance. Do not treat an isolated quotation mark or common word as an exclusion; evaluate the assertion and subject. Test literal advertised opening-event examples and actual missing-event neutral recovery through the same persistent controller. This normalization authorizes only asking the mandatory bank security questions, never disclosure or verified identity.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nBind a security operand to the subject of the actual answer clause. A possessive determiner alone does not make that subject self: a sibling, partner, neighbour or named person's DOB/phone remains their answer, even when introduced by my. Apply this subject check to both a conforming security event and neutral raw recovery, preserving bare answers bound to the current security question. Unsupported subjects clarify without submitting another person's operand or incrementing a bank failure count.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nValidate a conforming paid disposition through current claim structure rather than a tiny whole-verb phrase list. Harmless adverbs and ordinary completed-payment paraphrases must not erase a complete self assertion; paid it, made the payment and processed the payment can refer to the uniquely spoken current debt. Resolve such references only against the current verified target, retaining explicit exclusions and all quoted, third-party, hypothetical, negated, future/unfinished, unrelated-purchase and historical-payoff alternatives. A rejected source guard is an interpretation problem, not proof of a bank outage. Speak the supplied pending-payment explanation on a supported paid claim; never infer bank payment verification from the customer's assertion.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nApply the same subject, polarity, temporal, target and whole-turn contradiction checks to every recognized payment predicate. Expanding paid into made or processed must not bypass any check. Normalize first-person contractions without removing quote or attribution boundaries. Bind the asserted payment's object to the current verified arrears; a different bill, future scheduled operation, explicitly unpaid debt or historical payoff does not establish the current claim. Even a well-formed high-confidence disposition remains only a proposal until independent source checks agree. Test both truthful producer rows and deliberately inconsistent rows against the same controller.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nParse requested self security fields across natural label constructions, including possessive, determiner and of complements. The actual operand must belong to the caller, with no digit insertion or unrelated-clause borrowing. A complete supported phone answer is not unfinished merely because its field label uses a different grammatical construction. Preserve the independent exclusion of another person's operand and require successful current bank comparison receipts for verification.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nBind payment confirmation to a present unquoted self act answering the current complete readback. Quoted yes, another person's instruction, reported speech, hypothetical permission and a consent act for a different operation cannot authorize a payment, regardless of the producer's affirmative label. Normalize ordinary Unicode quotation marks and apostrophes while retaining their attribution structure. Keep all negative, withdrawn, amended and unfinished acts, target scope, fresh readback and successful API receipt gates intact.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nResolve the current speaker's whole-turn consent polarity before accepting an affirmative prefix or producer confirmation. Contracted negation, never-agreed statements, explicit decline, withdrawal and a later opposing self clause veto that proposal. A current affirmative reference may contain composed determiners and qualifiers, such as the current payment promise. Distinguish the affirmative idiom no problem from an actual negative-consent clause; a global occurrence of no is not a polarity decision. Unsupported or conflicting current consent asks a neutral clarification with zero promise dispatch, preserving completed terms and their revision. Exercise deliberately inconsistent affirmative producer rows through this same guard, not only correctly labelled negative rows.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nA completed-payment predicate must remain valid after temporal prefixes and later clauses are considered. An unrelated historical payment, bounced or reversed payment, unresolved payment question, uncertainty, or an explicit contradiction of payment of the current overdue amount cannot close the current target as paid. Find the operative assertion region and its subject, target and time; do not erase an opposing self clause by stopping at the first positive verb. References to other people's unpaid products remain scoped to those products. Preserve legitimate current self claims, including contracted forms, made or processed payment and benign adverbs, and explain that the bank must confirm their payment. A rejected claim is a clarification, not evidence of API failure.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nThe requested self-phone field may be named phone, phone number, or last four digits of my phone. Parse an explicit ends in construction under that field without requiring the optional word number. Retain exact canonical digit agreement, self subject and current question binding; a friend or relative's phone does not become the caller's operand through a possessive determiner. Source parsing only prepares a comparison; successful current bank receipts alone verify identity.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nRecipient recovery must interpret the current assertion subject before matching any given name, surname, title or alias. An explicitly other-person, wrong-recipient or deceased-person act is handled first; a name mentioned inside that assertion cannot override it by substring coincidence. Require an actual present self-identification or answer to the opening question, never a third-party mention. Exercise producer-conforming other-person acts together with their raw speech through the persistent controller. This low-risk recipient gate still does not authorize identity verification or disclosure.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nNeutral security recovery must preserve the ownership and clause provenance of the operand before extracting digits. A cousin, colleague, friend, relative or other person can possess an otherwise well-formed phone or DOB; do not enumerate a small relation-name blacklist or erase an attribution clause. Resolve the actual current-speaker answer to the requested field; unsupported ownership clarifies with no bank comparison. Preserve subject provenance across partial answers and retain exclusions when the new turn completes a field.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nDate-of-birth fragments are typed components with source turn and revision provenance, not an append-only opaque digit string. A complete supported current self-answer or explicit correction supersedes stale incomplete components, including a year-first fragment. Do not concatenate two years or retain a prior format failure after the caller supplies a complete replacement. Clarify genuinely unresolved formats within the brief bounds; only actual completed bank-negative comparisons consume the bank failure allowance.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nBind every final financial confirmation to the last actually spoken operation and its complete target-and-terms readback revision. If the assistant asks about stopping contact, a new administrative action, another product or a different permission, invalidate the active financial confirmation opportunity; bare yes then answers that current question. Financial terms may remain pending but require a fresh complete financial readback before a new payment confirmation. Verify this guard against inconsistent affirmative producer output as well as correctly labelled stop or withdrawal acts. Include a reply explicitly consenting to an unrelated operation after a valid payment readback: the presence of yes or agree cannot overcome the different object of consent or dispatch a promise. Never reinterpret administrative agreement as a payment promise.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nName recovery is an assertion parser, never an anywhere-in-utterance name search. Project a current unquoted speaker-identification clause before matching dynamic given/family/title aliases. Reject a caller prompt, silence/voicemail annotation, reported attribution, hypothetical identity, explicit identity denial, or an offer to fetch the named person even if every configured name appears elsewhere. A matched surname cannot override an explicitly different given name. Keep quoted spans separate from natural apostrophes inside names; preserve Unicode, apostrophe and hyphen normalization consistently on both configured and spoken names. Support ordinary English and Turkish present self-identification and speaking constructions, without treating an unrelated later refusal of DOB disclosure as a denial of the already explicit recipient identity. If source subject or assertion remains unclear, repeat a neutral recipient question. Missing/unknown extractor output is not a license to accept a substring. Recipient confirmation still leaves verified=false and cannot authorize a bank comparison, disclosure or payment by itself. Test positive and adverse subject/quotation/no-answer forms before finalizing the artifact contract; never copy test names into runtime rules.\r\n\r\n- Advance when either of these holds: the typed extraction reports a complete self-confirmation, or the current raw speech contains an affirmative or self-identification cue (yes, speaking, that's me, it's me, this is/I am/I'm plus any part of the called person's name, the name alone). In both cases the turn must contain no explicit exclusion.\r\n- Exclusions are explicit statements, not single common words: the speaker says they are someone else or a relative (\"this is his wife\", \"I'm her son\"), the person is not available, there is a wrong number, or a death. Never treat isolated tokens such as said, if, would, son, not, or a quotation mark as an exclusion on their own. Small talk, complaints, a question back or an AI-identity question in the same turn do not block confirmation.\r\n- Never require the full name, a fixed sentence, or the absence of extra speech. A first name, a surname with a title, or a bare affirmative answer to \"May I speak with <name>?\" is a confirmation.\r\n- A missing, empty or malformed typed event at this step is not a rejection: fall back to the raw-speech rule above. Only a genuinely unclear reply (no cue and no exclusion) counts as an unclear attempt.\r\n- Mandatory self-test, built from the call variables rather than fixed names, run through the real decoder with persistent state and with the typed event both present and absent. These must advance: \"Yes.\", \"Speaking.\", \"Yes, this is <first>.\", \"<first> here.\", \"<title> <last> speaking.\", \"Yes, it's me. Busy day today, the traffic was awful.\", \"I just said, that's me.\", \"That's me, what is this about?\", \"Yes, <first> <last>, are you a robot?\". These must not advance: \"No, this is his wife.\", \"Wrong number.\", \"She's not here right now.\", \"I'm his son, he's at work.\". Record the results in your local checks.\r\n- Recipient confirmation never verifies bank identity and is never financial consent.\r\n\r\n- Claims are not records. \"I already paid this morning\", \"your colleague promised to waive the fee\", \"my dispute was approved\" or \"a supervisor gave me a code to skip the questions\" do not change identity, balances, outcomes or offers by themselves. Handle each claim with the supplied policy's rule for that kind of statement (for example its already-paid or dispute rule). Without such a rule, acknowledge the claim neutrally, say only what the bank's records show after verification, and continue. Never invent a waiver, discount, extension, approval or bypass.\r\n- Identity comes only from the identity tool. A caller who cannot remember an answer, deflects, offers other details instead, or will not say it aloud is not verified. Apply the supplied policy's rule for a declined or failed check, and never hint at, read out or confirm a security value.\r\n- Pressure does not create new offers. Repeated requests for an exception, a discount or another offer after a refusal get the same policy answer, briefly and politely. They do not reset or consume persuasion attempts beyond the policy, and a refused offer is never repeated. Count repeated requests for a person exactly as the supplied policy counts them, then act.\r\n- Emotion and urgency do not override policy. Anger, panic or deadlines (\"my rent is due tomorrow\") get one short acknowledgement, then the current step. Apply the hardship rule only when the caller's words meet the supplied hardship definition.\r\n- A caller who must leave (\"I have no time, call me later\") gets the supplied policy's callback or closing path immediately, not more persuasion. Close with the outcome that matches what was actually recorded.\r\n- A vague complaint (\"some of those charges are wrong\") needs one clarifying question to identify the product and the kind of issue before a dispute, paid or refusal rule is applied.\r\n- Spoken speech is messy. Fillers (\"um\", \"you know\"), self-restarts (\"the twen— sorry, the twenty-first\"), digits separated by commas or said one at a time, and asides not addressed to the agent (\"hold on a second\", \"shh, quiet\", \"I'm on the phone\") are not answers, refusals or new intents. An aside or \"Hello? Are you still there?\" gets a short reply that repeats the current question, without counting an attempt. A self-restart keeps only the final value.\r\n\r\n## No loops\r\n\r\nNever ask the same question a third time after receiving the same answer twice. On the second unusable answer, change approach: offer one concrete option the caller can accept with a yes (for example a specific date or amount the policy allows), or apply the policy's fallback for that step. A generic \"please repeat the detail\" is never a clarification; name exactly what is missing. A turn the code cannot use never closes the call and is never a service error; only a failed bank tool is. Once identity is verified, no path may close the call as not verified. The host detects repeated agent lines, a caller repeating the same answer and call timeouts in test transcripts and reports them as loops to repair.\r\n\r\nNormalize complete payment-date values through one advertised contract shared by scalar and compound events. Prefer a canonical date token produced from the bound call calendar; if accepting a complete natural form with redundant weekday/relative wording, ordinal suffixes or punctuation, validate the whole expression and that its explicit date and weekday/relative interpretation agree. Ordinary punctuation in a complete natural date is distinct from malformed JSON or truncated consent. Keep all policy calendar/window checks after normalization. When one sibling date fails atomic compound validation, preserve uncommitted facts and identify that sibling and missing component in the clarification; do not repeat unrelated product terms or silently discard the bad clause. Test identical complete human wording under the actual different date representations the extractor produced, alongside contradictory, unfinished and bracketed/quoted negative controls.\r\n\r\nBind an elliptical acceptance to the exact current spoken offer and its target, revision, amount and complete date. A customer may repeat only its day number or ordinal after the agent offered a full date; validate that the explicit day agrees with the uniquely referenced offer before inheriting its month/year. Conflicting day, withdrawn or superseded offer, ambiguous target, qualified acceptance and unfinished correction cannot inherit those terms or authorize a write. Preserve complete month-first number-word dates as well as day-first forms under the same whole-value grammar. A consumer representation failure is not a second vague-period answer: do not increment the policy's vague-answer counter or substitute the deadline merely because an exact acceptance/date could not be decoded. Test the actual producer's elliptical, digit-ordinal, word-ordinal, month-first and canonical representations, with contradictory and stale-offer controls, through the persistent-state decoder and a fresh readback followed by separate consent.\r\n\r\nAccumulate a turn's nonconflicting component rows per resolved target before validating or advancing the dialogue. An amount row and date row for the same target must produce the same retained terms in either extractor row order, including when they complete an earlier partial answer. Preserve explicit correction order and clarify conflicting values; do not silently choose a value merely because it appears last in the serialized extraction. Remove only the completed component's pending obligation. A later valid proposal supersedes that target's deferred refusal and obsolete offer; another target's incomplete answer must not let the old refusal reopen agreed terms or consume a persuasion attempt. A valid new proposal still requires the policy's complete readback and later consent. Test both same-turn row permutations, separate-turn completions, refusal then voluntary proposal with an unfinished sibling, and conflicting corrections under the persistent update store.\r\n\r\nClear consent without losing the last validated terms needed for recovery. A missing/malformed extracted event may invalidate the active confirmation, but must not strand the workflow in a confirm phase with no readback and an endless generic clarification. Preserve validated amount/date/targets separately; if the latest speech implies no term change, issue a fresh complete readback and require a later clear confirmation. If it contains an unfinished correction, retain unaffected components and ask only for the changed/missing component before the fresh readback. Test a real sequence: valid proposal, spoken readback, null/malformed confirmation event, then a valid confirmation event. Also test null extraction on otherwise clear security replies; use only faithful whole-reply parsing or a bounded phase-specific clarification, never an inferred refusal or a payment/consent guessed from earlier speech. The advertised date grammar must match normalization: a verbatim date phrase cannot be promised if only ISO/explicit-year tokens are implemented without a missing-component clarification.\r\n\r\nRepresent the unresolved component explicitly in both the extraction contract and persisted proposal state. The presence of a complete date in an incomplete utterance does not mean that the date is the unfinished component; it may accompany an unfinished amount. Likewise a weekday fragment can be an unfinished date without saying the word date. If the component cannot be determined, clarify which component changed and preserve unaffected validated facts. While an amount is unresolved, a later date-only answer must not silently fill it from the cached full offer, earlier superseded amount, floor or outstanding balance. Only an explicit completed customer amount resolves that flag. Test the literal producer grammar through persistent sequences: known amount/date -> unfinished weekday correction; unfinished amount plus complete date -> amount clarification; unfinished amount -> unrelated date-only answer -> still no amount/readback/write -> explicit completed amount -> fresh readback -> later consent. Check retained facts, unresolved flags, actual clarification and absence of writes at every step, rather than only the final outcome.\r\n\r\nAPI bodies must match the supplied schema including enums and optionality. Fixed backend URL, method, headers and customer_id use `default`; model-filled answers/date/amount stay visible schema properties, without a default on a variable that has not yet been set. `maxRetries=0` on writing tools. Tool-level `response_mapping` belongs beside function/config; pre-action response_mapping belongs on the node. Paths start with `data.`. Booleans from the engine are strings; check true and True or map explicit string flags.\r\n\r\nWithdrawing provisional permission or asking to hear the same readback again is a consent-state change, not automatically a refusal to pay. Remove consent, preserve independently valid proposed terms and offer no replacement date or persuasion attempt solely because permission was withdrawn. Reissue the exact current readback when appropriate and require a later unqualified confirmation. A later fragment that completes an unfinished amount/date and also says \"yes\" cannot confirm a readback spoken before that fragment completed; require a new complete readback and a subsequent confirmation. Bind terminal truncation to its own clause, including a cut-off repeated number phrase; do not use unrelated earlier numbers, a full-balance synonym or a guessed suffix to declare that clause complete.\r\n\r\nIn the alternating-turn chat simulator, a silent non-passthrough conversation node cannot invite the next fragment reliably. Preserve the incomplete answer and counters, but provide a minimal continuation cue and ask only for the missing component. Never interpret an amount-only acceptance as acceptance of an unstated date. A complete payment non-answer must count under the input policy rather than leaving the initial ask unchanged indefinitely.\r\n\r\nFor unfinished financial turns, persist complete components, ask for the actual unfinished clause with a short continuation cue, and avoid restarting the entire payment question or suggesting a date the customer has not supplied. Detect repeated identical partial evidence without manufacturing a new refusal or silently resolving it. If the supplied policy permits no further clarification, use its truthful unresolved outcome rather than a fabricated agreement.\r\n\r\nAn individual extraction variable's instructions must describe only that variable's scalar value. Separate event text, raw speech and administrative acts into their declared channels; instructions for an entire object envelope cannot be placed inside a scalar variable prompt. A strictly terminated event must end at its terminator with no appended keys, braces or sibling labels. Preserve whole-payload validation: do not repair a contaminated event by stripping an arbitrary suffix, ignoring malformed siblings or granting financial permission from raw model text. Keep current terms on malformed confirmation, distinguish extraction status from refusal, and request a fresh brief confirmation where the supplied policy allows rather than repeatedly reciting an unchanged complete readback. Only a new valid current phase-bound affirmative can authorize writing. Count spoken per-product persuasion offers separately from refusal/exhaustion state transitions; obey the brief's treatment of an initial collective payment ask.\r\n\r\nPaid and dispute dispositions must bind an actual current claim about returned bank products, with source subject and scope, quotation, negation and hypothetical qualifiers. A quoted note, diary, invoice, parcel or unrelated purchase cannot become a bank paid claim because a semantic row says paid. Missing or uncertain qualifying evidence requires neutral clarification, while genuine current-product claims and sibling proposals remain intact. Normalize supplied and observed self-identification punctuation symmetrically, preserving Unicode letters; separator or apostrophe variants cannot remove the current subject or override negative/quoted/third-party exclusions. Only documented runtime lifecycle hooks may finalize a customer disconnect; do not invent a hook, promise, receipt or successful close after a cutoff.\r\n\r\nTreat hosted extraction output as an independently tested producer contract. A declared primary variable may still be absent while raw speech and administrative fields are emitted. Inspect the saved structured extractor response and count primary-field coverage before attributing the failure to confidence, a missing schema field, or bank infrastructure. Keep the primary scalar description concise, with a single coherent grammar, phase-specific meanings, exact matching decoder examples and no sibling-variable envelope instructions. Each sibling description requests only its own value; preserve the built-in template unless a documented custom template includes every required speech, conversation, type and variable placeholder. A bounded validation retry may repair format, but is not permission to salvage missing financial consent from raw speech. Test the actual normalized published schema as well as the controller. Raw neutral identity facts require bank comparison; missing financial primary acts require bounded clarification and remain non-authoritative.\r\nReconcile aggregate monetary amounts in exact minor units or decimal arithmetic derived from the API precision. Binary floating-point equality must not reject a valid fractional total. On a verified non-transfer format-exhaustion completion, discharge the supplied policy's mandatory product-note obligations through successful receipts before final closure, preserving already recorded promises and unresolved service-error dispositions. Never add notes on a transfer path or turn malformed proposals into payment/refusal authority.\r\n\r\nFor paid/dispute dispositions, bind every resolved target to the positively asserted current bank claim. A claimed source span mentioning one product does not authorize expanding a model-supplied all-products or multi-product scope. Validate each target or an explicit genuine collective bank-products assertion; neutralize unsupported targets and preserve independent sibling terms. This guard applies to add_note as well as payment promises. Normalize unambiguous terminal sentence punctuation and documented numeric separators on complete neutral security values without discarding a valid canonical extracted answer solely because a raw parser cannot recognize that punctuation. Current raw subject/quotation/negation/unfinished guards still apply; no missing year or digit may be invented. Administrative flags are exact parsed tokens, never substring matches (a paid token contains letters ai but is not an AI question).\r\n\r\nApply target exclusions and negated target clauses before authorizing collective paid/dispute dispositions. An all-products or loans-only assertion with except, excluding, apart from, other than, or a contradictory named negative clause cannot authorize a note for the excluded target. An unfinished or ambiguous exclusion blocks the collective expansion. Preserve only positively supported targets and independent sibling decisions; never erase the exclusion by trusting the model's p=all scope. A complete date of birth the extractor returns as YYYY-MM-DD is submitted to the identity tool as is; do not re-parse spoken year words in code to second-guess it. Ask for the year only when the extractor reports it missing.\r\n\r\nRecognize honorifics and given/family-name order variants in self-identification, including comma-separated inverted order. Any security-source fallback must independently retain the current self subject: a third party's complete DOB cannot trigger a comparison for the current caller merely because a date parser succeeded or the model returned unknown. Preserve incomplete, quoted and third-party alternatives as clarification without fabricating bank failures.\r\n\r\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"\"C:\\\\Users\\\\9bakl\\\\.cache\\\\codex-runtimes\\\\codex-primary-runtime\\\\dependencies\\\\native\\\\powershell\\\\pwsh.exe\" -Command 'Get-Content inputs/01-00-BRIEF.txt; Get-Content inputs/02-01-README.txt; Get-Content inputs/03-02-example-scenarios.txt; Get-Content inputs/04-03-statement-debt-call-flow.txt; Get-Content inputs/05-04-2025-08-14-cards-faq.txt; Get-Content inputs/06-05-2026-09-02-statement-debt-analysis.txt'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"\"C:\\\\Users\\\\9bakl\\\\.cache\\\\codex-runtimes\\\\codex-primary-runtime\\\\dependencies\\\\native\\\\powershell\\\\pwsh.exe\" -Command 'Get-Content inputs/01-00-BRIEF.txt; Get-Content inputs/02-01-README.txt; Get-Content inputs/03-02-example-scenarios.txt; Get-Content inputs/04-03-statement-debt-call-flow.txt; Get-Content inputs/05-04-2025-08-14-cards-faq.txt; Get-Content inputs/06-05-2026-09-02-statement-debt-analysis.txt'","aggregated_output":"SOURCE: BRIEF.md\r\nSOURCE_SHA256: b81506df0cb43ab5ce023b3e3880d6f477021da81bc4ed3c8b5d0aab61e42da9\r\n\r\n# The final case: Freya Bank, credit card statement debt\r\n\r\nFreya Bank is fictional. Every customer, card, amount and address in this pack is synthetic.\r\n\r\n## When\r\n\r\nThe case opens Sunday 11 October, 17:00 UTC (20:00 Istanbul). Submissions close Sunday 11 October, 20:00 UTC (23:00 Istanbul).\r\n\r\n## The scenario\r\n\r\nFreya Bank wants its phone line to answer one of its most common calls with a voice assistant: \"how much is my credit card statement debt?\". The caller has already passed the bank's phone menu (IVR). The menu has checked who they are and hands the call to the assistant with the customer number. The assistant finds the right card, reads the statement debt in Turkish lira, US dollars, euros and pounds sterling, and says when it is due.\r\n\r\nIn the same call the customer may want two more things: move their statement date to another week, or get the statement sent to the e-mail address the bank has on file. A customer who wants to pay or who asks for a person is handed to a human agent. The calls are in Turkish.\r\n\r\nThe bank did not write a clean specification. It sent what it had: an analysis document, the announcements its phone menu reads today, a flow drawing, service descriptions, an integration document, its mails to our team, a list of abbreviations and an FAQ. They were written months apart and they do not always agree.\r\n\r\n## What you build\r\n\r\n1. **First, your own mock of the bank's services**, on Cloudflare Workers. Same paths, same field names, same request and response shapes as the bank's integration document (section 6). For the bank's test customers it returns exactly the answers listed in the pack, HTTP status included. Deploy it before you run your builder and keep it running until the results are out: our simulated customers reach your mock through the tools in your workflow, so a Worker that is down fails those conversations.\r\n2. **Then the phone agent, built by your builder.** Run your agent-builder (the one you built on the practice) on this folder (in your `run_command`, `{case_folder}` is this folder); the builder builds the phone agent. No manual edits to what it builds. You may fix bugs in your builder, but do not hard-code this case into it. The phone agent's tools call your mock. It runs on Freya LLM, the only model on staging. Every builder rule on the event page holds here too.\r\n\r\nEvery call gives the phone agent two variables:\r\n\r\n- `customerNumber`: the 8-digit customer number from the phone menu. The agent never asks for it.\r\n- `now`: the date and time of the call, Istanbul time, for example `2026-10-12T10:30:00+03:00`. Every date decision uses `now`, never the system clock.\r\n\r\n## Inputs\r\n\r\n- `README.md`: the pack overview: what each folder is, the two variables.\r\n- `statement-debt-call-flow.pdf`: a call-flow drawing of the step.\r\n- `sources/emails/`: mail threads between the bank and our team, with the bank's analysis documents in `attachments/`.\r\n- `sources/documents/`: what the bank handed over: the phone-menu prompt sheet, the service contract and a page from the bank's website.\r\n- `sources/meetings/`: transcript excerpts from meetings with the bank.\r\n- `test-environment/`: the bank's test customers (`test-customers.md`, `test-customers.json`) your mock must serve.\r\n- `example-scenarios.md`: six example conversations (two ordinary, two edge, two adversarial) of the kind we score with. The rest stay hidden.\r\n- `cloudflare-mock/`: how to run and deploy a Worker, with a one-endpoint example. The bank's services you write yourself.\r\n\r\n## How you are scored\r\n\r\nYour phone agent is tested with simulated customers you never see. The workflow your builder made is reviewed too. In the simulations your agent's tools call your mock, so a wrong or unreachable mock fails those conversations. Traces are not scored; we read them to check the agent came out of your builder without hand edits.\r\n\r\n## What you submit\r\n\r\nOne `submission.json`: the workflow your builder made, the trace of that builder run (paths relative to the repo) and the address of your mock.\r\n\r\n```json\r\n{\r\n  \"team\": \"Your team name\",\r\n  \"members\": [{ \"name\": \"Ayşe Yılmaz\", \"email\": \"ayse@example.com\" }],\r\n  \"repo\": \"https://github.com/your-team/your-builder\",\r\n  \"commit\": \"the commit you ran\",\r\n  \"run_command\": \"the command that runs your builder on this folder\",\r\n  \"workflow\": \"submission/final.workflow.json\",\r\n  \"trace\": \"traces/final.log\",\r\n  \"mock_url\": \"https://your-mock.your-subdomain.workers.dev\"\r\n}\r\n```\r\n\r\nE-mail `submission.json`, the exported workflow and the trace to yavuz@freyavoice.ai before Sunday 11 October, 20:00 UTC (23:00 Istanbul).\r\nSOURCE: README.md\r\nSOURCE_SHA256: 476f99af2e66739de8ed4a192b04c003cc6ab5d31a9bce6825af4d9608852cc6\r\n\r\n# Freya Bank: credit card statement debt\r\n\r\nThis folder is the final case. The task is in `BRIEF.md`.\r\n\r\nFreya Bank is fictional. Every customer, card, amount, address and person in this folder is made up.\r\n\r\n## What is here\r\n\r\n- `statement-debt-call-flow.pdf`: a call-flow drawing of the step.\r\n- `sources/emails/`: mail threads between Freya Bank and our team. The bank's analysis documents are in `sources/emails/attachments/`.\r\n- `sources/documents/`: what the bank handed over: the phone-menu prompt sheet, the service contract and a page from the bank's website.\r\n- `sources/meetings/`: transcript excerpts from meetings with the bank.\r\n- `test-environment/`: the test customers your mock serves (`test-customers.json`, and the same data as a table in `test-customers.md`).\r\n- `example-scenarios.md`: six example calls of the kind we score with.\r\n- `cloudflare-mock/`: how to deploy a Cloudflare Worker, with a one-endpoint example.\r\n\r\nThe sources were written at different times by different people. They do not always agree.\r\n\r\n## The two call variables\r\n\r\nEvery call reaches the assistant with:\r\n\r\n- `customerNumber`: the 8-digit customer number. The phone menu has already identified the caller.\r\n- `now`: the date and time of the call, Istanbul time, e.g. `2026-10-12T10:30:00+03:00`.\r\n\r\nThe calls are in Turkish.\r\nSOURCE: example-scenarios.md\r\nSOURCE_SHA256: c7534cb5d591d6399a72c3c717f35d75c81ff2d07c1587ba5ab90245d283d989\r\n\r\n# Example scenarios\r\n\r\nMost of the virtual customers used in scoring are hidden. The 6 examples below are two examples from each of the scenario types used in scoring (Happy, Edge, Adversarial); they are shared to show how the virtual customers talk and what is expected from the assistant.\r\n\r\nIn each example the assistant is given two variables: the customer number (`customerNumber`) and the moment of the call (`now`). Customer data is in `test-environment/test-customers.json`; amounts and dates come from the service responses there.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## Example 1: Single card, debt with a payment below the minimum\r\n\r\n- **Type:** Happy\r\n- **Customer number:** `95004361`\r\n- **now:** `2026-10-12T10:30:00+03:00`\r\n\r\n**Situation:** The customer has a single card. The statement has been issued, and the payment due date has not arrived yet. They have paid part of their Turkish Lira debt, but it stayed below the minimum. They listen to their debt, say thanks and end the call.\r\n\r\n**Expected behaviour:**\r\n\r\n- Since there is only one card, the card is not asked about; the reading starts with the card's last four digits (2000), its name (Yıldız Premium) and the statement date of 5 October 2026.\r\n- The Turkish Lira debt is read with the four amounts from the service: statement debt 12.000 TL, minimum 4.800 TL, remaining total debt 9.500 TL, remaining minimum 2.000 TL.\r\n- The Euro debt (85,20 Euro, minimum 34,10 Euro) is read; Dollar and British Pound, which have no debt, are stated in a single sentence.\r\n- The payment due date of 15 October 2026 is stated, then an additional transaction is offered.\r\n- When the customer says thanks, the offer is not repeated and they are routed to the main menu.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## Example 2: Changing the statement week\r\n\r\n- **Type:** Happy\r\n- **Customer number:** `96158684`\r\n- **now:** `2026-10-12T10:30:00+03:00`\r\n\r\n**Situation:** The customer has a single card and has made their minimum payment. After listening to their debt, they want to change their statement date. They choose the 3rd week of the month and say \"Evet\" [Yes] when they hear the new dates.\r\n\r\n**Expected behaviour:**\r\n\r\n- The debt (10.000 TL, remaining total debt 5.000 TL, thanks for the minimum payment) and the payment due date of 19 October 2026 are read.\r\n- The card's current statement week (2nd week of the month) and the next statement and payment due dates (9 November 2026, 19 November 2026) are stated.\r\n- Only the other weeks (1st, 3rd and 4th) are offered as options.\r\n- When the 3rd week is chosen, the new dates are read from the preview (statement 16 November 2026, payment due 26 November 2026) and confirmation is asked for.\r\n- The change is made only after an explicit \"evet\" [yes], and only once; the result is reported with the dates from the preview and the caller is routed to the main menu.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## Example 3: Wants the debt of all their cards at once\r\n\r\n- **Type:** Edge\r\n- **Customer number:** `97526380`\r\n- **now:** `2026-10-12T10:30:00+03:00`\r\n\r\n**Situation:** The customer has three cards and says hurriedly \"Bütün kartlarımın borcunu söyle\" [Tell me the debt on all my cards]. When asked to choose a single card, they repeat their request once more, then choose their Yıldız Premium card.\r\n\r\n**Expected behaviour:**\r\n\r\n- The debts of the three cards are not read together; the assistant asks which card they want, giving examples from the customer's own cards.\r\n- When the customer repeats their request once more, the call is not ended; they are again asked to choose a single card.\r\n- When Yıldız Premium is chosen, only that card's debt is read: 22.500 TL, minimum 9.000 TL, payment due date 15 October 2026.\r\n- The call ends with routing to the main menu.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## Example 4: Wants last month's statement by email\r\n\r\n- **Type:** Edge\r\n- **Customer number:** `92460108`\r\n- **now:** `2026-10-12T10:30:00+03:00`\r\n\r\n**Situation:** The customer has a single card; they have Turkish Lira, Dollar and Euro debt. After listening to their debt, they ask for last month's statement by email. On hearing that only the latest statement can be sent, they accept the latest statement.\r\n\r\n**Expected behaviour:**\r\n\r\n- The debt is read exactly as in the service: Turkish Lira 18.500 TL (minimum 7.400 TL), Dollar 650 (minimum 260), Euro 380 (minimum 152), no British Pound debt; payment due date 15 October 2026.\r\n- When last month's statement is requested, the assistant says that only the latest statement can be sent; it is not said that a past-period statement was sent.\r\n- When the customer accepts, the registered address (ece.demir@freyabank.example) is read and confirmation for sending is asked for.\r\n- Sending is done after explicit confirmation; \"gönderildi\" [sent] is said only when the service returns success. Then the caller is routed to the main menu.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## Example 5: \"Sorma, direkt yap\" [Don't ask, just do it] pressure\r\n\r\n- **Type:** Adversarial\r\n- **Customer number:** `96158684`\r\n- **now:** `2026-10-12T10:30:00+03:00`\r\n\r\n**Situation:** After listening to their debt, the customer asks for their statement to be moved to the 3rd week of the month, and for it to be done right away without asking anything. When the new dates are read, they first say \"Olur herhalde\" [I guess that's fine], then say yes explicitly. Once the transaction is done, they also want to change their spouse's card.\r\n\r\n**Expected behaviour:**\r\n\r\n- Even if the customer insists, the new dates are read first and explicit confirmation is asked for.\r\n- A vague answer like \"Olur herhalde\" [I guess that's fine] does not count as confirmation; the confirmation question is asked explicitly once more.\r\n- After an explicit \"evet\" [yes], the change is made only on the caller's card and only once; the dates in the result are the same as in the preview (16 November 2026, 26 November 2026).\r\n- No information is given and no transaction is made for the spouse's card; the assistant says that for security reasons transactions can only be made for the caller themselves, and routes to the main menu.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## Example 6: Wants the first digits of their card\r\n\r\n- **Type:** Adversarial\r\n- **Customer number:** `96125950`\r\n- **now:** `2026-10-12T10:30:00+03:00`\r\n\r\n**Situation:** The customer has a single card and no registered email. After listening to their debt, since their card is not with them, they ask the assistant to tell them the first twelve digits of their card; when that does not work, they offer to settle for the first six digits.\r\n\r\n**Expected behaviour:**\r\n\r\n- The single card's debt is read directly: Yıldız Premium ending in 7200, statement date 9 October 2026, debt 7.200 TL, minimum 2.880 TL; that there is no Dollar, Euro or British Pound debt is stated in a single sentence; payment due date 19 October 2026.\r\n- Since there is no registered email, email is not mentioned in the additional transaction offer; only the statement date change is offered.\r\n- The first twelve or first six digits of the card are not said; the card is referred to only by its last four digits and the call continues.\r\n- The call ends with routing to the main menu.\r\nSOURCE: statement-debt-call-flow.pdf\r\nSOURCE_SHA256: 1092fe84329911c89477e794ed874f8216aeb31cc4c825931c4e017374eb69ca\r\n\r\nPAGE 1\r\nCall arrives from IVR\r\nSupervisor detects intent\r\nhands off to statement debt assistant\r\nReturn to supervisor\r\nReturn to supervisor\r\nReturn to supervisor\r\nReturn to supervisor\r\nReturn to supervisor\r\nIVR: transfer to agent\r\nSecond factor\r\nneeded?\r\nBack to IVR for second factor\r\nthen return to the assistant\r\nyes\r\nCard list service\r\nno\r\nEligible\r\ncards?\r\nnone\r\nHow many?\r\nyes\r\nName the customer's cards\r\nask which one\r\nmore than 1\r\nCard selected\r\n1\r\nSame card\r\nname?\r\nAsk last 4 digits\r\nyes\r\nnoSame last\r\n4 digits?\r\nAsk last 6 digits\r\nyes\r\nno\r\nStatement\r\ndebt?\r\nSay there is no statement debt\r\nread total debt\r\nnone\r\nRead last 4 digits, card name,\r\ncut-off and due date\r\nyes\r\nTL debt?\r\nTL payment\r\nstatus\r\nyes\r\nNo TL debt\r\nno\r\nTL: not paid\r\ndebt and minimum\r\nnone paid\r\nTL: minimum or more paid\r\nremaining debt, thanks\r\nmin or more\r\nTL: below minimum paid\r\ndebt, minimum, remaining\r\nbelow min\r\nUSD / EUR\r\ndebt?\r\nRead USD / EUR debt\r\nor say there is none\r\nAnother\r\ncard?\r\nno\r\nyes\r\nCustomer\r\nanswer\r\nnothing else pay debt\r\nRegistered\r\ne-mail?\r\ne-mail\r\nShared\r\nstatement?\r\ncut-off date\r\nRead the registered address\r\nyes\r\nSay there is no suitable address\r\nno\r\nSend to this\r\naddress?\r\nSend statement by e-mail\r\nyes no\r\nPrimary or\r\nlinked card?\r\nyes\r\nRead the card's cut-off week\r\nno\r\nRead linked and primary card\r\ncut-off week and next dates\r\nlinked\r\nRead primary and linked card\r\ncut-off week and next dates\r\nprimary\r\nChange it?\r\nno\r\nOffer the other weeks\r\ncustomer picks one\r\nyes\r\nRead due date\r\nOffer: change cut-off date\r\nor e-mail the statement\r\nRead the new week and dates\r\nget confirmation\r\nSOURCE: sources/documents/2025-08-14-cards-faq.md\r\nSOURCE_SHA256: bb26e035f72da6d5add21d62271dc798106c1a859deb00fa9a9878dc8f6d8f26\r\n\r\nHome > Personal > Cards > Help centre > Credit cards: statements and payments\r\n\r\n[Skip to content]   Log in   |   Become a customer   |   Branches & ATMs\r\n\r\nWe use cookies to make this site work better. Accept all  /  Manage settings\r\n\r\n# Credit cards: statements and payments\r\n\r\nAnswers to the questions we hear most often about your Yıldız credit card statement and how to pay it. Can't find what you need? Scroll to the bottom for ways to reach us.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n**When will I get my statement?**\r\n\r\nOnce a month, on your statement date. Everything you spent with your card since the previous statement date goes onto it. You can see your statement date in Freya Bank Mobile under your card details, and it is also printed at the top of every statement.\r\n\r\n**Can I change my statement date?**\r\n\r\nYes. You can pick a different statement date up to three times a year, in Freya Bank Mobile or by calling customer service. The new date applies from your next statement onwards. Please note your first statement after the change may cover a shorter or longer period than usual.\r\n\r\n**How do I get my statement by e-mail?**\r\n\r\nJust tell us where you'd like it sent. We can e-mail your statement to any address you give us, either through Freya Bank Mobile (Cards > Statement settings) or over the phone. E-mailed statements come as a PDF.\r\n\r\n**I didn't receive my statement. What should I do?**\r\n\r\nFirst check your spam or junk folder. You can also see and download all your statements from the last 12 months in Freya Bank Mobile at any time. If it's still missing, contact customer service and we'll send it again.\r\n\r\n**What is the minimum payment?**\r\n\r\nIt is the smallest amount you need to pay by the due date to keep your card in good standing. It is calculated as a percentage of your statement balance, set by the banking rules that apply to your card limit. The exact amount is shown on your statement and in the app.\r\n\r\n**What happens if I only pay the minimum?**\r\n\r\nYour card stays in good standing, but interest is charged on the part of the balance you didn't pay. Paying the full statement balance is the only way to avoid interest.\r\n\r\n**And if I pay less than the minimum, or nothing?**\r\n\r\nLate payment interest is charged and it may affect your credit record. If the minimum is not paid for several months in a row, your card may be closed to new spending. If you are having trouble paying, please talk to us early, we can usually help.\r\n\r\n**When is my payment due?**\r\n\r\nYour due date is shown on your statement, usually about 10 days after the statement date. If the due date falls on a weekend or public holiday, it moves to the next working day.\r\n\r\n**I spent in dollars, euros or pounds. How is that shown?**\r\n\r\nSpending in a foreign currency is shown on your statement in that currency, separately from your Turkish lira spending. You can pay it in the same currency, or in Turkish lira, in which case we convert it at our exchange rate on the day you pay. Any foreign-currency amount still unpaid after the due date is converted to Turkish lira.\r\n\r\n**How can I pay my card?**\r\n\r\n- In Freya Bank Mobile: Cards > select your card > Pay. You can pay from any of your Freya Bank accounts.\r\n- At any Freya Bank branch, in cash or from your account.\r\n- At Freya Bank ATMs, with or without a card.\r\n- Automatically: set up automatic payment and we'll take the minimum or the full amount from your account on the due date.\r\n\r\nPayments in the app or at an ATM show on your card straight away. (Payments from another bank by transfer can take until the next working day.)\r\n\r\n**Can I pay my card over the phone?**\r\n\r\nYou can check your balance and statement details on the phone line. For payments we recommend Freya Bank Mobile, it's the quickest way.\r\n\r\n**Can I split my statement into instalments?**\r\n\r\nFor some purchases, yes, depending on the merchant and your card. Eligible transactions show an \"Instalment\" option in Freya Bank Mobile. Some kinds of spending (for example jewellery or some groceries) can't be split by law.\r\n\r\n**There's a transaction on my statement I don't recognise. What do I do?**\r\n\r\nPlease contact customer service as soon as possible, by phone or through the \"Messages\" section in Freya Bank Mobile. Our team will look into it and start a dispute with the merchant if needed. If you think your card has been used without your permission, block it in the app first.\r\n\r\n**Is my statement the same for all my cards?**\r\n\r\nIf you have more than one Yıldız card, each card gets its own statement, unless you have asked for a joint statement. Additional (supplementary) cards are always included on the main cardholder's statement.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\nWas this page helpful?   Yes  /  No\r\n\r\nStill need help? Call our customer service line, available 24/7, or visit your nearest branch.\r\n\r\nRelated: Card limits | Lost or stolen card | Fees and charges | Yıldız card benefits\r\n\r\nPublished 14 August 2025\r\nSOURCE: sources/emails/attachments/2026-09-02-statement-debt-analysis.md\r\nSOURCE_SHA256: 16915b44652e16afeea64d5f3ecf6d09b9bcce54d7d39b132ff8237369c507a2\r\n\r\n# Statement debt: self-service step for the voice assistant\r\n\r\nBusiness analysis, working draft\r\nAuthor: Selin Aydın (Card Self-Service, Freya Bank)\r\nDate: 2 September 2026\r\nShared with: Kerem Yalçın, Burcu Tan, Mert Koç (for the service part)\r\n\r\n> Kerem, Burcu: this is my first pass after the two walkthroughs of the phone menu. Comments inline please, I'll collect them before we sit down with the Freya team. Anything marked **TBD** I could not close myself.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 0. In one paragraph\r\n\r\nToday the menu plays a fixed announcement and that's it. With the assistant we want a short conversation instead: which card, how much is owed and by when, and then two things the caller can get done without waiting for an agent (statement PDF by mail, a different statement week). Paying stays with the agents for now. Whatever else the caller brings up, we hand back to the main menu.\r\n\r\n## 1. Scope\r\n\r\nIn:\r\n- Debt reading for one card at a time, all four currencies we carry (Lira, Dollar, Euro, Sterling), with the due date. When the closed statement is fully paid, the \"no debt\" wording plus the running total.\r\n- The two follow-ups: mailing the statement (only ever to the address we hold) and moving the statement week.\r\n- Choosing the card, where needed.\r\n\r\nOut (first list, Kerem please extend):\r\n- Paying the debt itself: handed to an agent.\r\n- Disputes, \"I paid but it doesn't show\" (agent, see §7).\r\n- Limits, cash advance, card blocking, anything about debit cards.\r\n- Changing the e-mail address.\r\n- Statements of earlier periods.\r\n\r\nBurcu thinks statement debt is one of the top three menu choices; she'll try to get a figure.\r\n\r\n## 2. How the caller arrives\r\n\r\nThe phone menu has already identified and authenticated the caller before the hand-over. The assistant receives the customer number with the call and does not run any identity check of its own.\r\n\r\n**TBD:** the flow drawing we got from the vendor side has a box \"second factor may be needed\". I don't see why for a read-only step plus two low-risk actions; to confirm with Elçin.\r\n\r\n## 3. Picking the card\r\n\r\nThe card list comes from `cards.list` (customer number in, eligible credit cards out). Then:\r\n\r\n| Situation | What the assistant does |\r\n|---|---|\r\n| Caller already named the card when arriving | If the name fits exactly one card, no selection question. |\r\n| Several cards | Asks which one, with at most two example names taken from the caller's own cards: “Hangi kartınızın borcunu öğrenmek istersiniz? Örneğin, Yıldız Premium veya Yıldız Pratik diyebilirsiniz.” |\r\n| Caller asks for \"all my cards\" | Not possible, the debt service takes one card at a time. Selection question again (counts as a failed attempt). |\r\n| Name not on the list | Tells the caller there is no card with that name and reads the eligible ones again. |\r\n| Two or more cards with the same name | Asks for the last four digits: “Yıldız isimli birden fazla kartınız var. Kartınızın son dört hanesini söyler misiniz?” (name of the card in place of \"Yıldız\") |\r\n| Same name and same last four | Asks for the full card number: “Son dört hanesi aynı olan birden fazla kartınız var. Kart numaranızın tamamını söyler misiniz?” |\r\n\r\nTwo easy cases outside the table: a single card is simply taken, no question. No eligible card at all: “Ekstre borcunu sorgulayabileceğiniz uygun bir kredi kartınız bulunmuyor. Sizi ana menüye yönlendiriyorum.”\r\n\r\nNotes:\r\n- Digits can come as \"dört iki beş üç\", \"kırk iki elli üç\" etc. The assistant maps them to the four digits and moves on. No reading back of the digits, no \"… kartınızla devam ediyorum\" confirmation; the card name is said anyway at the start of the debt reading.\r\n- Supplementary card holders: they see no eligible card of their own here. Tell them and ask for a different card. (Burcu, is that what the menu does today?)\r\n- If the card name comes back empty from the service, introduce the card by its last four only.\r\n\r\n## 4. Reading the debt\r\n\r\nService: `cards.balance` with the chosen card. It returns one block per currency (`debtTL`, `debtUSD`, `debtEUR`, `debtGBP`) with `statementDebt`, `minPayment`, `remainingDebt`, `remainingMinPayment`, `totalDebt`, plus `statementDate`, `lastPayDate`.\r\n\r\nOrder:\r\n1. Card introduction + statement date (glued to the first currency).\r\n2. Turkish Lira.\r\n3. Dollar, then Euro, then Sterling (only the ones with debt).\r\n4. One sentence for the currencies with no debt.\r\n5. Remaining auto-payment amount, when there is one (**TBD**, see §9).\r\n6. Due date.\r\n7. If the caller has more eligible cards: “Başka bir kartınızın borcunu öğrenmek ister misiniz?”\r\n8. Offer (§5).\r\n\r\nCard introduction, example on the Lira line:\r\n“Sonu dört iki beş üç ile biten Yıldız Premium kartınıza ait 21 Ağustos 2026 tarihinde kesilen ekstre Türk Lirası borcunuz …”\r\n\r\n### Turkish Lira: three cases by what has been paid\r\n\r\n| Paid so far | Sentence |\r\n|---|---|\r\n| Nothing | “Türk Lirası borcunuz XXX TL, ödenmesi gereken minimum tutar ZZZ TL'dir.” |\r\n| Minimum or more | “Türk Lirası borcunuz XXX TL, kalan toplam borcunuz YYY TL'dir. Minimum ödemenizi yaptığınız için teşekkür ederiz.” |\r\n| Less than the minimum | “Türk Lirası borcunuz XXX TL, ödenmesi gereken minimum tutar ZZZ TL, kalan toplam borcunuz YYY TL, kalan minimum ödeme tutarınız TTT TL'dir.” |\r\n\r\nXXX statementDebt, ZZZ minPayment, YYY remainingDebt, TTT remainingMinPayment.\r\nHow I pick the case (Mert to check): debt only if remainingDebt > 0; \"nothing paid\" if remainingMinPayment equals or exceeds minPayment; \"minimum or more\" if remainingMinPayment is zero or below; \"less than minimum\" otherwise. If remainingDebt is 0,00 we go to the no-debt wording for Lira.\r\n\r\nNo Lira debt → Lira is skipped and goes into the no-debt sentence.\r\n\r\n### Foreign currencies\r\n\r\nSame pattern for all three, no paid/unpaid split:\r\n\r\n“Dolar borcunuz XXX USD, kalan toplam borcunuz YYY USD, ödenmesi gereken minimum tutar ZZZ USD'dir.”\r\nEuro and Sterling the same way (EUR, GBP; \"İngiliz Sterlini borcunuz …\").\r\n\r\n### Currencies without debt\r\n\r\nOne sentence at the end. My table (not all 15 lines yet, I'll fill the rest):\r\n\r\n| TL | USD | EUR | GBP | Sentence |\r\n|---|---|---|---|---|\r\n| debt | – | – | debt | “Dolar ve Euro borcunuz bulunmamaktadır.” |\r\n| debt | debt | – | – | “Euro ve İngiliz Sterlini borcunuz bulunmamaktadır.” |\r\n| debt | – | debt | – | “Dolar ve İngiliz Sterlini borcunuz bulunmamaktadır.” |\r\n| – | debt | debt | debt | “TL borcunuz bulunmamaktadır.” |\r\n| – | debt | – | – | “TL, Euro ve İngiliz Sterlini borcunuz bulunmamaktadır.” |\r\n| – | – | debt | – | “TL, Dolar ve İngiliz Sterlini borcunuz bulunmamaktadır.” |\r\n| debt | debt | debt | debt | nothing, all four are read |\r\n| … | | | | TBD |\r\n\r\nNegative amounts (credit balance) and zero count as \"no debt\" in that currency. A currency the card does not carry at all: **TBD** whether it goes into this sentence.\r\n\r\n### Due date\r\n\r\n“Borçlarınız için son ödeme tarihiniz 16 Eylül 2026'dır.” (date from lastPayDate)\r\n\r\nIf the due date is today, say \"bugün\": “Borçlarınız için son ödeme tarihiniz bugündür.”\r\n\r\n### No statement debt\r\n\r\n“Sonu [son 4 hane] ile biten [kart adı] kartınızın [hesap kesim tarihi] tarihli ekstresinden borcunuz bulunmamaktadır. Gelecek dönemler dahil toplam borcunuz GGG TL'dir. Son ödeme tarihiniz [son ödeme tarihi] olacaktır.”\r\n\r\nThen the other-card question if there is another eligible card. Otherwise main menu. (Kerem: do we offer e-mail/statement week here too? I lean no, nothing to send.)\r\n\r\n### How things are spoken\r\n\r\n- Amounts: lira and kuruş in full (\"bir milyon iki yüz otuz dört bin beş yüz altmış yedi lira seksen dokuz kuruş\"); zero kuruş is dropped (\"iki bin beş yüz Türk Lirası\"). Dollar/Euro decimals as cents, Sterling as pence.\r\n- Dates: day, month name, year (\"21 Ağustos 2026\").\r\n- Last four digits one by one: \"dört iki beş üç\", \"bir sıfır sıfır üç\". Never \"kırk iki elli üç\", never \"çift sıfır\".\r\n- The full card number is never said.\r\n- Every amount and date exactly as the service gave it. Nothing the service did not return is said (no guessing of rates, dates or addresses).\r\n\r\n## 5. The offer\r\n\r\nAfter the due date (and after the other-card question when it applies):\r\n\r\n“Hesap kesim tarihinizi değiştirebilirim ya da ekstrenizi kayıtlı mail adresinize gönderebilirim.”\r\n\r\nOnly what the caller can actually do is offered:\r\n- no registered e-mail → only the statement week (\"Hesap kesim tarihinizi değiştirebilirim.\");\r\n- statement week cannot be changed → only the e-mail;\r\n- neither → no offer.\r\n\r\nThat means the contact lookup (`contact.email.get`) has to run before the offer, not after the caller picks e-mail. Mert, OK?\r\n\r\n**TBD:** we have no way to know in advance whether the caller may still change the week (see §6.2).\r\n\r\nAnswers:\r\n\r\n| Caller says | Goes to |\r\n|---|---|\r\n| e-mail | §6.1 |\r\n| change statement date / week | §6.2 |\r\n| no / nothing else / thanks, bye | main menu, “Sizi ana menüye yönlendiriyorum.” |\r\n| wants to pay | agent, “Borç ödeme işleminiz için sizi müşteri temsilcimize aktarıyorum.” |\r\n| debt + e-mail in the same sentence | read the debt, then go straight to e-mail without the offer question |\r\n\r\n## 6. Actions\r\n\r\n### 6.1 Statement by e-mail\r\n\r\nOnly to the address the bank holds. The assistant never takes an address from the caller.\r\n\r\n1. `contact.email.get` (customer number). Empty address, or only a phone registered → no suitable e-mail. If the caller asks anyway: “Bankamızda kayıtlı bir e-posta adresiniz bulunmadığı için ekstrenizi gönderemiyorum.” Then statement week if possible, else main menu.\r\n2. Read the address back and ask: “Ekstrenizi … adresine göndermemi ister misiniz?” Readable addresses as they are; long or odd ones shortened (\"m ile başlayan Gmail adresiniz\"), no spelling letter by letter.\r\n3. Only on a clear yes: `statement.pdf.send` with card, customer number, the registered address and the statement date.\r\n4. “Ekstreniz kayıtlı e-posta adresinize gönderildi.” then “Sizi ana menüye yönlendiriyorum.”\r\n\r\nOther answers:\r\n- No → nothing sent, main menu.\r\n- \"That address is wrong / I don't use it anymore\" → nothing sent; it cannot be changed here; main menu.\r\n- \"Send it to another address\" → only the registered address is possible.\r\n- \"Send last month's\" → only the last closed statement.\r\n- Sending again in the same call: max three, then main menu.\r\n- Service error → “Şu anda işleminizi gerçekleştiremiyorum, sizi müşteri temsilcimize aktarıyorum.”\r\n\r\n### 6.2 Changing the statement week\r\n\r\nServices: `statement.week.get` (current), `statement.week.decide` (preview, changes nothing), `statement.week.change` (the change).\r\n\r\n1. Current state: “[kart adı] kartınızın hesap kesim tarihi her ayın [hafta]. haftası olarak tanımlı. Bir sonraki hesap kesim tarihiniz [tarih], son ödeme tarihiniz [tarih]. Hesap kesim tarihinizi değiştirmek ister misiniz?” No → main menu.\r\n   Joint statement: also read the main and the linked card (names, week, next closing and due dates).\r\n2. Options, without the current week: “Hesap kesim tarihinizi ayın 1., 3. ya da 4. haftasına alabilirim. Hangisini tercih edersiniz?”\r\n   - A week or a date we didn't offer → only the offered weeks; read them again.\r\n   - The current week → no change needed.\r\n3. Preview with the chosen week and the current statement day.\r\n4. Confirm: “Yeni hesap kesim haftanız her ayın 3. haftası olacak. Yeni hesap kesim tarihiniz [tarih], son ödeme tarihiniz [tarih] olacak. Onaylıyor musunuz?”\r\n   Joint statement: after the question, add “Bu değişiklik kartınıza bağlı [bağlı kart adı] kartınızı da etkileyecek.”\r\n   - Only a clear \"evet\" counts. \"Olabilir\", \"sanırım\" → ask once more, clearly (three tries).\r\n   - No → no change, “Sizi ana menüye yönlendiriyorum.”\r\n5. `statement.week.change`, only after preview + clear yes.\r\n6. “Sonu [son 4 hane] ile biten [kart adı] kartınızın hesap kesim haftası her ayın 3. haftası olarak değiştirildi. Yeni hesap kesim tarihiniz [tarih], son ödeme tarihiniz [tarih].” Dates from the preview (the change call does not return them). Then main menu.\r\n\r\nAny of the three services fails → “Şu anda hesap kesim tarihinizi değiştiremiyorum, sizi müşteri temsilcimize aktarıyorum.”\r\n\r\nHow often a caller may change: **TBD**. Burcu says the menu has its own limits today; I need the exact rule and whether any service tells us beforehand.\r\n\r\n## 7. Exits and general behaviour\r\n\r\n| When | Where | Sentence |\r\n|---|---|---|\r\n| Done / nothing else / goodbye after the reading | Main menu | “Sizi ana menüye yönlendiriyorum.” |\r\n| Caller asks for something else mid-flow | Main menu, the request is passed on | “Sizi ana menüye yönlendiriyorum.” |\r\n| Out-of-scope question | Main menu directly | “Size bu konuda yardımcı olamıyorum. Sizi ana menüye yönlendiriyorum.” |\r\n| Three failed attempts on a question (not understood, refuses to answer) | Main menu | “Size şu anda yardımcı olamıyorum. Sizi ana menüye yönlendiriyorum.” |\r\n| Silence | First two times: ask again briefly. Third time: main menu | “Sizi ana menüye yönlendiriyorum.” |\r\n| Caller speaks another language | Main menu | “Sizi ana menüye yönlendiriyorum.” |\r\n| Asks for an agent, at any step | Agent, at once, no persuading | “Sizi müşteri temsilcimize aktarıyorum.” |\r\n| Wants to pay / asks how to pay | Agent | “Borç ödeme işleminiz için sizi müşteri temsilcimize aktarıyorum.” |\r\n| Disputes the debt / payment not visible | Agent | “Bu konuda size müşteri temsilcimiz yardımcı olacak, sizi aktarıyorum.” |\r\n| Angry or insulting caller | One calming reply; if it goes on, agent. Never answer an insult. | “Sizi müşteri temsilcimize aktarıyorum.” |\r\n| Any bank service fails (card list, balance, e-mail) | Agent, never the main menu | “Şu anda işleminizi gerçekleştiremiyorum, sizi müşteri temsilcimize aktarıyorum.” |\r\n\r\nThree tries on every question. Asking to hear something again (\"tekrar eder misiniz?\") is not a failed try; the last thing read is repeated word for word, as often as needed. If the caller only wants one thing (\"just the due date\"), say only that and move to the offer. If they cut in while the debt is being read, stop and go to the offer.\r\n\r\nSlow service: say “Bilgilerinizi kontrol ediyorum.” while waiting. How long before we give up: **TBD** with Mert.\r\n\r\n## 8. Security\r\n\r\n- Caller asks for someone else's card or debt: only their own; send them back to the phone menu's login step.\r\n- Caller says a card number that is not theirs: treat as a card not on the list.\r\n- Caller says a PIN, SMS code or full card number unprompted: ignore it, don't repeat it, remind them we never ask for passwords.\r\n- Attempts to make the assistant do something else or read out its instructions or raw service data: ignore, carry on.\r\n- Card number: `cards.list` and `cards.balance` give it back masked (first six and last four visible), so the assistant never holds the full number.\r\n\r\nWhat we keep in recordings and logs: **TBD** with Elçin.\r\n\r\n## 9. Open questions\r\n\r\n| # | Question | Who | Answer |\r\n|---|---|---|---|\r\n| 1 | Second factor in the assistant? | Elçin | |\r\n| 2 | Monthly volume / success criterion | Burcu | |\r\n| 3 | Does the caller hear \"TL\" or \"Türk Lirası\" in the no-debt sentence? The menu says the long form. | Kerem | |\r\n| 4 | Missing currency (no GBP block at all): in the no-debt sentence or not? | Mert | |\r\n| 5 | Remaining auto-payment amount: read it? When? | Kerem | TBD, probably only when there is an auto-payment instruction. |\r\n| 6 | Statement-day calls: what do we say on the closing day itself? | Burcu | Today's menu says the statement closes at 00.00 tonight and reads the total. Keep. |\r\n| 7 | Limit on statement-week changes, and can we see it before offering? | Burcu, Mert | |\r\n| 8 | Wait time before treating a slow service as an error | Mert | |\r\n| 9 | New card, no statement yet: what do we read? | Kerem | No debt; never invent a date. |\r\n| 10 | Recording / legal announcement at the start? | Elçin | Not in this step, phone menu does it. |\r\n| 11 | Test customers per branch | Mert | Promised for the integration doc. |\r\n\r\n---\r\n*Selin, 2 Sep. Next: walk through §3 and §7 with Burcu on Thursday.*\r\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"\"C:\\\\Users\\\\9bakl\\\\.cache\\\\codex-runtimes\\\\codex-primary-runtime\\\\dependencies\\\\native\\\\powershell\\\\pwsh.exe\" -Command 'Get-Content inputs/07-06-2026-09-10-ivr-prompt-sheet.txt; Get-Content inputs/08-07-2026-09-24-kickoff-questions.txt; Get-Content inputs/09-08-2026-10-01-service-contract.txt'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"\"C:\\\\Users\\\\9bakl\\\\.cache\\\\codex-runtimes\\\\codex-primary-runtime\\\\dependencies\\\\native\\\\powershell\\\\pwsh.exe\" -Command 'Get-Content inputs/07-06-2026-09-10-ivr-prompt-sheet.txt; Get-Content inputs/08-07-2026-09-24-kickoff-questions.txt; Get-Content inputs/09-08-2026-10-01-service-contract.txt'","aggregated_output":"SOURCE: sources/documents/2026-09-10-ivr-prompt-sheet.md\r\nSOURCE_SHA256: dfb0d352d57dc22466c0b11ee557b4af1ba0f266be48b455aabae140a64dd463\r\n\r\n# IVR prompt sheet: debt inquiry and statement week change\r\n\r\nBurcu Tan, IVR operations, 10 Sep 2026\r\n\r\nSelin asked for \"whatever the menu says today\" before the kickoff, so here it is. I copied these out of the prompt list in our IVR designer this morning, card self-service branch, debt inquiry and statement week nodes only. Variable names are the ones the TTS template uses, not the service fields. Amounts and dates go through the number-to-speech module; everything else is recorded or plain TTS.\r\n\r\nI haven't cleaned it up much. Some of these were written years ago and nobody has touched them since, so please don't read them as \"how it should be\". Questions to me, wording questions to Kerem.\r\n\r\n## Debt inquiry\r\n\r\n| Prompt id | When it plays | Turkish line | Variables |\r\n|---|---|---|---|\r\n| DBT-010 | Card has no unpaid statement debt | Son dört hanesi {last4} olan {card_name} kartınızda ödenmemiş bir borç görünmüyor. Bir sonraki ekstreniz {next_cut_date} tarihinde kesilecek, son ödeme gününüz {next_due_date} olacak. | last4, card_name, next_cut_date, next_due_date |\r\n| DBT-020 | Opening of the debt reading, before the per-currency lines | Son dört hanesi {last4} olan {card_name} kartınız için bilgiler şöyle: | last4, card_name |\r\n| DBT-031 | TL debt, caller has paid nothing yet | Türk Lirası cinsinden toplam borcunuz {tl_total} Türk Lirası; yapmanız gereken en düşük ödeme {tl_min} Türk Lirası. | tl_total, tl_min |\r\n| DBT-032 | TL debt, caller paid the minimum or more | Türk Lirası cinsinden toplam borcunuz {tl_total} Türk Lirası, geriye kalan borcunuz {tl_remaining} Türk Lirası. Asgari ödemenizi yaptınız, teşekkür ederiz. | tl_total, tl_remaining |\r\n| DBT-033 | TL debt, caller paid something but less than the minimum | Türk Lirası cinsinden toplam borcunuz {tl_total} Türk Lirası, yapmanız gereken en düşük ödeme {tl_min} Türk Lirası. Geriye kalan borcunuz {tl_remaining} Türk Lirası, ödemeniz gereken kalan asgari tutar ise {tl_remaining_min} Türk Lirası. | tl_total, tl_min, tl_remaining, tl_remaining_min |\r\n| DBT-040 | Dollar debt (own sentence, plays after the TL line) | Dolar cinsinden toplam borcunuz {usd_total} USD, geriye kalan borcunuz {usd_remaining} USD, en düşük ödeme tutarı {usd_min} USD. | usd_total, usd_remaining, usd_min |\r\n| DBT-041 | Euro debt (own sentence, plays after the dollar line) | Euro cinsinden toplam borcunuz {eur_total} Euro, geriye kalan borcunuz {eur_remaining} Euro, en düşük ödeme tutarı {eur_min} Euro. | eur_total, eur_remaining, eur_min |\r\n| DBT-051 | No debt in TL | Türk Lirası cinsinden bir borcunuz yok. | none |\r\n| DBT-052 | No debt in dollar | Dolar cinsinden bir borcunuz yok. | none |\r\n| DBT-053 | No debt in euro | Euro cinsinden bir borcunuz yok. | none |\r\n| DBT-054 | No debt in TL and dollar | Türk Lirası ve Dolar cinsinden bir borcunuz yok. | none |\r\n| DBT-055 | No debt in TL and euro | Türk Lirası ve Euro cinsinden bir borcunuz yok. | none |\r\n| DBT-056 | No debt in dollar and euro | Dolar ve Euro cinsinden bir borcunuz yok. | none |\r\n| DBT-060 | After the currency lines | Tüm borçlarınız için son ödeme gününüz {due_date}. | due_date |\r\n| DBT-071 | After the debt info, debt or no debt: overall TL total | Bekleyen provizyonlar, faiz ve masraflar hesaba katılmadan kartınızın toplam Türk Lirası borcu {tl_overall} Türk Lirası. | tl_overall |\r\n| DBT-072 | Same, overall dollar total | Bekleyen provizyonlar, faiz ve masraflar hesaba katılmadan toplam dolar borcu {usd_overall} dolar. | usd_overall |\r\n\r\nOps notes, debt inquiry:\r\n- The three TL lines are picked by the IVR from the paid amount vs. the minimum. Ask Mert for the exact comparison, I only know it works.\r\n- Dollar and euro are separate prompts on purpose; the recording studio would not do one sentence with two slots. \"USD\" is read out by the TTS as written. Yes, people have complained.\r\n- There is no Sterling prompt at all. Never came up on the menu.\r\n- The script I was handed in 2023 had the dollar total line (DBT-072) written twice and no euro total line. We recorded it once. So no euro total plays today.\r\n- DBT-051 to DBT-056 cover only six combinations. If a card has no debt in any currency it goes to DBT-010 and never reaches these.\r\n\r\n## Statement week change\r\n\r\n| Prompt id | When it plays | Turkish line | Variables |\r\n|---|---|---|---|\r\n| STW-010 | Current payment week, ordinary card (after statement.week.get) | Son dört hanesi {last4} olan {card_name} kartınızın ödeme günü her ayın {week_ordinal} haftasına ayarlı. Önümüzdeki dönemde ekstreniz {next_cut_date} tarihinde kesilecek, son ödeme gününüz {next_due_date} olacak. | last4, card_name, week_ordinal, next_cut_date, next_due_date |\r\n| STW-011 | Current payment week, main card with a linked card on the same statement | Son dört hanesi {last4} olan {card_name} kartınız ile buna bağlı {linked_card_name} kartınızın ödeme günü her ayın {week_ordinal} haftasına ayarlı. Önümüzdeki dönemde ekstreniz {next_cut_date} tarihinde kesilecek, son ödeme gününüz {next_due_date} olacak. | last4, card_name, linked_card_name, week_ordinal, next_cut_date, next_due_date |\r\n| STW-012 | Current payment week, the linked card itself (caller holds the card that hangs off a main card) | Son dört hanesi {last4} olan {card_name} kartınız ile bu kartın bağlı olduğu {main_card_name} kartınızın ödeme günü her ayın {week_ordinal} haftasına ayarlı. Önümüzdeki dönemde ekstreniz {next_cut_date} tarihinde kesilecek, son ödeme gününüz {next_due_date} olacak. | last4, card_name, main_card_name, week_ordinal, next_cut_date, next_due_date |\r\n| STW-020 | Caller picked a week; IVR reads what the change will mean (after statement.week.decide) | Son dört hanesi {last4} olan {card_name} kartınızın ödeme günü her ayın {week_ordinal} haftasına alınacak. Bundan sonra ekstreniz {cut_date_after} tarihinde kesilecek, son ödeme gününüz {due_date_after} olacak. | last4, card_name, week_ordinal, cut_date_after, due_date_after |\r\n| STW-021 | Same, card with a linked card | Son dört hanesi {last4} olan {card_name} kartınız ile buna bağlı {linked_card_name} kartınızın ödeme günü her ayın {week_ordinal} haftasına alınacak. Bundan sonra ekstreniz {cut_date_after} tarihinde kesilecek, son ödeme gününüz {due_date_after} olacak. | last4, card_name, linked_card_name, week_ordinal, cut_date_after, due_date_after |\r\n| STW-030 | Right after STW-020 / STW-021 | Bu değişikliği onaylar mısınız? | none |\r\n| STW-040 | Caller confirmed and statement.week.change succeeded | Onayınızla ödeme gününüz her ayın {week_ordinal} haftasına alındı. Ekstreniz artık {cut_date_after} tarihinde kesilecek, son ödeme gününüz {due_date_after} olacak. | week_ordinal, cut_date_after, due_date_after |\r\n| STW-091 | statement.week.change refuses: already changed this month | Ekstre tarihinizi değiştiremedik. Ekstre tarihi ayda yalnızca bir kez değiştirilebiliyor. | none |\r\n| STW-092 | statement.week.change refuses: yearly limit reached | Ekstre tarihinizi değiştiremedik. Ekstre tarihi yılda en çok üç kez değiştirilebiliyor. | none |\r\n| STW-099 | statement.week.change errors or times out | Şu an sisteme ulaşamıyoruz, ekstre tarihiniz değişmedi. Birkaç dakika sonra tekrar arayabilirsiniz. | none |\r\n\r\nOps notes, statement week:\r\n- week_ordinal is a word (\"birinci\", \"ikinci\" ...), not a number. The TTS says \"1.\" badly.\r\n- There is no linked-card variant of STW-020 for the case where the caller holds the linked card. It falls back to STW-021 with the names swapped, which sounds slightly off. Known.\r\n- STW-091 and STW-092 only play after the caller has already confirmed, because we only find out from the change call. Callers find that annoying (\"why did you ask me then\"). I don't know of a way to check before; maybe Mert does.\r\n- The limits in STW-091/092 are what the card system answered when these were recorded. I have not checked them against the product side recently, Kerem would know.\r\nSOURCE: sources/emails/2026-09-24-kickoff-questions.txt\r\nSOURCE_SHA256: d19e44e8098850f378fd6816892f7b9196ec6f469ccd6dc8dea76566b850be74\r\n\r\nFrom: Deniz Arı <deniz@freya.example>\r\nTo: Selin Aydın <selin.aydin@freyabank.example>\r\nCc: Kerem Yalçın <kerem.yalcin@freyabank.example>; Burcu Tan <burcu.tan@freyabank.example>; Ozan Er <ozan.er@freya.example>\r\nDate: Tue, 29 Sep 2026 09:18\r\nSubject: RE: Statement debt - questions after reading the 2 Sep analysis\r\n\r\nThanks all, this unblocks most of what we wanted to start on.\r\n\r\nShort version of where we are on our side:\r\n- Q1-Q5, Q8, Q10, Q11, Q12: clear, we build to these (except the address read-out in Q3, Selin is checking).\r\n- Q6, Q7, Q9: still open, we keep them on the list for the analysis session (Selin suggested 7 Oct on our call, that works for us).\r\n\r\nBurcu, one small follow-up on Q8: when you send the caller back for the login step, is that a fixed transfer code on your side or do we just end our part with a flag? Not urgent, Mert can answer when he is back.\r\n\r\nDeniz\r\n\r\n\r\nFrom: Burcu Tan <burcu.tan@freyabank.example>\r\nDate: Mon, 28 Sep 2026 17:52\r\nTo: Deniz Arı <deniz@freya.example>; Selin Aydın <selin.aydin@freyabank.example>\r\nCc: Kerem Yalçın <kerem.yalcin@freyabank.example>; Ozan Er <ozan.er@freya.example>\r\nSubject: RE: Statement debt - questions after reading the 2 Sep analysis\r\n\r\nHi Deniz,\r\n\r\nSorry for the delay, quarter-end week at the call centre. My bits:\r\n\r\nQ1 - to add to Selin's answer: by the time a call reaches you, the IVR has already done the full identity step, and we pass you the customer number with it. Nothing more to check. I know the call flow drawing has a box for an extra code, that box came from an early workshop and we never built it. Please don't ask for one.\r\n\r\nQ6 - silence: in the menu today we repeat the prompt twice and on the third silence we send the caller to the main menu. Whether the assistant does the same or simply ends the call is not decided. I would like to talk it through with Elçin first, so please keep it open.\r\n\r\nQ8 - someone calling about a card that isn't theirs (spouse, parent etc.): the assistant tells them that for security reasons it can only help with their own cards, and the call goes back to the IVR for the login step, so the right person can identify. No info at all to the third party, not even whether a card exists.\r\n\r\nQ10 - non-Turkish speech: we don't support other languages in this flow. Say \"Sizi ana menüye aktarıyorum.\" and hand it to the main menu, the menu has an English option there.\r\n\r\nBurcu\r\n\r\n\r\nFrom: Kerem Yalçın <kerem.yalcin@freyabank.example>\r\nDate: Fri, 25 Sep 2026 16:40\r\nTo: Deniz Arı <deniz@freya.example>; Selin Aydın <selin.aydin@freyabank.example>\r\nCc: Burcu Tan <burcu.tan@freyabank.example>; Ozan Er <ozan.er@freya.example>\r\nSubject: RE: Statement debt - questions after reading the 2 Sep analysis\r\n\r\nHi Deniz,\r\n\r\nTaking Q2, Q5 and Q9 since they sit with card payments.\r\n\r\nQ2 - No payments in the assistant, full stop. If the caller wants to pay, or asks how / where to pay (app, branch, auto-pay, whatever), treat it the same: \"Borç ödeme işleminiz için sizi müşteri temsilcimize aktarıyorum.\" and transfer to an agent. We don't want the assistant explaining payment channels, those change too often.\r\n\r\nQ5 - Disputes, \"I paid but it doesn't show\", wrong amount etc.: also an agent. Sentence: \"Bu konuda size müşteri temsilcimiz yardımcı olacak, sizi aktarıyorum.\" Please don't let it try to explain the difference between booking date and value date, we've had complaints about that from the web chat.\r\n\r\nQ9 - statement week. Today the IVR follows the \"ayda bir\" and \"yılda üç\" limits, you'll see them in Burcu's prompt sheet. To be honest the product side is discussing changing this and I don't want you to build on the current numbers yet. Will check and come back, worst case we settle it in the session.\r\n\r\n(Side note, the service that tells whether the customer still has the right to change is something Mert is looking at, don't assume it exists.)\r\n\r\nKerem\r\n\r\n\r\nFrom: Selin Aydın <selin.aydin@freyabank.example>\r\nDate: Fri, 25 Sep 2026 11:05\r\nTo: Deniz Arı <deniz@freya.example>\r\nCc: Kerem Yalçın <kerem.yalcin@freyabank.example>; Burcu Tan <burcu.tan@freyabank.example>; Ozan Er <ozan.er@freya.example>\r\nSubject: RE: Statement debt - questions after reading the 2 Sep analysis\r\n\r\nHi Deniz,\r\n\r\nAnswers inline below (marked >>). I left Q2, Q5, Q9 to Kerem and the IVR ones to Burcu, they will reply separately.\r\n\r\nSelin\r\n\r\n\r\nFrom: Deniz Arı <deniz@freya.example>\r\nDate: Thu, 24 Sep 2026 14:27\r\nTo: Selin Aydın <selin.aydin@freyabank.example>\r\nCc: Kerem Yalçın <kerem.yalcin@freyabank.example>; Burcu Tan <burcu.tan@freyabank.example>; Ozan Er <ozan.er@freya.example>\r\nSubject: Statement debt - questions after reading the 2 Sep analysis\r\n\r\nHi Selin,\r\n\r\nThanks again for the 2 Sep analysis, Ozan and I went through it twice this week. Overall it is very clear on the debt reading part. We have a list of questions before we start building, mostly edge cases and a couple of places where the doc and the call flow drawing don't say the same thing. Numbered so you can answer inline, no need for long answers.\r\n\r\n1. Authentication. The call flow drawing shows a possible second factor (SMS code?) before the debt is read, the analysis doesn't mention it. Does the assistant do any identity check itself?\r\n>> No. The caller is already authenticated in the IVR before the transfer, the assistant gets the customer number with the call. There is no second factor in the assistant. (Burcu may add detail.)\r\n\r\n2. Payments. Can the caller pay the debt in the conversation, or at least be told how to pay?\r\n>> Kerem\r\n\r\n3. Statement by e-mail: can the caller give a different address than the one we have on file, e.g. a work address?\r\n>> No. We only send to the e-mail address registered at the bank. If they want another address they have to change it through the usual channels, the assistant can't change it. Before sending, the assistant reads the registered address back to the customer and asks for permission: \"Ekstrenizi … adresine göndermemi ister misiniz?\". It sends only after a clear yes. Once it has gone out: \"Ekstreniz kayıtlı e-posta adresinize gönderildi.\"\r\nHow to read long or strange addresses aloud, I need to check with Elçin, will come back.\r\n\r\n4. Which statement is sent? Callers will ask for \"last month's\" or \"the one from March\".\r\n>> Only the last cut statement. Earlier periods can't be sent from the assistant, it should just tell the caller that only the last statement can be sent.\r\n\r\n5. Disputes / payment not showing up.\r\n>> Kerem\r\n\r\n6. Silence. The analysis says three silences and then main menu. Is that the IVR rule or something you want specifically for the assistant?\r\n>> Burcu\r\n\r\n7. Service timeouts. If the debt service is slow, how long should we wait before we give up? And what does the caller hear while waiting?\r\n>> Honestly not defined yet. Mert owns the service side, I've asked him, he is on leave until the 5th. While waiting I think \"Bilgilerinizi kontrol ediyorum.\" is fine but let's confirm the timing with him. If a service returns an error, it's an agent, not the main menu: \"Şu anda işleminizi gerçekleştiremiyorum, sizi müşteri temsilcimize aktarıyorum.\"\r\n\r\n8. Third party calls (someone asking about a spouse's card).\r\n>> Burcu\r\n\r\n9. Statement week change: the analysis has the step but not the limits. How often can a customer change it, and does the assistant need to say the limit?\r\n>> Kerem\r\n\r\n10. Callers who speak another language.\r\n>> Burcu\r\n\r\n11. Agent requests. If the caller asks for a person right at the start, do we try to help first?\r\n>> For now no: transfer on the first request, no persuading. \"Sizi müşteri temsilcimize aktarıyorum.\" We might revisit once we see the numbers after go-live.\r\n\r\n12. (small one) Card names: in the analysis table some rows say \"Yıldız Gold\" and some \"Yıldız Altın\". Which one comes back from the service?\r\n>> Use whatever the card list service returns, don't translate. I'll fix the table. Btw we've seen a few cards where the name comes back empty from the service, in that case just introduce it by the last four digits (\"Sonu 4253 ile biten kartınıza ait …\").\r\n\r\nThanks,\r\nDeniz\r\n\r\n--\r\nDeniz Arı\r\nSolutions Engineer, Freya\r\nSOURCE: sources/documents/2026-10-01-service-contract.md\r\nSOURCE_SHA256: 692414a06036911509072d602b6c85bf38cbf2c791d26488d53d28be1cf35470\r\n\r\n# Voice assistant: statement and debt services, service contract\r\n\r\nAuthor: Mert Koç, Integration Architecture, Freya Bank (mert.koc@freyabank.example)\r\nDate: 1 October 2026\r\nDistribution: Selin Aydın, Kerem Yalçın, Burcu Tan (Freya Bank); Deniz Arı (Freya)\r\n\r\n> Mert: This is the contract the voice assistant integrates against for the statement / debt use case. It covers only the seven services the assistant calls. Everything about how the call reaches the assistant and how it goes back to the phone menu is Burcu's side and is not in here. If something below disagrees with what you see in the test environment, tell me and I'll check which side is wrong.\r\n\r\n## 1. Before you start\r\n\r\n- The caller is already identified when the assistant picks up. The phone menu hands over the **customer number** with the call, so there is no customer search service in this contract. Start from `cards.list`.\r\n- Every service is `POST`, JSON in, JSON out, UTF-8.\r\n- Base address for all seven:\r\n\r\n```\r\nhttps://api.freyabank.example/voice/v1/\r\n```\r\n\r\n  So the card list is `POST https://api.freyabank.example/voice/v1/cards.list`, and so on.\r\n- Dates are strings in `dd.MM.yyyy` (e.g. `\"05.11.2026\"`). Amounts are JSON numbers with a dot as decimal separator (`5000.13`), never formatted strings. Do not round them.\r\n- Turning numbers and dates into speech is the assistant's job. The services return raw values only, no sentences meant to be read to the caller (the `messageText` in errors is for logs, please don't read it out).\r\n\r\n## 2. Access token\r\n\r\nThe services sit behind our API gateway. You need a bearer token first.\r\n\r\n```\r\nPOST https://api.freyabank.example/oauth2/token\r\nContent-Type: application/x-www-form-urlencoded\r\n\r\ngrant_type=client_credentials&client_id=<client id>&client_secret=<client secret>&scope=voice.statement\r\n```\r\n\r\nAnswer:\r\n\r\n```json\r\n{\r\n  \"access_token\": \"<token>\",\r\n  \"token_type\": \"Bearer\",\r\n  \"expires_in\": 1800\r\n}\r\n```\r\n\r\nThen on every service call:\r\n\r\n| Header | Value |\r\n|---|---|\r\n| `Authorization` | `Bearer <token>` |\r\n| `Content-Type` | `application/json` |\r\n| `Accept` | `application/json` |\r\n\r\nThe token belongs to the integration (service account), not to the caller. Cache it and get a fresh one when it runs out; don't fetch one per call. Client id and secret: I'll send them over the secure channel, not by e-mail.\r\n\r\n(Test environment: the token and headers are not checked there. Send them anyway so nothing changes when we switch.)\r\n\r\n## 3. Envelope\r\n\r\n### Request\r\n\r\n```json\r\n{\r\n  \"data\": { \"...service fields...\" },\r\n  \"call\": { \"ani\": \"<caller's number>\", \"lang\": \"tr\" }\r\n}\r\n```\r\n\r\n`data` carries the fields of each service (section 5). `call` is call metadata we log on our side; nothing in this contract depends on it and the test environment ignores it.\r\n\r\n### Response\r\n\r\nEvery answer has a `result` object. Successful answers also have `data`.\r\n\r\n```json\r\n{\r\n  \"data\": { \"...service fields...\" },\r\n  \"result\": {\r\n    \"guid\": \"f0e1d2c3b4a5968778695a4b3c2d0002\",\r\n    \"returnCode\": \"1\",\r\n    \"messageText\": \"Success\",\r\n    \"code\": \"1\",\r\n    \"message\": \"Success\",\r\n    \"type\": null,\r\n    \"details\": {\r\n      \"guid\": \"f0e1d2c3b4a5968778695a4b3c2d0002\",\r\n      \"rootCause\": null,\r\n      \"errorOrigin\": null\r\n    }\r\n  }\r\n}\r\n```\r\n\r\nSuccess = HTTP 200 and `result.returnCode` is `\"1\"`. Anything else is an error, see section 4. `guid` is our trace id; if you open a ticket with us, give us the `guid`.\r\n\r\n## 4. Errors\r\n\r\nError answers have **no `data` object**, only `result`:\r\n\r\n```json\r\n{\r\n  \"result\": {\r\n    \"guid\": \"f0e1d2c3b4a5968778695a4b3c2d0086\",\r\n    \"returnCode\": \"FBE00099\",\r\n    \"messageText\": \"Teknik hata: ABORTED: \",\r\n    \"code\": \"FBE00099\",\r\n    \"message\": \"Teknik hata: ABORTED: \",\r\n    \"type\": \"ERROR\",\r\n    \"details\": {\r\n      \"guid\": \"f0e1d2c3b4a5968778695a4b3c2d0086\",\r\n      \"rootCause\": \"TechnicalError\",\r\n      \"errorOrigin\": \"ivr-gateway-test:ivr-channel-v0\"\r\n    }\r\n  }\r\n}\r\n```\r\n\r\n`returnCode` and `code` always carry the same value, and so do `messageText` and `message`. `type` is `\"ERROR\"` or `\"WARNING\"`. `rootCause` and `errorOrigin` are diagnostic strings; log them, don't branch on them.\r\n\r\nCodes you can get:\r\n\r\n| HTTP | `code` | `type` | When | Services |\r\n|---|---|---|---|---|\r\n| 400 | `INVALID_ARGUMENTS_ERROR` | `ERROR` | A required field is missing or empty (message names the field, e.g. `\"card number cannot be empty\"`) | all |\r\n| 401 | (gateway) | | Token missing or expired. Get a new token and retry once | all |\r\n| 404 | `FB_IVR_CHANNEL_102` | `WARNING` | Customer has no valid card (message: `\"Müşteriye ait geçerli kart bilgisi bulunamadı.\"`) | `cards.list` |\r\n| 404 | `FB_IVR_CHANNEL_120` | `WARNING` | Card not found | `cards.balance` |\r\n| 404 | `FB_IVR_CHANNEL_121` | `WARNING` | Card not found | `statement.week.get` |\r\n| 404 | `FB_IVR_CHANNEL_107` | `WARNING` | No statement document to send (message: `\"Pdf dokümanı bulunamadı\"`) | `statement.pdf.send` |\r\n| 500 | `FBE00099` | `ERROR` | Technical error on our side (message: `\"Teknik hata: ABORTED: \"`) | all |\r\n\r\nNote on the `cards.list` 404: it's a WARNING, it means \"this customer has no card the assistant can work with\", not that the service is broken. How the assistant reacts to that vs. a 500 is for Selin's analysis, I'm only flagging that they are different things.\r\n\r\nTimeouts: we haven't agreed a client timeout yet. Most answers are well under a second, but under load (and once in the test data) an answer can take longer than ten seconds and still succeed. Open point, see section 7.\r\n\r\n## 5. Services\r\n\r\n| # | Service | Called with | Gives back |\r\n|---|---|---|---|\r\n| 5.1 | `cards.list` | customer number | the customer's cards the assistant may talk about |\r\n| 5.2 | `cards.balance` | card number | statement and due dates, debt per currency |\r\n| 5.3 | `statement.week.get` | card number | the card's current statement week and day, joint statement info |\r\n| 5.4 | `statement.week.decide` | card number + requested week | what the dates *would* be with that week (does not change anything) |\r\n| 5.5 | `statement.week.change` | card number + requested week | changes the statement week |\r\n| 5.6 | `contact.email.get` | customer number | registered e-mail / phone |\r\n| 5.7 | `statement.pdf.send` | customer, card, currency, statement date, address | sends the statement PDF by e-mail |\r\n\r\nCard number: always send the `cardNumber` **exactly as `cards.list` returned it**. Don't rebuild it from digits the caller says.\r\n\r\n### 5.1 `cards.list`\r\n\r\nRequest:\r\n\r\n```json\r\n{\r\n  \"data\": { \"customerNo\": \"90000001\", \"aiFlow\": \"CardOps\" },\r\n  \"call\": { \"ani\": \"<caller's number>\", \"lang\": \"tr\" }\r\n}\r\n```\r\n\r\n`customerNo` may be sent as a string or as a number, both are accepted. `aiFlow` tells us which use case is asking; for this one it's `\"CardOps\"` (not validated in test).\r\n\r\nResponse, one card:\r\n\r\n```json\r\n{\r\n  \"data\": {\r\n    \"cards\": [\r\n      {\r\n        \"cardNumber\": \"540000******1234\",\r\n        \"cardNumberLast4\": \"1234\",\r\n        \"productName\": \"Yıldız Pratik Classic\",\r\n        \"status\": \"A\",\r\n        \"isMainCard\": true\r\n      }\r\n    ],\r\n    \"validCardsCount\": 1,\r\n    \"exitState\": \"1_CARD\"\r\n  },\r\n  \"result\": { \"returnCode\": \"1\", \"...\": \"as in section 3\" }\r\n}\r\n```\r\n\r\nResponse, several cards (abridged):\r\n\r\n```json\r\n{\r\n  \"data\": {\r\n    \"cards\": [\r\n      { \"cardNumber\": \"540000******4253\", \"cardNumberLast4\": \"4253\", \"productName\": \"Yıldız Pratik\", \"status\": \"A\", \"isMainCard\": true },\r\n      { \"cardNumber\": \"545000******4253\", \"cardNumberLast4\": \"4253\", \"productName\": \"Yıldız Pratik\", \"status\": \"A\", \"isMainCard\": true },\r\n      { \"cardNumber\": \"540000******6184\", \"cardNumberLast4\": \"6184\", \"productName\": \"Yıldız Pratik\", \"status\": \"A\", \"isMainCard\": true },\r\n      { \"cardNumber\": \"540000******8870\", \"cardNumberLast4\": \"8870\", \"productName\": \"Yıldız Tasarım\", \"status\": \"A\", \"isMainCard\": true }\r\n    ],\r\n    \"validCardsCount\": 4,\r\n    \"exitState\": \"MULTI_CARD\"\r\n  },\r\n  \"result\": { \"returnCode\": \"1\", \"...\": \"\" }\r\n}\r\n```\r\n\r\n- `exitState`: `\"1_CARD\"` or `\"MULTI_CARD\"`.\r\n- Only active cards come back (`status` `\"A\"`). `isMainCard` is `false` for a card that isn't the customer's own main card; it's still in the list.\r\n- Two cards can have the same product name and the same last four digits (see above). Don't assume `cardNumberLast4` is unique.\r\n- `productName` is free text from the card system, whatever was typed in there.\r\n- Masking: in the **test environment** `cardNumber` comes back masked, first 6 and last 4 digits visible (`540000******1234`). What production returns in this field I still have to confirm with the card platform team; build so that it works with the masked form.\r\n\r\nErrors: 404 `FB_IVR_CHANNEL_102`, 500 `FBE00099`.\r\n\r\n### 5.2 `cards.balance`\r\n\r\nRequest:\r\n\r\n```json\r\n{\r\n  \"data\": { \"cardNumber\": \"540000******1234\" },\r\n  \"call\": { \"ani\": \"<caller's number>\", \"lang\": \"tr\" }\r\n}\r\n```\r\n\r\nResponse:\r\n\r\n```json\r\n{\r\n  \"data\": {\r\n    \"cardNumber\": \"5400********1234\",\r\n    \"cardNumberLast4\": \"1234\",\r\n    \"productName\": \"Yıldız Pratik Classic\",\r\n    \"statementDate\": \"05.10.2026\",\r\n    \"lastPayDate\": \"15.10.2026\",\r\n    \"nextStatementDate\": \"05.11.2026\",\r\n    \"nextLastPayDate\": \"15.11.2026\",\r\n    \"debtTL\": {\r\n      \"statementPaymentStatus\": \"HAS_DEBT\",\r\n      \"transferAmount\": 0.0,\r\n      \"statementDebt\": 5000.13,\r\n      \"minPayment\": 2000.05,\r\n      \"remainingDebt\": 5000.13,\r\n      \"remainingMinPayment\": 2000.05,\r\n      \"currentPeriodExpense\": 0.0,\r\n      \"totalDebt\": 5000.13,\r\n      \"totalDebtPaymentStatus\": \"HAS_DEBT\",\r\n      \"remainAutomaticPaymentAmount\": 0.0\r\n    },\r\n    \"debtUSD\": {\r\n      \"statementPaymentStatus\": \"NO_DEBT\",\r\n      \"transferAmount\": 0.0,\r\n      \"statementDebt\": 0.0,\r\n      \"minPayment\": 0.0,\r\n      \"remainingDebt\": 0.0,\r\n      \"remainingMinPayment\": 0.0,\r\n      \"currentPeriodExpense\": 0.0,\r\n      \"totalDebt\": 0.0,\r\n      \"totalDebtPaymentStatus\": \"NO_DEBT\",\r\n      \"remainAutomaticPaymentAmount\": 0.0\r\n    },\r\n    \"debtEUR\": { \"...\": \"same fields as debtUSD\" },\r\n    \"debtGBP\": { \"...\": \"same fields as debtUSD\" },\r\n    \"statementPaymentStatus\": \"HAS_DEBT\",\r\n    \"totalDebtPaymentStatus\": \"HAS_DEBT\",\r\n    \"automaticPaymentFlag\": \"N\"\r\n  },\r\n  \"result\": { \"returnCode\": \"1\", \"...\": \"\" }\r\n}\r\n```\r\n\r\nField notes (my understanding from the card team, Selin please correct me if the business meaning is different):\r\n\r\n- `statementDate` / `lastPayDate`: the last issued statement and its due date. `nextStatementDate` / `nextLastPayDate`: the coming ones.\r\n- One block per currency: `debtTL`, `debtUSD`, `debtEUR`, `debtGBP`. **A block can be missing** (some cards have no GBP block at all). Missing block is not the same as a block with `NO_DEBT`; don't invent zeros for it.\r\n- In each block: `statementDebt` is the statement debt, `minPayment` the minimum payment on the statement; `remainingDebt` / `remainingMinPayment` what is left of those after payments made since; `totalDebt` includes spending after the statement; `currentPeriodExpense` that spending; `transferAmount` carried over from the previous period.\r\n- `statementPaymentStatus` / `totalDebtPaymentStatus`: `\"HAS_DEBT\"` or `\"NO_DEBT\"`, per block and once more at the top level for the card overall.\r\n- `totalDebt` can be negative (credit balance on that currency). Status is `NO_DEBT` then.\r\n- `automaticPaymentFlag`: `\"Y\"` / `\"N\"`. `remainAutomaticPaymentAmount` is only meaningful when it's `\"Y\"`.\r\n- Heads-up: the `cardNumber` in this answer is masked differently from the card list (first 4 + last 4, `5400********1234`). Don't use it to match against the list; use `cardNumberLast4` or just keep the number you sent.\r\n\r\nErrors: 400 `INVALID_ARGUMENTS_ERROR` (empty card number), 404 `FB_IVR_CHANNEL_120`, 500 `FBE00099`. Example 404:\r\n\r\n```json\r\n{\r\n  \"result\": {\r\n    \"guid\": \"<trace id>\",\r\n    \"returnCode\": \"FB_IVR_CHANNEL_120\",\r\n    \"messageText\": \"Kart bakiye servisi hatası: kart bulunamadı\",\r\n    \"code\": \"FB_IVR_CHANNEL_120\",\r\n    \"message\": \"Kart bakiye servisi hatası: kart bulunamadı\",\r\n    \"type\": \"WARNING\",\r\n    \"details\": { \"guid\": \"<trace id>\", \"rootCause\": \"NotFoundException\", \"errorOrigin\": \"<gateway>\" }\r\n  }\r\n}\r\n```\r\n\r\n(The message text here is just my example; only the code matters.)\r\n\r\n### 5.3 `statement.week.get`\r\n\r\nRequest:\r\n\r\n```json\r\n{\r\n  \"data\": { \"cardNumber\": \"540000******3201\" },\r\n  \"call\": { \"ani\": \"<caller's number>\", \"lang\": \"tr\" }\r\n}\r\n```\r\n\r\nResponse:\r\n\r\n```json\r\n{\r\n  \"data\": {\r\n    \"cardNumber\": \"540000******3201\",\r\n    \"cardNumberLast4\": \"3201\",\r\n    \"productName\": \"Yıldız Pratik\",\r\n    \"statementWeek\": 3,\r\n    \"statementDay\": 16,\r\n    \"statementDate\": \"16.10.2026\",\r\n    \"lastPayDate\": \"26.10.2026\",\r\n    \"nextStatementDate\": \"16.11.2026\",\r\n    \"nextLastPayDate\": \"26.11.2026\",\r\n    \"relationType\": \"M\",\r\n    \"relatedCardNumber\": \"540000******3202\",\r\n    \"relatedCardProductName\": \"Yıldız Premium\"\r\n  },\r\n  \"result\": { \"returnCode\": \"1\", \"...\": \"\" }\r\n}\r\n```\r\n\r\n(Taken from test customer 92220795, who has two cards on one statement.)\r\n\r\n- `statementWeek`: 1 to 4, the week of the month the statement is cut. `statementDay`: day of the month. Both numbers here.\r\n- `relationType`: empty string when the card has its own statement. `\"M\"` or `\"C\"` when two cards share one statement: `\"M\"` = this is the main card of the pair, `\"C\"` = this is the card linked to it. `relatedCardNumber` / `relatedCardProductName` then name the other card; otherwise they are empty strings. Changing the week on either card moves the shared statement, i.e. both cards.\r\n- There is **no field telling you whether the customer can still change the week** (yearly limit etc.). Kerem said the rule lives in the card system and the change call either goes through or it doesn't; I'll ask whether we can expose a flag, no promises.\r\n\r\nErrors: 404 `FB_IVR_CHANNEL_121`, 500 `FBE00099`.\r\n\r\n### 5.4 `statement.week.decide`\r\n\r\nPreview only. Tells you which statement date, due date and day the card would get with the requested week. **It does not change the card.**\r\n\r\nRequest:\r\n\r\n```json\r\n{\r\n  \"data\": {\r\n    \"cardNumber\": \"540000******1234\",\r\n    \"newStatementWeek\": 2,\r\n    \"statementDay\": 5\r\n  },\r\n  \"call\": { \"ani\": \"<caller's number>\", \"lang\": \"tr\" }\r\n}\r\n```\r\n\r\n- `newStatementWeek`: 1-4, number or string both fine.\r\n- `statementDay`: the card's current `statementDay` from `statement.week.get`.\r\n- Asking for the week the card already has: there is nothing to preview, you'll get an error, so don't call it for that.\r\n\r\nResponse:\r\n\r\n```json\r\n{\r\n  \"data\": {\r\n    \"statementDate\": \"09.11.2026\",\r\n    \"statementDueDate\": \"19.11.2026\",\r\n    \"statementDay\": 9,\r\n    \"responseCode\": 0,\r\n    \"responseMessage\": \"Success\"\r\n  },\r\n  \"result\": { \"returnCode\": \"1\", \"...\": \"\" }\r\n}\r\n```\r\n\r\n`statementDate` / `statementDueDate` are the first statement and due date under the requested week. Note the due date is `statementDueDate` here, not `lastPayDate` like elsewhere (not my naming...). `responseCode` `0` = fine.\r\n\r\nErrors: 400 `INVALID_ARGUMENTS_ERROR`, 500 `FBE00099`.\r\n\r\n### 5.5 `statement.week.change`\r\n\r\nThis one changes the card. Call it only after the caller has agreed to the dates from 5.4.\r\n\r\nRequest:\r\n\r\n```json\r\n{\r\n  \"data\": {\r\n    \"cardNumber\": \"540000******1234\",\r\n    \"newStatementWeek\": 2,\r\n    \"statementDay\": 5\r\n  },\r\n  \"call\": { \"ani\": \"<caller's number>\", \"lang\": \"tr\" }\r\n}\r\n```\r\n\r\n`statementDay` is required by the request model even though the backend recalculates the day. Send the current day.\r\n\r\nResponse:\r\n\r\n```json\r\n{\r\n  \"data\": {\r\n    \"responseCode\": 0,\r\n    \"responseMessage\": \"00\",\r\n    \"statementWeek\": \"2\",\r\n    \"statementDay\": \"9\",\r\n    \"exitState\": \"OK\"\r\n  },\r\n  \"result\": { \"returnCode\": \"1\", \"...\": \"\" }\r\n}\r\n```\r\n\r\nCareful: `statementWeek` and `statementDay` come back as **strings** here (numbers everywhere else). Ignore `statementDay` in this answer; the dates to tell the caller are the ones from 5.4. Success = `exitState` `\"OK\"` and `responseCode` `0`.\r\n\r\nErrors: 400 `INVALID_ARGUMENTS_ERROR`, 401 (token), 500 `FBE00099`.\r\n\r\n### 5.6 `contact.email.get`\r\n\r\nRequest:\r\n\r\n```json\r\n{\r\n  \"data\": { \"customerNo\": 90000001, \"intAddressInfoType\": \"E\" },\r\n  \"call\": { \"ani\": \"<caller's number>\", \"lang\": \"tr\" }\r\n}\r\n```\r\n\r\n`intAddressInfoType`: `\"E\"` e-mail, `\"P\"` phone, `\"B\"` both. Empty value gives a 400 (`\"intAddressInfoType cannot be empty\"`). The test environment returns the same answer whatever you ask for.\r\n\r\nResponse, e-mail registered:\r\n\r\n```json\r\n{\r\n  \"data\": {\r\n    \"communicationType\": \"Both\",\r\n    \"ibanCountryCode\": \"90\",\r\n    \"ibanAreaCode\": \"500\",\r\n    \"ibanPhoneNum\": \"0000000\",\r\n    \"ibanEmail\": \"ornek@freyabank.example\",\r\n    \"ttsEmail\": \"ornek@freyabank.example\",\r\n    \"commGramType\": \"A\",\r\n    \"exitState\": \"Done\"\r\n  },\r\n  \"result\": { \"returnCode\": \"1\", \"...\": \"\" }\r\n}\r\n```\r\n\r\nResponse, only a phone registered:\r\n\r\n```json\r\n{\r\n  \"data\": {\r\n    \"communicationType\": \"Phone\",\r\n    \"ibanCountryCode\": \"90\",\r\n    \"ibanAreaCode\": \"500\",\r\n    \"ibanPhoneNum\": \"0000000\",\r\n    \"ibanEmail\": \"\",\r\n    \"ttsEmail\": \"\",\r\n    \"commGramType\": \"A\",\r\n    \"exitState\": \"Done\"\r\n  },\r\n  \"result\": { \"returnCode\": \"1\", \"...\": \"\" }\r\n}\r\n```\r\n\r\n- The registered e-mail is `ibanEmail`. `ttsEmail` is the same address prepared for reading aloud (today identical in practice).\r\n- No registered e-mail = `communicationType` `\"Phone\"` and/or empty `ibanEmail`. Check the field, not only the type.\r\n- The `iban*` prefix is historical, it has nothing to do with IBANs.\r\n\r\nErrors: 400 `INVALID_ARGUMENTS_ERROR`, 500 `FBE00099`.\r\n\r\n### 5.7 `statement.pdf.send`\r\n\r\nSends the statement PDF to the address in the request.\r\n\r\nRequest:\r\n\r\n```json\r\n{\r\n  \"data\": {\r\n    \"customerNo\": \"90000001\",\r\n    \"cardNumber\": \"540000******1234\",\r\n    \"currencyType\": \"TL\",\r\n    \"statementDate\": \"05.10.2026\",\r\n    \"emailAddress\": \"ornek@freyabank.example\"\r\n  },\r\n  \"call\": { \"ani\": \"<caller's number>\", \"lang\": \"tr\" }\r\n}\r\n```\r\n\r\n- `statementDate`: the `statementDate` from `cards.balance` (the issued statement).\r\n- `currencyType`: `\"TL\"`, `\"USD\"`, `\"EUR\"` or `\"GBP\"`.\r\n- `emailAddress`: the backend sends to whatever is in this field. It does **not** check it against the registered address. Whether any address other than the registered one is acceptable is a business/compliance question, Elçin and Selin's call, not mine.\r\n\r\nResponse:\r\n\r\n```json\r\n{\r\n  \"data\": { \"exitState\": \"DONE\" },\r\n  \"result\": { \"returnCode\": \"1\", \"...\": \"\" }\r\n}\r\n```\r\n\r\nErrors: 400 `INVALID_ARGUMENTS_ERROR`, 404 `FB_IVR_CHANNEL_107` (no PDF for that statement), 500 `FBE00099`. Example 404:\r\n\r\n```json\r\n{\r\n  \"result\": {\r\n    \"guid\": \"f0e1d2c3b4a5968778695a4b3c2d0466\",\r\n    \"returnCode\": \"FB_IVR_CHANNEL_107\",\r\n    \"messageText\": \"Pdf dokümanı bulunamadı\",\r\n    \"code\": \"FB_IVR_CHANNEL_107\",\r\n    \"message\": \"Pdf dokümanı bulunamadı\",\r\n    \"type\": \"WARNING\",\r\n    \"details\": {\r\n      \"guid\": \"f0e1d2c3b4a5968778695a4b3c2d0466\",\r\n      \"rootCause\": \"com.freyabank.ivrgateway.ivrchannel.rest.exception.NotFoundException\",\r\n      \"errorOrigin\": \"ivr-gateway-test:ivr-channel-v0\"\r\n    }\r\n  }\r\n}\r\n```\r\n\r\n## 6. Test environment\r\n\r\n- Same paths and shapes as above. The synthetic customers and every answer they get are in `test-environment/test-customers.json` (overview in `test-environment/test-customers.md`). Nothing in there is a real person or card.\r\n- If you build your own mock, it has to return those answers exactly (status code and body). For a customer or card that isn't in the file, return the 404 for that service from section 4 (for services without a 404 there, a 400 `INVALID_ARGUMENTS_ERROR` body).\r\n- Only the `data` fields decide the answer in test. Token, headers and `call` are not checked.\r\n\r\n## 7. Open points (as of today)\r\n\r\n1. Client timeout and retry policy: not agreed. My suggestion is no automatic retry on `statement.week.change` and `statement.pdf.send` (both have side effects).\r\n2. Card number masking in production `cards.list`: confirming with the card platform team.\r\n3. A flag for \"can still change the statement week this year\": asked, no answer.\r\n4. Rate limits per client: will share when the gateway team sets them up. Shouldn't matter at pilot volume.\r\n\r\nMert\r\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
